It's buddy awesome to be here, let me tell you that.

66
00:03:56.623 --> 00:04:00.486
It's been a long time coming, you're right,

67
00:04:00.486 --> 00:04:02.986
but I think it's nice timing with the book coming out.

68
00:04:02.986 --> 00:04:06.108
Yeah, absolutely, man and I'm excited to get into the book

69
00:04:06.108 --> 00:04:06.968
and everything like that,

70
00:04:06.968 --> 00:04:11.968
but just sort of a prefatory way of contextualizing this,

71
00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:14.288
the thing that I...

72
00:04:14.288 --> 00:04:15.220
Besides our friendship,

73
00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:19.665
the thing that I really appreciate about you the most

74
00:04:19.665 --> 00:04:22.998
is your very kind of grounded, rational,

75
00:04:24.471 --> 00:04:27.577
non-absolutist approach to nutrition.

76
00:04:27.577 --> 00:04:31.649
Everything that you talk about and advocate is objective,

77
00:04:31.649 --> 00:04:36.417
it's evidence-based, you're not driven by ideology,

78
00:04:36.417 --> 00:04:38.597
you're calm in your delivery,

79
00:04:38.597 --> 00:04:41.401
you have experience in education and nutrition,

80
00:04:41.401 --> 00:04:43.227
you're not hyperbolic

81
00:04:43.227 --> 00:04:46.812
and your analysis of nutrition science

82
00:04:46.812 --> 00:04:49.857
is always deeply-rooted in the studies

83
00:04:49.857 --> 00:04:54.301
and your advocacy is oriented around actionable,

84
00:04:54.301 --> 00:04:57.218
doable changes that people can make

85
00:04:58.989 --> 00:05:01.400
and you deliver all of this in a non-judgmental

86
00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.645
and very even keeled approach,

87
00:05:04.645 --> 00:05:09.587
which I think is something that we not only need right now,

88
00:05:09.587 --> 00:05:12.380
but I think we're thirsting for

89
00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.973
in a kind of social media landscape at least

90
00:05:15.973 --> 00:05:20.556
that tilts towards extremism and hyperbolic narratives.

91
00:05:24.947 --> 00:05:27.597
Well, that means a lot to me

92
00:05:27.597 --> 00:05:30.496
because I think I pride myself

93
00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:33.587
on trying to communicate the science

94
00:05:33.587 --> 00:05:35.861
as objectively as possible.

95
00:05:35.861 --> 00:05:39.008
And I'm a big believer in science

96
00:05:39.008 --> 00:05:43.395
and how science can help us better understand the world

97
00:05:43.395 --> 00:05:46.890
and better navigate it and improve our lives,

98
00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:48.011
but you're right.

99
00:05:48.011 --> 00:05:52.594
I think a lot of the nuanced conversation has been lost

100
00:05:53.887 --> 00:05:57.554
and some of that is a result of social media

101
00:05:59.141 --> 00:06:00.713
and the algorithms that are at play

102
00:06:00.713 --> 00:06:04.130
and the absolute message is often favored

103
00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:09.321
from an engagement point of view.

104
00:06:09.321 --> 00:06:11.988
It will reinforce one's identity

105
00:06:13.164 --> 00:06:15.796
and at the same time perhaps challenge

106
00:06:15.796 --> 00:06:19.028
and trigger the other side's identity,

107
00:06:19.028 --> 00:06:21.420
which is the ultimate result

108
00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:25.498
if you're looking to create engagement and excitement.

109
00:06:25.498 --> 00:06:28.331
So, ever since I was a little kid,

110
00:06:29.572 --> 00:06:33.217
I have had a deep appreciation for science

111
00:06:33.217 --> 00:06:36.050
and being able to use it as a tool

112
00:06:37.764 --> 00:06:41.097
to help reduce the amount of uncertainty

113
00:06:42.785 --> 00:06:45.346
and that's what science is.

114
00:06:45.346 --> 00:06:50.096
It's using a method to test our intuition, our hypotheses

115
00:06:54.451 --> 00:06:56.133
and to reduce that uncertainty

116
00:06:56.133 --> 00:06:59.966
so we can make decisions with more confidence.

117
00:07:01.751 --> 00:07:05.445
And so my approach with nutrition science

118
00:07:05.445 --> 00:07:09.442
has always been to let the science do the talking

119
00:07:09.442 --> 00:07:14.025
and be led by the data or be open to changing your mind

120
00:07:14.891 --> 00:07:18.974
and a lot of that I've really learnt from my dad.

121
00:07:21.026 --> 00:07:24.292
Your dad was a scientist, a medical doctor, yeah

122
00:07:24.292 --> 00:07:27.381
and you have this kind of evolution story

123
00:07:27.381 --> 00:07:30.358
of how you got into nutrition science

124
00:07:30.358 --> 00:07:32.524
that really begins with a health crisis

125
00:07:32.524 --> 00:07:34.774
that was faced by your dad.

126
00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:40.372
Yeah, when I was maybe three or four years old,

127
00:07:41.907 --> 00:07:46.578
that's sort of as far back as my memory goes, maybe five.

128
00:07:46.578 --> 00:07:49.021
As early as I can recall,

129
00:07:49.021 --> 00:07:52.270
I would be surrounded by scientific papers,

130
00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:55.449
my dad would print these things out

131
00:07:55.449 --> 00:07:57.416
and they would fill up the coffee table

132
00:07:57.416 --> 00:07:59.806
and if he picked me up from school,

133
00:07:59.806 --> 00:08:01.503
I'd have to lift them up off the car seat

134
00:08:01.503 --> 00:08:04.387
just to get into the car and they'd always be highlighted

135
00:08:04.387 --> 00:08:06.788
and I couldn't make much sense of them,

136
00:08:06.788 --> 00:08:10.302
but I could see how important science was to my dad

137
00:08:10.302 --> 00:08:13.158
and the work that he was doing.

138
00:08:13.158 --> 00:08:17.825
He got his PhD in Texas so we moved as a family to Texas

139
00:08:18.719 --> 00:08:21.754
where he did his PhD in physiology

140
00:08:21.754 --> 00:08:23.921
and has gone on since then

141
00:08:25.719 --> 00:08:30.552
to have 30 or 40 years of academic research as a professor

142
00:08:32.465 --> 00:08:34.882
and publishing in circulation

143
00:08:36.365 --> 00:08:38.160
and metabolism-leading journals

144
00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:41.077
and a very well-respected professor

145
00:08:43.323 --> 00:08:44.646
in his field of physiology,

146
00:08:44.646 --> 00:08:48.477
which is looking right under a microscope

147
00:08:48.477 --> 00:08:51.463
at the micro level mechanisms, deep science,

148
00:08:51.463 --> 00:08:53.447
much deeper than what I do.

149
00:08:53.447 --> 00:08:57.276
I tend to zoom out and think about what's the big picture,

150
00:08:57.276 --> 00:08:58.589
what does this mean to you and I?

151
00:08:58.589 --> 00:09:01.026
How can we make better decisions and be healthier

152
00:09:01.026 --> 00:09:04.515
and how can we consider the world around us?

153
00:09:04.515 --> 00:09:07.682
But his research equally as important,

154
00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:11.988
understanding the mechanisms that are at play

155
00:09:11.988 --> 00:09:16.821
has been dedicated to looking at how our arteries function

156
00:09:18.521 --> 00:09:19.777
and dysfunction

157
00:09:19.777 --> 00:09:24.159
and what happens when you develop type 2 diabetes

158
00:09:24.159 --> 00:09:25.993
and cardiovascular disease.

159
00:09:25.993 --> 00:09:30.076
And this is somewhat ironic because you're right,

160
00:09:31.243 --> 00:09:33.394
I did experience for the first time

161
00:09:33.394 --> 00:09:37.311
what loss of health looks like and on weekends,

162
00:09:39.941 --> 00:09:41.015
my dad and my brother...

163
00:09:41.015 --> 00:09:45.481
You've met my brother a couple years ago here in LA, James

164
00:09:45.481 --> 00:09:48.472
and he's actually the one that introduced me

165
00:09:48.472 --> 00:09:50.472
to your show originally.

166
00:09:51.798 --> 00:09:56.348
On weekends when we had moved back to Australia...

167
00:09:56.348 --> 00:09:58.256
So, when I was about 10 years old

168
00:09:58.256 --> 00:10:01.045
and my dad had finished his PhD

169
00:10:01.045 --> 00:10:04.049
and was working as an assistant professor in America,

170
00:10:04.049 --> 00:10:05.873
we moved back to Australia.

171
00:10:05.873 --> 00:10:08.638
And on weekends we would go

172
00:10:08.638 --> 00:10:12.736
and explore an area called the Yarra Valley.

173
00:10:12.736 --> 00:10:17.430
And it's a beautiful wine district in Victoria,

174
00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:21.513
rolling hills, incredible scenery and we would go

175
00:10:23.675 --> 00:10:26.155
and visit little wineries,

176
00:10:26.155 --> 00:10:29.616
my dad always liked to choose the small ones.

177
00:10:29.616 --> 00:10:33.533
And if you went to these more boutique wineries

178
00:10:34.401 --> 00:10:37.206
and this wasn't about the alcohol,

179
00:10:37.206 --> 00:10:39.170
it was about the experience.

180
00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:43.428
And you could speak to the winemaker usually

181
00:10:43.428 --> 00:10:46.955
and you could see the passion they had

182
00:10:46.955 --> 00:10:48.755
for what they were doing.

183
00:10:48.755 --> 00:10:51.459
And so we would have a lot of fun together

184
00:10:51.459 --> 00:10:55.443
exploring these areas and on this one particular day,

185
00:10:55.443 --> 00:10:57.526
it was just my dad and I,

186
00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:02.601
we had a fantastic day out together

187
00:11:02.601 --> 00:11:05.351
and my dad had a MGB Convertible,

188
00:11:07.666 --> 00:11:09.729
I can remember these days vividly.

189
00:11:09.729 --> 00:11:14.229
My dad had an MG Midget so I know the vibe.

190
00:11:14.229 --> 00:11:18.616
Great, great cars and the roof would come down

191
00:11:18.616 --> 00:11:22.135
and we would play classics like "You Too"

192
00:11:22.135 --> 00:11:24.906
and "Rolling Stones" and Cat Stevens

193
00:11:24.906 --> 00:11:27.105
and all that sort of stuff

194
00:11:27.105 --> 00:11:30.668
and I think a lot of people listening

195
00:11:30.668 --> 00:11:32.264
perhaps can relate to that

196
00:11:32.264 --> 00:11:36.066
perhaps with experiences with their parents or uncle

197
00:11:36.066 --> 00:11:39.531
or someone in their life those days you look back on,

198
00:11:39.531 --> 00:11:43.948
20, 30 years ago and with very sort of fond memories.

199
00:11:45.643 --> 00:11:47.471
These were those days for me.

200
00:11:47.471 --> 00:11:50.983
And on this one day in particular

201
00:11:50.983 --> 00:11:53.781
as we're driving home, it's dusk,

202
00:11:53.781 --> 00:11:56.674
I could see that my dad was uncomfortable

203
00:11:56.674 --> 00:11:59.410
and he was slightly grimacing

204
00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:02.318
and he was also rubbing his chest.

205
00:12:02.318 --> 00:12:04.603
And I asked him if everything was okay

206
00:12:04.603 --> 00:12:07.913
and he said he was feeling some chest pain,

207
00:12:07.913 --> 00:12:09.728
but downplayed it

208
00:12:09.728 --> 00:12:13.890
and we continued to go home and cooked dinner

209
00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:16.379
and I remember checking in again

210
00:12:16.379 --> 00:12:19.868
and he said that everything was fine.

211
00:12:19.868 --> 00:12:22.189
So, we had dinner and thinking nothing of it,

212
00:12:22.189 --> 00:12:24.332
I went off to bed.

213
00:12:24.332 --> 00:12:26.749
And a short while afterwards,

214
00:12:28.141 --> 00:12:30.513
I woke to some noise in the kitchen

215
00:12:30.513 --> 00:12:32.371
and I thought,

216
00:12:32.371 --> 00:12:35.897
"Well, thinking about what had happened,

217
00:12:35.897 --> 00:12:38.837
I better go and check if he's okay."

218
00:12:38.837 --> 00:12:40.837
And when I went out,

219
00:12:40.837 --> 00:12:43.520
I could see he was making his way to the phone,

220
00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.932
he had the phone in his hand by that stage

221
00:12:45.932 --> 00:12:50.932
and he was on his knees, at this stage, this was, Rich,

222
00:12:51.249 --> 00:12:56.166
the first time that I've ever seen my dad with so much fear

223
00:12:59.607 --> 00:13:00.828
in his eyes.

224
00:13:00.828 --> 00:13:04.942
It was a point now that he could no longer deny

225
00:13:04.942 --> 00:13:06.356
what was happening.

226
00:13:06.356 --> 00:13:09.439
And he was pale, he was out of breath

227
00:13:11.587 --> 00:13:14.806
and it was very obvious that he was struggling.

228
00:13:14.806 --> 00:13:17.889
And so I actually spoke with the lady

229
00:13:21.616 --> 00:13:24.307
on the other end of the line, hit code triple zero,

230
00:13:24.307 --> 00:13:26.033
which is like calling 911 here

231
00:13:26.033 --> 00:13:29.283
and I was explaining what was happening

232
00:13:30.584 --> 00:13:34.167
and all of this was happening super quickly

233
00:13:35.349 --> 00:13:39.171
and I was 15 at the time, my dad was 41,

234
00:13:39.171 --> 00:13:41.171
this was out of nowhere.

235
00:13:44.027 --> 00:13:47.772
He was not reliant on the healthcare system,

236
00:13:47.772 --> 00:13:50.319
he had no medical diagnosis,

237
00:13:50.319 --> 00:13:52.566
he was not taking any medications,

238
00:13:52.566 --> 00:13:56.818
he was living the standard Australian lifestyle.

239
00:13:56.818 --> 00:13:59.453
And if you looked at my dad at that stage,

240
00:13:59.453 --> 00:14:00.708
you would not look at him

241
00:14:00.708 --> 00:14:03.791
and say he is particularly unhealthy.

242
00:14:05.665 --> 00:14:08.713
He was in decent shape and would exercise

243
00:14:08.713 --> 00:14:11.040
and for all intents and purposes

244
00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.180
was just representative of young, Australian dad.

245
00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:18.262
And studying vascular physiology at the time.

246
00:14:18.262 --> 00:14:20.595
Right, so many years later

247
00:14:23.426 --> 00:14:26.889
and now a better understanding what happened

248
00:14:26.889 --> 00:14:30.478
and the disease at play, which I'll go into,

249
00:14:30.478 --> 00:14:31.493
it's become clear to me

250
00:14:31.493 --> 00:14:35.215
that my dad knew exactly what was happening at the time.

251
00:14:35.215 --> 00:14:38.465
And I think this denial is quite common

252
00:14:40.256 --> 00:14:44.170
particularly in males that are experiencing poor health

253
00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:48.837
perhaps if you deny what you're feeling it will go away.

254
00:14:51.645 --> 00:14:53.305
I'm not sure it works like that.

255
00:14:53.305 --> 00:14:56.305
I have some familiarity with that.

256
00:14:57.512 --> 00:15:01.095
So, the lady on the other end of the line

257
00:15:02.101 --> 00:15:05.601
after me explaining what was happening said,

258
00:15:05.601 --> 00:15:08.154
"We need to send a helicopter."

259
00:15:08.154 --> 00:15:11.737
And so where we were at that point in time,

260
00:15:13.495 --> 00:15:16.963
my dad had a place in Kinglake so my parents were separated,

261
00:15:16.963 --> 00:15:20.173
when we moved back they got divorced,

262
00:15:20.173 --> 00:15:21.328
when we moved back to Australia

263
00:15:21.328 --> 00:15:23.911
and my mom and my brother and I

264
00:15:25.355 --> 00:15:27.388
we were actually living in Melbourne

265
00:15:27.388 --> 00:15:31.242
and then we would spend the weekends with dad.

266
00:15:31.242 --> 00:15:34.650
So, they said, "We need to send a helicopter,"

267
00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:36.624
Kinglake is quite remote,

268
00:15:36.624 --> 00:15:38.387
there was like this mud brick house

269
00:15:38.387 --> 00:15:39.509
in the middle of nowhere.

270
00:15:39.509 --> 00:15:43.452
My dad's always loved being out in the middle of nowhere

271
00:15:43.452 --> 00:15:44.785
and then any way

272
00:15:46.329 --> 00:15:48.388
it was a long way from the nearest hospital.

273
00:15:48.388 --> 00:15:52.471
So, they sent a helicopter and it came so quickly

274
00:15:53.478 --> 00:15:57.130
and they rushed in and scooped him up,

275
00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:01.118
put him onto a stretcher and put him on oxygen

276
00:16:01.118 --> 00:16:05.701
and were checking his vital signs and before I knew it,

277
00:16:06.544 --> 00:16:09.136
he was in the helicopter and it was flying off

278
00:16:09.136 --> 00:16:12.519
and I couldn't fit in the helicopter so they said to me

279
00:16:12.519 --> 00:16:17.519
I could follow in a car, in an ambulance to the hospital.

280
00:16:17.998 --> 00:16:22.248
And by that time, I called my mother and my brother

281
00:16:24.875 --> 00:16:27.896
and said, "You probably should come to the hospital.

282
00:16:27.896 --> 00:16:29.331
This is what's happening.

283
00:16:29.331 --> 00:16:31.150
I don't know what the outcome is here,

284
00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:33.738
we really don't know the specifics,

285
00:16:33.738 --> 00:16:36.330
but dad's not in a good way."

286
00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:39.080
And that car ride to the hospital

287
00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:45.040
it felt like it took forever and we got to the hospital

288
00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:49.290
and we waited and we waited and the doctor came out

289
00:16:50.221 --> 00:16:51.471
and said to us,

290
00:16:52.886 --> 00:16:55.264
"we've saved your father's life.

291
00:16:55.264 --> 00:16:57.733
He's had a severe heart attack,"

292
00:16:57.733 --> 00:17:01.203
which of course the fact they saved his life

293
00:17:01.203 --> 00:17:05.086
was what we were most concerned about then and there,

294
00:17:05.086 --> 00:17:07.865
but we were very shocked that at 41,

295
00:17:07.865 --> 00:17:10.620
this had come out of nowhere.

296
00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:14.287
And the next day, there was a family meeting

297
00:17:15.475 --> 00:17:19.636
as there often is, it's a bit of a debrief, what's happened,

298
00:17:19.636 --> 00:17:22.491
what's the prognosis, et cetera

299
00:17:22.491 --> 00:17:26.105
and so we met with the cardiologist

300
00:17:26.105 --> 00:17:28.855
and he had taken my dad's history

301
00:17:30.569 --> 00:17:34.156
and it was quite clear that cardiovascular disease

302
00:17:34.156 --> 00:17:36.607
does run in my family.

303
00:17:36.607 --> 00:17:39.117
And my grandfather had had a heart attack

304
00:17:39.117 --> 00:17:42.864
at about age 61 I believe, my grandmother...

305
00:17:42.864 --> 00:17:47.763
So, that was my dad's dad, my dad's mom, my grandmother,

306
00:17:47.763 --> 00:17:49.846
she had vascular dementia

307
00:17:52.229 --> 00:17:55.167
and so the cardiologist said to my brother...

308
00:17:55.167 --> 00:17:57.887
My brother was 18, I was 15,

309
00:17:57.887 --> 00:18:02.370
he said that, "Cardiovascular disease runs in families,

310
00:18:02.370 --> 00:18:03.938
clearly it's running in yours

311
00:18:03.938 --> 00:18:07.134
and this will be something that you need to be screened for

312
00:18:07.134 --> 00:18:08.945
as you get older."

313
00:18:08.945 --> 00:18:13.362
And looking back on that today, that is great advice.

314
00:18:17.873 --> 00:18:21.040
But the conversation ended there and--

315
00:18:24.453 --> 00:18:28.043
It lacks any sense of agency, this is a predestined thing,

316
00:18:28.043 --> 00:18:31.029
this is genetically motivated

317
00:18:31.029 --> 00:18:34.800
and you're gonna have to contend with it at some point

318
00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.845
and that's very much of that time as well.

319
00:18:37.845 --> 00:18:40.748
Like we know a lot more now about how we can intervene

320
00:18:40.748 --> 00:18:42.274
and make these lifestyle choices,

321
00:18:42.274 --> 00:18:44.567
but I was told the same thing.

322
00:18:44.567 --> 00:18:48.661
My grandfather died early, like I know that whole drill

323
00:18:48.661 --> 00:18:50.476
and what it leaves you with

324
00:18:50.476 --> 00:18:54.220
and you tell me how you felt was this sense

325
00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:56.837
that there really isn't much you can do about it.

326
00:18:56.837 --> 00:18:59.920
Yeah, you've been dealt a bad card.

327
00:19:01.147 --> 00:19:03.136
I mean, that is how I felt

328
00:19:03.136 --> 00:19:05.395
and my brother felt for many years,

329
00:19:05.395 --> 00:19:10.173
was that what's to say we're not going to follow

330
00:19:10.173 --> 00:19:12.006
in my dad's footsteps?

331
00:19:13.049 --> 00:19:15.317
He was living a typical Australian lifestyle,

332
00:19:15.317 --> 00:19:18.119
he wasn't from the outside unhealthy

333
00:19:18.119 --> 00:19:19.265
and this has happened to him,

334
00:19:19.265 --> 00:19:22.629
so why will it be any different for us?

335
00:19:22.629 --> 00:19:24.490
If you do the same thing as everyone around you,

336
00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:27.598
how can you expect different results?

337
00:19:27.598 --> 00:19:31.348
So, it's disempowering when you get that news

338
00:19:32.819 --> 00:19:36.819
and you put your health down purely to genetics.

339
00:19:39.267 --> 00:19:44.184
And it was many years later that I would come to learn that

340
00:19:46.586 --> 00:19:49.919
while genetics do create predispositions

341
00:19:51.724 --> 00:19:53.809
and can increase your likelihood

342
00:19:53.809 --> 00:19:57.200
of developing a certain disease, your lifestyle

343
00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:00.394
and the way that you're navigating your life

344
00:20:00.394 --> 00:20:02.192
has a lot more say.

345
00:20:02.192 --> 00:20:04.859
And there are a bunch of studies

346
00:20:06.217 --> 00:20:08.692
looking at identical twins for example,

347
00:20:08.692 --> 00:20:12.334
the exact same genes, different environments growing up,

348
00:20:12.334 --> 00:20:14.877
nature versus nurture, what happens?

349
00:20:14.877 --> 00:20:18.291
And these studies have been able to tease out

350
00:20:18.291 --> 00:20:20.666
what is the impact of genetics

351
00:20:20.666 --> 00:20:23.723
versus lifestyle broadly speaking

352
00:20:23.723 --> 00:20:25.675
and of course this is different

353
00:20:25.675 --> 00:20:29.246
depending on the type of genes that someone has,

354
00:20:29.246 --> 00:20:30.260
but broadly speaking,

355
00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:33.564
if we're thinking about these big chronic diseases

356
00:20:33.564 --> 00:20:36.855
like cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes,

357
00:20:36.855 --> 00:20:41.210
fatty liver disease, even Alzheimer's dementia,

358
00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:45.441
broadly speaking, your genes are controlling about 20%

359
00:20:45.441 --> 00:20:47.464
of your health fate.

360
00:20:47.464 --> 00:20:50.561
And that means that your environment

361
00:20:50.561 --> 00:20:54.079
and your lifestyle is controlling 80%,

362
00:20:54.079 --> 00:20:56.509
is four times more powerful

363
00:20:56.509 --> 00:21:01.509
and so of course when you come across that information

364
00:21:01.647 --> 00:21:04.789
then the game changes, the narrative flips

365
00:21:04.789 --> 00:21:09.311
from being disempowered to being incredibly empowered.

366
00:21:09.311 --> 00:21:10.860
So, is your brother...

367
00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:14.006
Correct me if I'm wrong, who was the first instigator

368
00:21:14.006 --> 00:21:17.782
in terms of getting you interested in nutrition?

369
00:21:17.782 --> 00:21:19.009
You've done your research.

370
00:21:19.009 --> 00:21:20.367
A little bit.

371
00:21:20.367 --> 00:21:23.617
I know you two, so it's not like, yeah.

372
00:21:26.531 --> 00:21:28.781
I didn't expect any less.

373
00:21:29.797 --> 00:21:32.464
So, when I finished high school,

374
00:21:36.042 --> 00:21:39.327
I actually wanted to become a doctor.

375
00:21:39.327 --> 00:21:43.768
And I got really, really strong grades at school,

376
00:21:43.768 --> 00:21:45.021
but not quite strong enough

377
00:21:45.021 --> 00:21:47.718
to get into medicine in Melbourne.

378
00:21:47.718 --> 00:21:51.856
And so I was accepted to do medicine, but in Tasmania

379
00:21:51.856 --> 00:21:54.106
and I wasn't quite prepared

380
00:21:55.935 --> 00:21:59.823
to make the move down to Tasmania and give up my friendships

381
00:21:59.823 --> 00:22:01.117
and give up playing football

382
00:22:01.117 --> 00:22:04.929
and so I made the decision to stick around

383
00:22:04.929 --> 00:22:08.679
and I did physiotherapy instead in Melbourne.

384
00:22:09.814 --> 00:22:12.314
And the reason I tell you this

385
00:22:15.909 --> 00:22:19.795
is because I was very much indoctrinated

386
00:22:19.795 --> 00:22:21.936
and I was surrounded by this fitness culture,

387
00:22:21.936 --> 00:22:25.063
I was working in football club environments

388
00:22:25.063 --> 00:22:29.556
and these are environments where animal protein is king.

389
00:22:29.556 --> 00:22:31.889
And so there was a Christmas

390
00:22:35.155 --> 00:22:39.778
where my brother was coming up to stay with me

391
00:22:39.778 --> 00:22:43.348
with his fiancee at the time, Lauren who's now his wife

392
00:22:43.348 --> 00:22:46.015
and he called me up and he said,

393
00:22:47.319 --> 00:22:50.865
"We've been reading and listening to a few things,"

394
00:22:50.865 --> 00:22:52.706
probably your podcast

395
00:22:52.706 --> 00:22:56.388
and probably some of Dan Buettner's work I believe

396
00:22:56.388 --> 00:22:59.638
and he had come across some information

397
00:23:01.587 --> 00:23:05.548
to suggest that there were populations

398
00:23:05.548 --> 00:23:09.125
that were not experiencing the same levels,

399
00:23:09.125 --> 00:23:11.554
the same degree of cardiovascular disease

400
00:23:11.554 --> 00:23:14.398
that our societies experience.

401
00:23:14.398 --> 00:23:18.268
And of course thinking back to our dad's history

402
00:23:18.268 --> 00:23:21.514
and grandfather's history, that was meaningful information

403
00:23:21.514 --> 00:23:23.383
for my brother to come across

404
00:23:23.383 --> 00:23:25.959
and enough for him to want to explore

405
00:23:25.959 --> 00:23:28.876
and he's from a business background

406
00:23:29.761 --> 00:23:33.572
so it was not something that was inspiring him to get deep

407
00:23:33.572 --> 00:23:36.431
into the science or the weights with,

408
00:23:36.431 --> 00:23:40.438
he felt that the information was of high enough quality

409
00:23:40.438 --> 00:23:41.716
to act on.

410
00:23:41.716 --> 00:23:43.716
And he called me to say,

411
00:23:45.909 --> 00:23:48.379
"Lauren and I have changed the way we're eating a bit"

412
00:23:48.379 --> 00:23:50.896
and it was a bit of a heads-up.

413
00:23:50.896 --> 00:23:52.469
They were coming to stay with me

414
00:23:52.469 --> 00:23:55.459
and I was going to do some grocery shopping of course

415
00:23:55.459 --> 00:23:58.876
and take them out for dinner and he said,

416
00:24:01.187 --> 00:24:04.845
"We're no longer eating red meat and chicken,

417
00:24:04.845 --> 00:24:07.121
we've adopted a pescatarian diet."

418
00:24:07.121 --> 00:24:09.645
I think this might have been like my introduction

419
00:24:09.645 --> 00:24:11.867
to dietary labels.

420
00:24:11.867 --> 00:24:14.126
Before then, I hadn't given a whole lot of thought

421
00:24:14.126 --> 00:24:16.502
to all the different dietary labels that exist

422
00:24:16.502 --> 00:24:18.111
or the dietary wars,

423
00:24:18.111 --> 00:24:20.528
I was just doing what I knew.

424
00:24:22.199 --> 00:24:26.061
And I said to him, "Well, that's cool.

425
00:24:26.061 --> 00:24:29.256
I eat fish and we can make that work

426
00:24:29.256 --> 00:24:31.730
and I'm sure we can find restaurants

427
00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:34.060
that will cater for that."

428
00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:38.046
And that was a few months before him coming up

429
00:24:38.046 --> 00:24:41.470
and then about a week before he came up,

430
00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:44.387
he called me up again and he said,

431
00:24:45.313 --> 00:24:47.171
"We've changed our diet a little more."

432
00:24:47.171 --> 00:24:49.163
(both laugh)

433
00:24:49.163 --> 00:24:50.817
The volume is getting turned up.

434
00:24:50.817 --> 00:24:55.817
Yeah and I thought, "Okay," I thought maybe he'd reverted

435
00:24:56.320 --> 00:25:00.426
and he said, "Well, we've decided to remove the fish."

436
00:25:00.426 --> 00:25:04.093
And I was thinking, "What the fuck is left?"

437
00:25:05.653 --> 00:25:06.720
How long ago was this?

438
00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:08.387
This is 2014, '15.

439
00:25:10.276 --> 00:25:11.171
Not that long ago.

440
00:25:11.171 --> 00:25:15.088
Yeah, 2014, '15 and so I said to him, I said,

441
00:25:18.263 --> 00:25:21.876
"Look, well, I'm definitely going to need some help

442
00:25:21.876 --> 00:25:24.000
with regards to knowing what to buy

443
00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:26.127
and certainly with restaurants."

444
00:25:26.127 --> 00:25:28.544
I had no business in choosing the restaurants

445
00:25:28.544 --> 00:25:32.961
that would be catering for vegan meals, to be honest,

446
00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:37.982
I had never really considered a diet

447
00:25:37.982 --> 00:25:40.134
without animal products.

448
00:25:40.134 --> 00:25:44.551
And he came up and both he and Lauren are great cooks

449
00:25:48.867 --> 00:25:53.867
and particularly Lauren and so they did a lot of cooking

450
00:25:54.198 --> 00:25:56.084
and we went out to some restaurants

451
00:25:56.084 --> 00:25:59.862
and the feeling that I was left with

452
00:25:59.862 --> 00:26:01.814
at the end of those two weeks

453
00:26:01.814 --> 00:26:04.314
was no any pressure from them,

454
00:26:05.567 --> 00:26:09.146
they weren't asking me to consider how I was eating,

455
00:26:09.146 --> 00:26:10.950
we just had a great two weeks together

456
00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:13.184
and their food was delicious

457
00:26:13.184 --> 00:26:17.135
and so the feeling that I was left with was,

458
00:26:17.135 --> 00:26:19.355
"Well, if my brother's right

459
00:26:19.355 --> 00:26:21.775
and there is some information out there

460
00:26:21.775 --> 00:26:25.634
that suggests you can change the way you're eating

461
00:26:25.634 --> 00:26:29.247
and reduce your risk of cardiovascular disease

462
00:26:29.247 --> 00:26:33.723
and I know that I don't have to sacrifice on flavor and joy

463
00:26:33.723 --> 00:26:35.778
and the experience,

464
00:26:35.778 --> 00:26:40.554
then this is something that I should be looking into."

465
00:26:40.554 --> 00:26:42.486
But for those that aren't watching on YouTube

466
00:26:42.486 --> 00:26:44.261
and are just listening,

467
00:26:44.261 --> 00:26:46.270
what you might not know is that

468
00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:48.948
Simon is a physical specimen,

469
00:26:48.948 --> 00:26:51.598
like you're a buffed, big strong guy

470
00:26:51.598 --> 00:26:54.987
and you were working with Australian rules football guys

471
00:26:54.987 --> 00:26:59.904
at the time, like your focus is on performance and strength

472
00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:03.698
and agility and all of these things.

473
00:27:03.698 --> 00:27:07.382
So, how does that information mesh with everything

474
00:27:07.382 --> 00:27:10.182
that you knew up to that point and were interested in

475
00:27:10.182 --> 00:27:13.604
because, "Yes, oh, I feel good and this tastes good,

476
00:27:13.604 --> 00:27:15.687
but how is this going to fuel me

477
00:27:15.687 --> 00:27:18.562
and keep me in the physical condition

478
00:27:18.562 --> 00:27:20.426
to which I'm accustomed?"

479
00:27:20.426 --> 00:27:23.176
So, I had that fear completely.

480
00:27:24.806 --> 00:27:27.223
I think the first step for me

481
00:27:28.289 --> 00:27:32.463
was first looking at the information and science

482
00:27:32.463 --> 00:27:35.162
around chronic disease.

483
00:27:35.162 --> 00:27:39.120
And then when I had unearthed information there

484
00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:40.826
and it became clear to me

485
00:27:40.826 --> 00:27:42.276
that there was a way to eat

486
00:27:42.276 --> 00:27:46.100
to reduce your risk of chronic disease, the next step was,

487
00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:48.338
"Am I going to have to sacrifice

488
00:27:48.338 --> 00:27:51.312
the physical performance, athletic pursuits?

489
00:27:51.312 --> 00:27:54.083
Am I going to be able to maintain,

490
00:27:54.083 --> 00:27:56.500
improve strength, et cetera?"

491
00:27:58.104 --> 00:28:02.437
And so the first step, Rich for me after that was...

492
00:28:05.365 --> 00:28:08.024
I was trained in physiotherapy initially, remember,

493
00:28:08.024 --> 00:28:10.983
at this time I actually hadn't done my master's

494
00:28:10.983 --> 00:28:13.491
in nutrition science.

495
00:28:13.491 --> 00:28:15.418
That's been done in the last seven years.

496
00:28:15.418 --> 00:28:19.497
So, when my brother came and stayed with me

497
00:28:19.497 --> 00:28:22.028
and inspired me to think about this,

498
00:28:22.028 --> 00:28:24.450
I had no formal training in nutrition.

499
00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:28.082
I had done an undergraduate degree in physiotherapy,

500
00:28:28.082 --> 00:28:31.184
which is a four-year sort of bachelor in science,

501
00:28:31.184 --> 00:28:33.724
so you cover a lot of physiology and anatomy

502
00:28:33.724 --> 00:28:38.614
and I did an honors year where I wrote my own thesis,

503
00:28:38.614 --> 00:28:41.030
performed the review, conducted my own study

504
00:28:41.030 --> 00:28:42.109
and wrote it up.

505
00:28:42.109 --> 00:28:47.034
So, I understood how to look at research and analyze it

506
00:28:47.034 --> 00:28:51.860
albeit from a different lens, a different area of science.

507
00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:55.257
So, the first step for me was to try

508
00:28:55.257 --> 00:28:57.521
and get into the literature

509
00:28:57.521 --> 00:29:01.810
and see if what my brother was telling me was true or not.

510
00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:05.977
And I quite often say I wanted to prove him wrong.

511
00:29:08.944 --> 00:29:13.027
I was healthy, I wasn't having any health issues,

512
00:29:13.991 --> 00:29:16.054
I was strong, I was fit,

513
00:29:16.054 --> 00:29:19.750
everything about my diet seemed to be working for me,

514
00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:21.000
not against me.

515
00:29:22.273 --> 00:29:26.356
And as I was getting into the research initially,

516
00:29:27.375 --> 00:29:32.375
what I realized was how many polarizing views there are

517
00:29:32.604 --> 00:29:35.521
and how confusing it is and tribal.

518
00:29:36.443 --> 00:29:39.047
And one moment I thought I understood something

519
00:29:39.047 --> 00:29:41.824
then I'd say the exact opposite.

520
00:29:41.824 --> 00:29:44.089
And that's what inspired me to go

521
00:29:44.089 --> 00:29:46.395
and do the master's in nutrition science

522
00:29:46.395 --> 00:29:50.425
because I realized even though I had training

523
00:29:50.425 --> 00:29:52.916
at an undergraduate level in science,

524
00:29:52.916 --> 00:29:56.919
I was not equipped with the skills to make full sense of it

525
00:29:56.919 --> 00:29:59.374
and to really decode it.

526
00:29:59.374 --> 00:30:02.041
And so it was back to university

527
00:30:04.528 --> 00:30:09.361
and to really focusing on the research base side of things

528
00:30:10.596 --> 00:30:12.581
and then get right down into the weeds

529
00:30:12.581 --> 00:30:16.414
of the nutrition science to try and understand

530
00:30:17.411 --> 00:30:21.100
exactly what an optimal diet looks like,

531
00:30:21.100 --> 00:30:24.590
So, you get your master's in nutrition science,

532
00:30:24.590 --> 00:30:27.879
there's a whole like sort of backstory or backdrop here,

533
00:30:27.879 --> 00:30:30.291
this entrepreneurial journey that you've been on

534
00:30:30.291 --> 00:30:32.563
that's almost its own podcast,

535
00:30:32.563 --> 00:30:34.953
we can maybe revisit that a little bit,

536
00:30:34.953 --> 00:30:37.886
but you emerge from that experience,

537
00:30:37.886 --> 00:30:40.477
you become steeped in the science,

538
00:30:40.477 --> 00:30:44.121
you have this kind of urgent call to advocacy

539
00:30:44.121 --> 00:30:47.454
that leads you to create plant proof.com

540
00:30:48.477 --> 00:30:50.247
and the Plant Proof Podcast

541
00:30:50.247 --> 00:30:53.499
and the latest iteration now being this new book,

542
00:30:53.499 --> 00:30:55.556
"The Proof Is In The Plants",

543
00:30:55.556 --> 00:30:58.921
but walk me through how you arrived

544
00:30:58.921 --> 00:31:03.171
at a certain kind of global thesis around nutrition

545
00:31:05.229 --> 00:31:07.562
and perhaps state that case.

546
00:31:09.083 --> 00:31:13.570
So, I guess the thesis to start here with the thesis

547
00:31:13.570 --> 00:31:15.722
and then I can go into how I came to that

548
00:31:15.722 --> 00:31:17.842
and how I look at science in general,

549
00:31:17.842 --> 00:31:22.842
the thesis is that there is a set of characteristics

550
00:31:22.984 --> 00:31:26.151
or a theme that define an optimal diet

551
00:31:27.651 --> 00:31:31.128
rather than one particular dietary brand

552
00:31:31.128 --> 00:31:35.522
being absolutely proven by science as the most optimal.

553
00:31:35.522 --> 00:31:40.439
And this theme is that diets that are low in saturated fat,

554
00:31:42.265 --> 00:31:44.975
that provide good amounts of unsaturated fat,

555
00:31:44.975 --> 00:31:46.892
that are rich in fiber,

556
00:31:47.852 --> 00:31:50.907
that have a good amount of plant protein

557
00:31:50.907 --> 00:31:53.458
and are low in ultra-processed foods,

558
00:31:53.458 --> 00:31:56.878
time and time again lead to good health outcomes.

559
00:31:56.878 --> 00:31:58.216
These are the people

560
00:31:58.216 --> 00:32:02.086
that are experiencing less cardiovascular disease,

561
00:32:02.086 --> 00:32:05.367
less likelihood of having a heart attack or a stroke,

562
00:32:05.367 --> 00:32:07.448
less risk of developing type 2 diabetes,

563
00:32:07.448 --> 00:32:10.771
less risk of fatty liver disease,

564
00:32:10.771 --> 00:32:13.325
less risk of various cancers

565
00:32:13.325 --> 00:32:17.737
and less risk of neurodegenerative diseases.

566
00:32:17.737 --> 00:32:21.781
And the cool thing I guess about that theme

567
00:32:21.781 --> 00:32:25.697
is that there are many variations of that.

568
00:32:25.697 --> 00:32:28.605
That could be a really thoughtfully constructed

569
00:32:28.605 --> 00:32:30.056
Mediterranean diet,

570
00:32:30.056 --> 00:32:33.773
Mediterranean diet as described by Ancel Keys

571
00:32:33.773 --> 00:32:37.402
who was a very prominent scientist in the mid 1900s,

572
00:32:37.402 --> 00:32:40.650
he described that as a largely vegetarian diet.

573
00:32:40.650 --> 00:32:42.344
It does include some animal products,

574
00:32:42.344 --> 00:32:44.360
but they're not as emphasized

575
00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:47.296
as a standard Western diet today.

576
00:32:47.296 --> 00:32:50.295
It could be a really well-done pescetarian diet,

577
00:32:50.295 --> 00:32:53.148
it could be a really well-done vegetarian diet,

578
00:32:53.148 --> 00:32:55.941
it could be a completely plant-exclusive, whole food,

579
00:32:55.941 --> 00:32:57.517
plant-based diet.

580
00:32:57.517 --> 00:33:01.100
And so it means that there are lots of ways

581
00:33:03.219 --> 00:33:06.111
to sort of eat your way to good health.

582
00:33:06.111 --> 00:33:09.868
And that's a sort of less absolute message

583
00:33:09.868 --> 00:33:11.501
and perhaps not as sexy--

584
00:33:11.501 --> 00:33:13.730
Yeah, that makes it difficult,

585
00:33:13.730 --> 00:33:15.785
you can't come up with a label for this

586
00:33:15.785 --> 00:33:20.035
or some kind of contrite, specific, drill down term

587
00:33:22.549 --> 00:33:24.858
that allows it to trend on Twitter

588
00:33:24.858 --> 00:33:27.191
and create a simplified idea

589
00:33:29.441 --> 00:33:32.937
that can get lodged in someone's brain.

590
00:33:32.937 --> 00:33:34.026
But that's science.

591
00:33:34.026 --> 00:33:35.313
That's science

592
00:33:35.313 --> 00:33:38.500
and the way I like to kind of describe this to people

593
00:33:38.500 --> 00:33:40.884
is that any of those diets,

594
00:33:40.884 --> 00:33:42.768
if you compare them to a standard diet

595
00:33:42.768 --> 00:33:46.066
and there are a bunch of studies that have done those,

596
00:33:46.066 --> 00:33:50.207
you'll see significant risk reductions for various diseases.

597
00:33:50.207 --> 00:33:52.407
But if you were to run

598
00:33:52.407 --> 00:33:56.673
let's say a randomized controlled trial from birth

599
00:33:56.673 --> 00:33:59.487
and just include those dietary patterns

600
00:33:59.487 --> 00:34:00.599
that I just mentioned,

601
00:34:00.599 --> 00:34:05.301
which are all sort of very thoughtfully constructed diets

602
00:34:05.301 --> 00:34:10.247
and you would follow those people across their lifespan,

603
00:34:10.247 --> 00:34:13.555
you're unlikely to see any difference in outcomes

604
00:34:13.555 --> 00:34:18.305
because all of those diets are so significantly different

605
00:34:19.707 --> 00:34:21.171
to the standard Western diet,

606
00:34:21.171 --> 00:34:25.588
which is the diet that is provoking disease the most.

607
00:34:28.523 --> 00:34:30.798
So, how do I look at science

608
00:34:30.798 --> 00:34:33.798
and sort of come to that conclusion?

609
00:34:37.021 --> 00:34:40.334
A few different principles kind of guide the work I guess

610
00:34:40.334 --> 00:34:41.167
that I do

611
00:34:42.261 --> 00:34:44.269
and when you understand these principles,

612
00:34:44.269 --> 00:34:46.418
it also makes social media

613
00:34:46.418 --> 00:34:49.668
and the headlines a little frustrating.

614
00:34:51.345 --> 00:34:54.402
But I guess firstly, not all science is equal.

615
00:34:54.402 --> 00:34:57.343
Right, so we could talk about the hierarchy of science.

616
00:34:57.343 --> 00:35:01.096
We hear terms like randomized controlled trials,

617
00:35:01.096 --> 00:35:04.422
population studies, epidemiological studies, all of that

618
00:35:04.422 --> 00:35:08.873
and perhaps we have some familiarity with what those mean,

619
00:35:08.873 --> 00:35:11.780
but walk us through that hierarchy

620
00:35:11.780 --> 00:35:14.560
and how we parse fact from fiction

621
00:35:14.560 --> 00:35:18.887
when it comes to evaluating the multitude of studies

622
00:35:18.887 --> 00:35:23.137
that are out there among which you can find a study

623
00:35:23.976 --> 00:35:25.247
or cherry-pick this or that

624
00:35:25.247 --> 00:35:28.927
that's going to affirm your thesis.

625
00:35:28.927 --> 00:35:33.212
So, yeah, the evidence hierarchy allows us

626
00:35:33.212 --> 00:35:35.670
to kind of add weight to certain evidence

627
00:35:35.670 --> 00:35:38.837
based on how reliable and valid it is.

628
00:35:40.090 --> 00:35:42.465
And at the very bottom,

629
00:35:42.465 --> 00:35:44.216
which is the sort of least reliable

630
00:35:44.216 --> 00:35:47.799
and least valid evidence is expert opinion.

631
00:35:49.012 --> 00:35:53.066
If I just say something without a citation,

632
00:35:53.066 --> 00:35:55.804
someone, a professor or a doctor just says something,

633
00:35:55.804 --> 00:35:58.887
that's considered very weak evidence.

634
00:36:01.043 --> 00:36:04.604
Just above that is laboratory studies.

635
00:36:04.604 --> 00:36:07.024
These are in vitro studies

636
00:36:07.024 --> 00:36:11.692
looking at cells under a microscope or animal studies

637
00:36:11.692 --> 00:36:16.692
and it's not to say that this science is not important.

638
00:36:16.745 --> 00:36:20.389
It can be an important part of piecing together the puzzle.

639
00:36:20.389 --> 00:36:22.972
But it's hypothesis-generating.

640
00:36:23.931 --> 00:36:27.733
The idea that a certain metabolic pathway in a mouse

641
00:36:27.733 --> 00:36:29.101
that you could extrapolate

642
00:36:29.101 --> 00:36:33.087
to how that applies to human health is tenuous the best.

643
00:36:33.087 --> 00:36:38.067
Yeah and we see time and time again lectins or soy

644
00:36:38.067 --> 00:36:42.772
and a lot of people generating fear about these

645
00:36:42.772 --> 00:36:46.671
and they are arriving at that conclusion

646
00:36:46.671 --> 00:36:50.017
based on this sort of in vitro animal-based study

647
00:36:50.017 --> 00:36:54.444
and overlooking the human health outcome data,

648
00:36:54.444 --> 00:36:58.513
which really is a more valid source of information

649
00:36:58.513 --> 00:37:00.416
for public health recommendations.

650
00:37:00.416 --> 00:37:04.607
It's interesting to see what happens in a cell or an animal,

651
00:37:04.607 --> 00:37:06.173
but we also then have to zoom out

652
00:37:06.173 --> 00:37:09.484
and see what happens in a human.

653
00:37:09.484 --> 00:37:13.734
And also you can fall into some traps with research

654
00:37:14.976 --> 00:37:16.976
in an animal or in vitro

655
00:37:18.144 --> 00:37:21.727
where the exposure level is very important.

656
00:37:23.194 --> 00:37:25.329
For example you could...

657
00:37:25.329 --> 00:37:29.912
If you're looking at phytoestrogens in soy for example,

658
00:37:32.710 --> 00:37:35.563
you could look in an animal model

659
00:37:35.563 --> 00:37:37.813
and show estrogenic effects

660
00:37:39.172 --> 00:37:41.865
and then perhaps that could get you worried

661
00:37:41.865 --> 00:37:44.776
about the development of hormone-dependent cancers

662
00:37:44.776 --> 00:37:48.120
like prostate and breast cancer.

663
00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:51.953
But what a lot of those studies have looked at

664
00:37:53.967 --> 00:37:55.886
if you were to look at the exposure level

665
00:37:55.886 --> 00:38:00.026
on a per kilograms of those phytoestrogens,

666
00:38:00.026 --> 00:38:04.387
per kilogram of body mass or pound of body mass,

667
00:38:04.387 --> 00:38:08.088
what you would see is that the exposure level

668
00:38:08.088 --> 00:38:10.755
in that rat is magnitudes higher

669
00:38:12.358 --> 00:38:13.400
than the exposure level

670
00:38:13.400 --> 00:38:15.408
that any human would ever be exposed to

671
00:38:15.408 --> 00:38:18.741
through eating soy or tempeh, et cetera.

672
00:38:21.004 --> 00:38:26.004
So, not only is physiology different at the animal level

673
00:38:26.119 --> 00:38:27.919
between an animal and a human,

674
00:38:27.919 --> 00:38:32.919
but also sometimes exposure can be significantly different.

675
00:38:33.602 --> 00:38:38.352
So, that's I guess the sort of level above expert opinion

676
00:38:40.810 --> 00:38:45.484
and I think something else that is probably useful here

677
00:38:45.484 --> 00:38:48.151
is Fleming discovered penicillin

678
00:38:50.168 --> 00:38:54.086
and I think that was back in 1936 or something.

679
00:38:54.086 --> 00:38:55.776
Yeah, mid 30s.

680
00:38:55.776 --> 00:38:59.109
Yeah and he discovered that through...

681
00:39:01.208 --> 00:39:04.075
Started in a petri dish that he'd left out

682
00:39:04.075 --> 00:39:08.893
and he saw that this mold was destroying the bacterial cells

683
00:39:08.893 --> 00:39:10.355
and then by studying the mold,

684
00:39:10.355 --> 00:39:13.482
he was able to isolate penicillin.

685
00:39:13.482 --> 00:39:18.315
And then he was able to use penicillin effectively in rats

686
00:39:20.391 --> 00:39:21.828
as an antibiotic

687
00:39:21.828 --> 00:39:24.771
and then was able to do it again in humans

688
00:39:24.771 --> 00:39:27.633
and that kind of progression,

689
00:39:27.633 --> 00:39:31.052
that progression is actually very rare.

690
00:39:31.052 --> 00:39:35.775
You might have 50, 100,000 discoveries in a rat

691
00:39:35.775 --> 00:39:39.974
that don't play out in human for every one that does.

692
00:39:39.974 --> 00:39:43.124
So, we hear of these success stories

693
00:39:43.124 --> 00:39:45.092
like Fleming and penicillin and we think,

694
00:39:45.092 --> 00:39:49.509
"Well, the animal model is a great model with sound,"

695
00:39:52.268 --> 00:39:56.650
but what we don't hear of is how often it fails.

696
00:39:56.650 --> 00:39:58.372
So, I think it's just an important reminder

697
00:39:58.372 --> 00:40:02.511
that it is a hypothesis-generating level of science

698
00:40:02.511 --> 00:40:04.565
and really we shouldn't

699
00:40:04.565 --> 00:40:07.060
be making public health recommendations

700
00:40:07.060 --> 00:40:09.594
from that level of science alone

701
00:40:09.594 --> 00:40:12.783
unless there is zero human outcome data

702
00:40:12.783 --> 00:40:17.207
and you wanna take a sort of precautionary stance.

703
00:40:17.207 --> 00:40:18.775
So, then the rank above that

704
00:40:18.775 --> 00:40:22.025
is observational science, epidemiology.

705
00:40:23.284 --> 00:40:25.270
And there are a bunch of different types

706
00:40:25.270 --> 00:40:28.524
of observational study designs,

707
00:40:28.524 --> 00:40:33.100
but the key point here is you're looking at humans

708
00:40:33.100 --> 00:40:35.328
that live in a population

709
00:40:35.328 --> 00:40:38.507
and you're observing the way they live.

710
00:40:38.507 --> 00:40:40.972
In the case of nutrition, you're looking at what they eat

711
00:40:40.972 --> 00:40:45.972
or don't eat and you're looking at specific health outcomes

712
00:40:46.637 --> 00:40:47.864
and you're trying to see

713
00:40:47.864 --> 00:40:52.029
if you can find any sort of tight associations.

714
00:40:52.029 --> 00:40:54.003
These people that are eating more of this

715
00:40:54.003 --> 00:40:56.888
end up having a higher risk of this.

716
00:40:56.888 --> 00:40:59.141
For example, people that are eating

717
00:40:59.141 --> 00:41:01.718
more ultra processed meats

718
00:41:01.718 --> 00:41:05.546
have around 18% higher risk of colorectal cancer

719
00:41:05.546 --> 00:41:08.046
per 50 grams consumed per day.

720
00:41:09.360 --> 00:41:14.058
And it's not a perfect science, but no science is

721
00:41:14.058 --> 00:41:18.391
and I think often observational science gets slammed

722
00:41:21.252 --> 00:41:24.167
by certain folks out there

723
00:41:24.167 --> 00:41:28.171
and one of the common sort of criticisms

724
00:41:28.171 --> 00:41:31.421
is that there is a healthy user effect.

725
00:41:32.343 --> 00:41:36.417
And that is that if you're looking at populations of people

726
00:41:36.417 --> 00:41:39.834
who are eating more fruits and vegetables

727
00:41:40.966 --> 00:41:44.604
and you're looking at health outcomes be it cancer

728
00:41:44.604 --> 00:41:47.180
or cardiovascular disease,

729
00:41:47.180 --> 00:41:48.564
perhaps it's not the fact that

730
00:41:48.564 --> 00:41:50.444
they're eating fruits and vegetables,

731
00:41:50.444 --> 00:41:52.685
but that that's a sign that they live a healthy life.

732
00:41:52.685 --> 00:41:54.756
Sure, there's all these co-founding variables

733
00:41:54.756 --> 00:41:56.879
and the Achilles heel

734
00:41:56.879 --> 00:42:01.574
being that when you're in this observational modality,

735
00:42:01.574 --> 00:42:04.138
you can't control for any of those variables,

736
00:42:04.138 --> 00:42:05.514
people are just living their lives.

737
00:42:05.514 --> 00:42:10.091
So, there's also the risk of life misreporting as well

738
00:42:10.091 --> 00:42:12.866
because it's based on people reporting what they're eating

739
00:42:12.866 --> 00:42:15.088
and how they're living on some level.

740
00:42:15.088 --> 00:42:18.304
So, are people actually writing down

741
00:42:18.304 --> 00:42:19.366
what they're really doing

742
00:42:19.366 --> 00:42:22.297
or are they writing down what they think you wanna hear?

743
00:42:22.297 --> 00:42:25.466
There's room for all kinds of error, but at the same time,

744
00:42:25.466 --> 00:42:28.323
if you're taking massive populations of people,

745
00:42:28.323 --> 00:42:31.836
you can extrapolate from that some general trends

746
00:42:31.836 --> 00:42:34.176
and there is some level of reliability

747
00:42:34.176 --> 00:42:35.468
with what you're observing.

748
00:42:35.468 --> 00:42:36.352
It's just limited

749
00:42:36.352 --> 00:42:39.289
in terms of what you can extrapolate from that.

750
00:42:39.289 --> 00:42:42.622
Yeah, you can't create a sort of cause

751
00:42:43.712 --> 00:42:45.632
and effect conclusion,

752
00:42:45.632 --> 00:42:50.632
you can't say that variable A is causing this outcome.

753
00:42:50.757 --> 00:42:54.007
So, you can only speak to associations,

754
00:42:55.082 --> 00:42:59.804
but I think something that's really important here is that

755
00:42:59.804 --> 00:43:01.136
the researchers are aware

756
00:43:01.136 --> 00:43:04.142
of the limitations of this study design

757
00:43:04.142 --> 00:43:07.892
and so the confounding variables for example,

758
00:43:09.309 --> 00:43:10.711
although you might not be able

759
00:43:10.711 --> 00:43:13.870
to remove their complete effect,

760
00:43:13.870 --> 00:43:16.989
they do through statistics control for them.

761
00:43:16.989 --> 00:43:18.239
So, the idea is

762
00:43:21.909 --> 00:43:24.603
if you have a really well-defined population

763
00:43:24.603 --> 00:43:27.928
that you are as best as possible working out

764
00:43:27.928 --> 00:43:31.200
how often people drink alcohol, how often they smoke,

765
00:43:31.200 --> 00:43:34.950
how often they exercise and in your analysis,

766
00:43:35.791 --> 00:43:39.901
let's say for example you wanna look at the fiber's effect

767
00:43:39.901 --> 00:43:41.835
on a certain health outcome.

768
00:43:41.835 --> 00:43:46.835
The idea with controlling for confounding variables is that

769
00:43:47.376 --> 00:43:50.126
we're not going to compare people

770
00:43:51.185 --> 00:43:56.185
that have significantly different consumption of alcohol.

771
00:43:56.714 --> 00:43:59.960
What we're going to do is compare high and low fiber

772
00:43:59.960 --> 00:44:02.267
between people who are exposed

773
00:44:02.267 --> 00:44:04.571
to the same amount of alcohol.

774
00:44:04.571 --> 00:44:07.087
So, that's the idea through adjustment

775
00:44:07.087 --> 00:44:08.697
is to remove some of the effect

776
00:44:08.697 --> 00:44:11.307
of these confounding variables,

777
00:44:11.307 --> 00:44:14.140
so there is a statistical protocol

778
00:44:15.081 --> 00:44:17.064
called multivariate analysis

779
00:44:17.064 --> 00:44:22.064
and the researchers use this to the best of their ability.

780
00:44:22.492 --> 00:44:24.772
When it comes to the food frequency questionnaires,

781
00:44:24.772 --> 00:44:27.165
they do have their limitations,

782
00:44:27.165 --> 00:44:30.340
but they also validate them in that population too

783
00:44:30.340 --> 00:44:34.078
if it's a good study, that's what they should be doing.

784
00:44:34.078 --> 00:44:34.911
They have tools

785
00:44:34.911 --> 00:44:37.926
to validate that food frequency questionnaire,

786
00:44:37.926 --> 00:44:41.316
which means going out and performing a study

787
00:44:41.316 --> 00:44:43.566
to make sure that as you say,

788
00:44:43.566 --> 00:44:45.415
what people are reporting is reliable

789
00:44:45.415 --> 00:44:47.754
and there are methods of doing that.

790
00:44:47.754 --> 00:44:50.632
So, it's sort of they're not just going out

791
00:44:50.632 --> 00:44:53.248
and completely shooting in the dark,

792
00:44:53.248 --> 00:44:57.375
there are steps and processes within epidemiology

793
00:44:57.375 --> 00:45:00.162
to produce good epidemiology

794
00:45:00.162 --> 00:45:03.373
and of course they can be bad epidemiology as well.

795
00:45:03.373 --> 00:45:06.420
And then above that, what's the next tier up?

796
00:45:06.420 --> 00:45:10.392
So, above that is your randomized controlled trials,

797
00:45:10.392 --> 00:45:12.840
these are clinical interventions

798
00:45:12.840 --> 00:45:17.840
and the idea here is that by having a clinical intervention,

799
00:45:18.845 --> 00:45:21.854
you can remove the effect of other variables

800
00:45:21.854 --> 00:45:23.910
other than what you're looking at.

801
00:45:23.910 --> 00:45:25.413
So, it's very controlled,

802
00:45:25.413 --> 00:45:27.664
perhaps you have two different groups

803
00:45:27.664 --> 00:45:29.825
and the only difference between those two groups

804
00:45:29.825 --> 00:45:31.952
is the exposure that you're looking at.

805
00:45:31.952 --> 00:45:36.316
Maybe it is for example the addition of fiber to a diet

806
00:45:36.316 --> 00:45:39.367
or the addition of a certain vegetable oil.

807
00:45:39.367 --> 00:45:44.117
And therefore you can have a greater degree of confidence

808
00:45:45.662 --> 00:45:47.637
that the outcome that you're measuring

809
00:45:47.637 --> 00:45:50.837
is a result of the difference between those two variables.

810
00:45:50.837 --> 00:45:51.945
The cause and effect.

811
00:45:51.945 --> 00:45:53.434
Yeah.

812
00:45:53.434 --> 00:45:57.735
And then above that, we have these meta analyses.

813
00:45:57.735 --> 00:45:59.807
Like where you take all of these studies

814
00:45:59.807 --> 00:46:03.772
that are of a certain ilk and lump them into one pile

815
00:46:03.772 --> 00:46:06.509
and kind of compare and contrast all of them

816
00:46:06.509 --> 00:46:09.528
to draw even deeper conclusions.

817
00:46:09.528 --> 00:46:12.123
Yeah, so the idea is

818
00:46:12.123 --> 00:46:15.540
if you pull together 30 randomized controlled trials

819
00:46:15.540 --> 00:46:16.667
from around the world

820
00:46:16.667 --> 00:46:19.128
that are all looking at the same thing

821
00:46:19.128 --> 00:46:22.736
from different researchers in different labs

822
00:46:22.736 --> 00:46:27.699
and you pull them together and perform an analysis,

823
00:46:27.699 --> 00:46:31.532
you're likely to reduce the chance of any bias

824
00:46:33.242 --> 00:46:36.470
that could be affecting any individual study.

825
00:46:36.470 --> 00:46:39.553
That said, a meta analysis is also...

826
00:46:41.591 --> 00:46:43.659
There are times where a meta analysis

827
00:46:43.659 --> 00:46:45.659
can be performed poorly,

828
00:46:47.525 --> 00:46:50.981
it has to define what its inclusion criteria is

829
00:46:50.981 --> 00:46:52.895
and therefore what studies it includes

830
00:46:52.895 --> 00:46:57.541
and if you put garbage in you're going to get garbage out.

831
00:46:57.541 --> 00:47:00.236
So, meta analysis again is not bulletproof,

832
00:47:00.236 --> 00:47:02.401
you still have to be able to look at it,

833
00:47:02.401 --> 00:47:07.068
understand what the inclusion and exclusion criteria was

834
00:47:07.988 --> 00:47:10.321
and then look at the results

835
00:47:11.394 --> 00:47:12.936
in the context of everything else

836
00:47:12.936 --> 00:47:15.519
and that's the key here is that

837
00:47:16.434 --> 00:47:17.839
when you're reviewing the science

838
00:47:17.839 --> 00:47:20.132
and you're looking at any individual study,

839
00:47:20.132 --> 00:47:22.073
you then have to step back out

840
00:47:22.073 --> 00:47:25.227
and look at the totality of the evidence.

841
00:47:25.227 --> 00:47:29.289
Ideally what we're looking for is converging lines,

842
00:47:29.289 --> 00:47:32.261
the mechanistic studies under the microscope

843
00:47:32.261 --> 00:47:34.748
and in the animals are pointing in the same direction

844
00:47:34.748 --> 00:47:36.967
as what the epidemiology is pointing in,

845
00:47:36.967 --> 00:47:38.279
is pointing in the same direction

846
00:47:38.279 --> 00:47:42.079
as what the clinical intervention studies are.

847
00:47:42.079 --> 00:47:44.034
I think this is all really important.

848
00:47:44.034 --> 00:47:45.775
I mean, the reason I wanted to dive

849
00:47:45.775 --> 00:47:48.777
into this hierarchy of evidence

850
00:47:48.777 --> 00:47:50.831
is simply because I think it's important

851
00:47:50.831 --> 00:47:51.664
that people develop

852
00:47:51.664 --> 00:47:55.497
some level of evidentiary literacy around this

853
00:47:57.272 --> 00:47:59.453
particularly when people are going online

854
00:47:59.453 --> 00:48:01.999
and they're reading stuff on Facebook or Twitter

855
00:48:01.999 --> 00:48:05.267
and somebody is promoting a certain diet or lifestyle

856
00:48:05.267 --> 00:48:08.527
and they're using study X, Y or Z

857
00:48:08.527 --> 00:48:11.206
as evidentiary proof of this,

858
00:48:11.206 --> 00:48:12.221
it's important to understand,

859
00:48:12.221 --> 00:48:13.742
"Well, what kind of study was that?

860
00:48:13.742 --> 00:48:14.805
What are the circumstances

861
00:48:14.805 --> 00:48:17.984
under which it was conducted, et cetera"

862
00:48:17.984 --> 00:48:19.071
and what you see

863
00:48:19.071 --> 00:48:22.840
with respect to the tribalism that abounds

864
00:48:22.840 --> 00:48:23.673
is a certain group...

865
00:48:23.673 --> 00:48:27.917
And this cuts across every dietary tribe,

866
00:48:27.917 --> 00:48:32.917
like they will throw an epidemiological study under the bus

867
00:48:33.025 --> 00:48:34.220
because that's trash,

868
00:48:34.220 --> 00:48:37.914
but then they'll cite one that supports their perspective

869
00:48:37.914 --> 00:48:42.431
and there's this kind of war of ideas that goes on

870
00:48:42.431 --> 00:48:47.328
where shots are fired across the bow at each other

871
00:48:47.328 --> 00:48:49.470
and for the average consumer, it's like,

872
00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:50.564
"I'm not reading these studies.

873
00:48:50.564 --> 00:48:52.323
I'm not going on NBCI

874
00:48:52.323 --> 00:48:56.194
and like trying to figure all this stuff out."

875
00:48:56.194 --> 00:48:58.292
And it's really hard to find somebody

876
00:48:58.292 --> 00:49:01.366
who's coming from an objective point of view

877
00:49:01.366 --> 00:49:03.338
to help people parse all of this out

878
00:49:03.338 --> 00:49:07.862
and I feel like you're a good actor in this space,

879
00:49:07.862 --> 00:49:09.741
somebody who's willing to admit when they're wrong,

880
00:49:09.741 --> 00:49:12.248
who's teachable, who's learning as we go,

881
00:49:12.248 --> 00:49:14.532
sharing what you're finding along the way,

882
00:49:14.532 --> 00:49:17.324
calling out BS when you see it

883
00:49:17.324 --> 00:49:20.074
and that's a real public service.

884
00:49:21.818 --> 00:49:24.678
There's a lot of false equivalence

885
00:49:24.678 --> 00:49:26.481
is how I like to describe it

886
00:49:26.481 --> 00:49:31.231
where on social media, every one now if you read the blog

887
00:49:32.301 --> 00:49:36.884
or the posts on social, the first two words, study show

888
00:49:37.976 --> 00:49:42.809
and yeah how can studies show such contradictory positions

889
00:49:45.529 --> 00:49:49.145
and how can such contradictory positions exist?

890
00:49:49.145 --> 00:49:51.978
So, it's frustrating and I can see

891
00:49:53.484 --> 00:49:56.271
from someone who's just trying to make sense of things

892
00:49:56.271 --> 00:49:59.154
that it's incredibly difficult

893
00:49:59.154 --> 00:50:02.146
and I think many people are left with this idea

894
00:50:02.146 --> 00:50:04.407
that science isn't settled,

895
00:50:04.407 --> 00:50:07.308
science doesn't understand what a healthy diet is

896
00:50:07.308 --> 00:50:10.661
looking at all these different opinions that are out there

897
00:50:10.661 --> 00:50:13.610
and then when you're in that position,

898
00:50:13.610 --> 00:50:15.605
it's very easy to kind of just throw your hands up

899
00:50:15.605 --> 00:50:18.351
in the air and stick with what you know

900
00:50:18.351 --> 00:50:20.851
and where you're comfortable.

901
00:50:22.149 --> 00:50:25.160
So, I do think it's important

902
00:50:25.160 --> 00:50:29.090
for people to understand this evidence hierarchy,

903
00:50:29.090 --> 00:50:30.840
but equally speaking,

904
00:50:32.104 --> 00:50:36.687
I think something that I've also sort of grown to learn

905
00:50:37.894 --> 00:50:41.887
over time in trying to communicate this message

906
00:50:41.887 --> 00:50:46.009
is not to expect too much from the average person

907
00:50:46.009 --> 00:50:47.493
trying to make sense of this.

908
00:50:47.493 --> 00:50:51.887
And what I mean by that is people are busy,

909
00:50:51.887 --> 00:50:53.819
they have their own careers, their own jobs

910
00:50:53.819 --> 00:50:55.702
and they're not like me sitting down

911
00:50:55.702 --> 00:50:58.537
and reading nutrition science papers

912
00:50:58.537 --> 00:51:01.239
seven, eight hours a day and geeking out on them.

913
00:51:01.239 --> 00:51:02.072
And that's cool,

914
00:51:02.072 --> 00:51:04.989
they've got their own passions and interests.

915
00:51:04.989 --> 00:51:08.239
And we hear this kind of throwaway line

916
00:51:09.283 --> 00:51:12.950
of do your own research or I did my research

917
00:51:14.624 --> 00:51:18.541
and I kind of laugh sometimes when I hear that,

918
00:51:20.347 --> 00:51:21.924
I mean, really doing your own research

919
00:51:21.924 --> 00:51:25.302
if you think about that phrase literally

920
00:51:25.302 --> 00:51:28.956
it would mean going and getting trained in a bachelor's

921
00:51:28.956 --> 00:51:32.265
and then becoming a professor and having a research question

922
00:51:32.265 --> 00:51:37.200
and then raising grant money and performing the research

923
00:51:37.200 --> 00:51:39.783
and hiring the statisticians...

924
00:51:41.070 --> 00:51:43.324
I always fumble that word

925
00:51:43.324 --> 00:51:47.907
and the research assistants and performing the research

926
00:51:49.628 --> 00:51:51.484
or it could be someone

927
00:51:51.484 --> 00:51:53.941
who's maybe not performing the research themselves,

928
00:51:53.941 --> 00:51:58.191
but is trained to read it and to get into the study

929
00:52:00.471 --> 00:52:04.274
and understand exactly what they're looking at.

930
00:52:04.274 --> 00:52:08.581
And to look at the exposure that I spoke about before

931
00:52:08.581 --> 00:52:09.725
compared to what

932
00:52:09.725 --> 00:52:12.847
if we're looking at a certain food or nutrient

933
00:52:12.847 --> 00:52:14.239
and we're looking at an outcome,

934
00:52:14.239 --> 00:52:15.607
what are we comparing it to?

935
00:52:15.607 --> 00:52:19.857
Such an important question that's often overlooked,

936
00:52:20.847 --> 00:52:23.642
understanding the evidence hierarchy, being able to zoom out

937
00:52:23.642 --> 00:52:26.319
and think about the totality of the evidence,

938
00:52:26.319 --> 00:52:27.597
all of this stuff,

939
00:52:27.597 --> 00:52:31.762
I think it's a big ask to us the average person

940
00:52:31.762 --> 00:52:35.128
to be doing that on the regular to make sense of things

941
00:52:35.128 --> 00:52:39.758
and this is where I think these consensus guideline papers

942
00:52:39.758 --> 00:52:42.348
from the American College of Cardiology

943
00:52:42.348 --> 00:52:45.681
or the American College of Endocrinology

944
00:52:47.064 --> 00:52:50.327
or the American Cancer Society,

945
00:52:50.327 --> 00:52:53.123
these guideline papers are,

946
00:52:53.123 --> 00:52:54.525
they are those people

947
00:52:54.525 --> 00:52:57.655
who are conducting the research themselves,

948
00:52:57.655 --> 00:52:59.454
who are trained to read the research

949
00:52:59.454 --> 00:53:01.999
to understand the totality of the evidence

950
00:53:01.999 --> 00:53:04.749
and then provide recommendations.

951
00:53:05.881 --> 00:53:10.298
And so when I see someone say, "Do your own research"

952
00:53:12.648 --> 00:53:17.099
to someone, I think that it's a little unfair

953
00:53:17.099 --> 00:53:18.698
and I think in many ways,

954
00:53:18.698 --> 00:53:23.698
what's more constructive is to point them to a good resource

955
00:53:23.756 --> 00:53:28.198
where there are very credible, experienced people

956
00:53:28.198 --> 00:53:31.074
that have been engaged to do exactly that.

957
00:53:31.074 --> 00:53:33.479
Do your own research often means

958
00:53:33.479 --> 00:53:37.349
I found the guy who I like and I resonate with him

959
00:53:37.349 --> 00:53:40.306
and I find him to be credible and believable.

960
00:53:40.306 --> 00:53:41.297
Yes.

961
00:53:41.297 --> 00:53:42.840
On some level, there's a leap of faith

962
00:53:42.840 --> 00:53:44.738
and a level of trust

963
00:53:44.738 --> 00:53:49.155
that that person is telling me the straight truth.

964
00:53:49.155 --> 00:53:52.155
And the less aware you are I guess

965
00:53:54.851 --> 00:53:56.573
of the way that science works,

966
00:53:56.573 --> 00:54:01.164
I think the more effected you can be by confirmation bias.

967
00:54:01.164 --> 00:54:03.831
And so it can be easy to just go

968
00:54:04.872 --> 00:54:08.884
and find the information that reinforces your current views,

969
00:54:08.884 --> 00:54:12.002
established views, your identity

970
00:54:12.002 --> 00:54:14.151
and at the same time turn a blind eye

971
00:54:14.151 --> 00:54:17.761
to anything that perhaps challenges that

972
00:54:17.761 --> 00:54:20.261
and so I think that right now,

973
00:54:25.813 --> 00:54:27.939
I empathize with the average person

974
00:54:27.939 --> 00:54:31.606
trying to make sense of what on earth to eat

975
00:54:33.486 --> 00:54:36.421
and people are terribly confused

976
00:54:36.421 --> 00:54:38.227
and caught in the middle of all this.

977
00:54:38.227 --> 00:54:42.239
So, as somebody who has quote, unquote, done the research,

978
00:54:42.239 --> 00:54:44.884
like you're a guy who's done the research,

979
00:54:44.884 --> 00:54:47.211
you've looked at the totality of evidence,

980
00:54:47.211 --> 00:54:49.131
you've extrapolated from that

981
00:54:49.131 --> 00:54:53.556
to develop this thesis that you spoke about earlier,

982
00:54:53.556 --> 00:54:56.889
which on some level I feel could be said

983
00:54:57.873 --> 00:55:02.206
in more simple terms to be a plant-predominant diet,

984
00:55:03.102 --> 00:55:06.957
perhaps not a plant-exclusive diet, although that's great,

985
00:55:06.957 --> 00:55:10.874
but a diet that leans in towards mostly plants.

986
00:55:12.327 --> 00:55:13.377
Is that fair?

987
00:55:13.377 --> 00:55:14.210
That's fair.

988
00:55:14.210 --> 00:55:18.024
So, a plant-predominant to a plant-exclusive diet

989
00:55:18.024 --> 00:55:19.941
and why does this work?

990
00:55:21.490 --> 00:55:26.490
Well, there's a two-fold kind of effect going on here.

991
00:55:26.678 --> 00:55:31.678
You're reducing your exposure to certain foods and nutrients

992
00:55:31.815 --> 00:55:36.658
that we know when consumed in excess will promote disease.

993
00:55:36.658 --> 00:55:39.604
Things like saturated fat for example

994
00:55:39.604 --> 00:55:42.061
that are the most common source of saturated fats

995
00:55:42.061 --> 00:55:44.246
is in animal foods,

996
00:55:44.246 --> 00:55:46.617
you're reducing your exposure

997
00:55:46.617 --> 00:55:49.450
to certain carcinogenic compounds,

998
00:55:51.139 --> 00:55:54.081
you're at the same time reducing your exposure

999
00:55:54.081 --> 00:55:56.196
to ultra-processed foods,

1000
00:55:56.196 --> 00:55:59.173
let's not forget that in this country,

1001
00:55:59.173 --> 00:56:01.125
60% of the average person's calories

1002
00:56:01.125 --> 00:56:03.238
come from ultra-processed foods.

1003
00:56:03.238 --> 00:56:08.238
And in Australia, it's nearly 50% and similar in the UK

1004
00:56:08.445 --> 00:56:09.612
and in Canada.

1005
00:56:11.010 --> 00:56:14.843
And we know from clinical intervention studies

1006
00:56:15.975 --> 00:56:18.276
and we could go into that if you want to explore it

1007
00:56:18.276 --> 00:56:22.609
that these ultra-processed foods are hyperpalatable,

1008
00:56:24.886 --> 00:56:29.615
they make it super easy to overconsume calories.

1009
00:56:29.615 --> 00:56:31.865
They promote hedonic hunger

1010
00:56:32.969 --> 00:56:34.412
where we're consuming calories

1011
00:56:34.412 --> 00:56:38.161
in the absence of a physiological requirement

1012
00:56:38.161 --> 00:56:41.453
and they're designed by food scientists to do that,

1013
00:56:41.453 --> 00:56:43.654
to hit the bliss point

1014
00:56:43.654 --> 00:56:46.232
and they've done a good job

1015
00:56:46.232 --> 00:56:49.573
if you look at the consumption levels,

1016
00:56:49.573 --> 00:56:54.323
so we're reducing our exposure to certain parts of a diet

1017
00:56:55.873 --> 00:56:58.317
that are promoting poor health and then at the same time

1018
00:56:58.317 --> 00:57:01.567
we're increasing our exposure to fiber.

1019
00:57:03.648 --> 00:57:07.940
And fiber is probably the most overlooked nutrient.

1020
00:57:07.940 --> 00:57:11.170
If there was one thing that I was going to say to people

1021
00:57:11.170 --> 00:57:15.599
to focus on that would just straighten up their diet

1022
00:57:15.599 --> 00:57:19.299
and would reduce their risk of so many of these diseases

1023
00:57:19.299 --> 00:57:21.650
and help them live healthier for longer,

1024
00:57:21.650 --> 00:57:24.623
it would be to get more fiber into their diet.

1025
00:57:24.623 --> 00:57:26.920
And I know you've had our mutual friend, Dr. B--

1026
00:57:26.920 --> 00:57:30.024
Yeah, at your insistence earlier,

1027
00:57:30.024 --> 00:57:31.061
you were the guy who was like,

1028
00:57:31.061 --> 00:57:32.269
"You gotta check this dude out"

1029
00:57:32.269 --> 00:57:34.746
and then I had him on of course he's a rockstar.

1030
00:57:34.746 --> 00:57:36.688
He's been on your show many times,

1031
00:57:36.688 --> 00:57:38.113
he wrote the foreword to your book.

1032
00:57:38.113 --> 00:57:42.077
He wrote the foreword and the microbiome is wild

1033
00:57:42.077 --> 00:57:46.244
and in many ways what's going on in our microbiome

1034
00:57:48.179 --> 00:57:52.634
and the loss of biodiversity reflects the macro world.

1035
00:57:52.634 --> 00:57:55.604
And it's no coincidence that farming

1036
00:57:55.604 --> 00:57:59.181
and food production is affecting both.

1037
00:57:59.181 --> 00:58:03.931
So, the introduction of more fiber to the diet is crucial

1038
00:58:04.868 --> 00:58:06.903
for nourishing the microbiome,

1039
00:58:06.903 --> 00:58:11.736
for creating a healthy balance of the 38 trillion microbes

1040
00:58:12.831 --> 00:58:16.723
that call your large intestine their home.

1041
00:58:16.723 --> 00:58:20.146
And when you are nourishing them and feeding them,

1042
00:58:20.146 --> 00:58:22.142
they reward you.

1043
00:58:22.142 --> 00:58:25.744
And so that's obviously an extremely important benefit

1044
00:58:25.744 --> 00:58:29.626
of increasing these plant foods in the diet,

1045
00:58:29.626 --> 00:58:33.449
you're also increasing the amount of plant protein

1046
00:58:33.449 --> 00:58:37.662
in your diet and we see very, very consistently

1047
00:58:37.662 --> 00:58:39.259
in large observational studies

1048
00:58:39.259 --> 00:58:41.689
that look at substitution analyses

1049
00:58:41.689 --> 00:58:44.663
what happens when you reduce animal protein

1050
00:58:44.663 --> 00:58:47.592
in someone's diet and increase plant protein.

1051
00:58:47.592 --> 00:58:49.963
And we see dramatic reductions

1052
00:58:49.963 --> 00:58:52.779
in the risk of all of these chronic diseases,

1053
00:58:52.779 --> 00:58:55.650
we see reductions in total mortality,

1054
00:58:55.650 --> 00:58:57.736
the risk of premature death.

1055
00:58:57.736 --> 00:58:59.903
So, that's a huge benefit.

1056
00:59:00.806 --> 00:59:05.278
And perhaps one of the most important benefits

1057
00:59:05.278 --> 00:59:08.387
is the exposure to all of these phytochemicals

1058
00:59:08.387 --> 00:59:12.387
and there are 5, 10, 20,000 plus phytochemicals.

1059
00:59:14.599 --> 00:59:17.680
We haven't even yet identified all of them.

1060
00:59:17.680 --> 00:59:21.535
And you've probably heard a lot about polyphenols

1061
00:59:21.535 --> 00:59:26.166
and people probably immediately think of resveratrol

1062
00:59:26.166 --> 00:59:29.666
in red wine or chlorogenic acids in coffee

1063
00:59:30.555 --> 00:59:35.009
or curcumin in turmeric or catechins in green tea.

1064
00:59:35.009 --> 00:59:38.044
These polyphenol compounds,

1065
00:59:38.044 --> 00:59:40.711
while not essential nutrients...

1066
00:59:42.634 --> 00:59:44.115
And when I say not essential nutrients,

1067
00:59:44.115 --> 00:59:46.539
they are not essential for your survival,

1068
00:59:46.539 --> 00:59:49.940
but they are essential for promoting optimal health.

1069
00:59:49.940 --> 00:59:54.501
And we know just through work in the last 10 years

1070
00:59:54.501 --> 00:59:57.175
that most of the polyphenols you eat

1071
00:59:57.175 --> 00:59:59.588
actually pass through your small intestine,

1072
00:59:59.588 --> 01:00:02.155
they land in your large intestine

1073
01:00:02.155 --> 01:00:03.362
and it's the microbiome,

1074
01:00:03.362 --> 01:00:07.327
it's the bacteria that metabolize them

1075
01:00:07.327 --> 01:00:09.223
and produce 1000s of metabolites

1076
01:00:09.223 --> 01:00:11.736
that have downstream effects through the body

1077
01:00:11.736 --> 01:00:14.442
that are driving down inflammation

1078
01:00:14.442 --> 01:00:18.859
both systemically, also in the central nervous system

1079
01:00:20.120 --> 01:00:24.120
and so as we're making these changes to our diet

1080
01:00:26.214 --> 01:00:30.881
and eating in this more plant-predominant pattern,

1081
01:00:30.881 --> 01:00:33.185
this way of eating that the Blue Zones aid,

1082
01:00:33.185 --> 01:00:36.102
Okinawans in Japan, the Sardinians,

1083
01:00:38.009 --> 01:00:40.216
the folks in Loma Linda

1084
01:00:40.216 --> 01:00:42.464
who on average are living around 10 years longer

1085
01:00:42.464 --> 01:00:44.714
than the standard American,

1086
01:00:45.815 --> 01:00:49.398
we are producing the risk of these diseases

1087
01:00:51.828 --> 01:00:55.579
through a range of risk factors that we're modifying.

1088
01:00:55.579 --> 01:00:57.833
That's the key part here.

1089
01:00:57.833 --> 01:01:02.324
By driving down cholesterol, by driving down blood pressure,

1090
01:01:02.324 --> 01:01:06.824
by improving our insulin resistance, all of this stuff

1091
01:01:08.018 --> 01:01:11.634
along with an anti-inflammatory diet

1092
01:01:11.634 --> 01:01:15.884
that is helping promote a more robust immune system

1093
01:01:16.849 --> 01:01:19.709
is creating a healthier body.

1094
01:01:19.709 --> 01:01:21.922
So, it's multi-factored

1095
01:01:21.922 --> 01:01:24.141
in the sense that moving towards

1096
01:01:24.141 --> 01:01:27.037
a more plant-predominant diet,

1097
01:01:27.037 --> 01:01:30.072
you're crowding out space for some of those foods

1098
01:01:30.072 --> 01:01:32.225
that are contributing to the ailments

1099
01:01:32.225 --> 01:01:35.140
and the chronic lifestyle diseases.

1100
01:01:35.140 --> 01:01:36.988
But on top of that,

1101
01:01:36.988 --> 01:01:39.848
by increasing the diversity

1102
01:01:39.848 --> 01:01:43.297
of what that plant-predominant diet looks like,

1103
01:01:43.297 --> 01:01:46.714
you are enhancing health in no small part

1104
01:01:48.294 --> 01:01:50.841
due to the fact that you're feeding your microbiome

1105
01:01:50.841 --> 01:01:51.716
in a manner

1106
01:01:51.716 --> 01:01:55.548
in which has all of these extra downstream impacts

1107
01:01:55.548 --> 01:01:57.381
on health enhancement.

1108
01:01:58.676 --> 01:02:01.664
Yeah, let's perhaps zoom in on the microbiome

1109
01:02:01.664 --> 01:02:03.164
for a moment here.

1110
01:02:04.144 --> 01:02:07.474
And not to repeat what you've done with Dr. B,

1111
01:02:07.474 --> 01:02:10.224
but perhaps we can build on that.

1112
01:02:13.714 --> 01:02:18.297
The human body is made up of around 23,000 human genes.

1113
01:02:20.988 --> 01:02:25.905
There are around 100 times that many genes in our microbes.

1114
01:02:27.259 --> 01:02:31.604
So, we are genetically more microbe than we are human.

1115
01:02:31.604 --> 01:02:35.314
And I think there's been some revision on the maths

1116
01:02:35.314 --> 01:02:38.532
around how many human cells are there to microbe cells.

1117
01:02:38.532 --> 01:02:42.012
Right, yeah, there's been some changes.

1118
01:02:42.012 --> 01:02:46.186
There's been some changes and used to be 10 to one,

1119
01:02:46.186 --> 01:02:48.737
it was thought that it was 10 microbial cells

1120
01:02:48.737 --> 01:02:50.677
to one human cell.

1121
01:02:50.677 --> 01:02:55.271
And that's been revised to around 1.3 microbial cells

1122
01:02:55.271 --> 01:02:56.986
to one human cell.

1123
01:02:56.986 --> 01:02:58.103
It's a big change.

1124
01:02:58.103 --> 01:02:59.922
It's a big change,

1125
01:02:59.922 --> 01:03:02.922
but it still speaks to the fact that

1126
01:03:04.599 --> 01:03:05.667
from a cell point of view,

1127
01:03:05.667 --> 01:03:07.732
we still have more microbial cells

1128
01:03:07.732 --> 01:03:09.580
than we do have human cells.

1129
01:03:09.580 --> 01:03:13.771
I mentioned before there are around 38 trillion microbes

1130
01:03:13.771 --> 01:03:16.354
in our gastrointestinal system.

1131
01:03:20.088 --> 01:03:21.609
That is more than the number of stars

1132
01:03:21.609 --> 01:03:23.776
that are in the Milky Way.

1133
01:03:25.892 --> 01:03:26.725
It's wild.

1134
01:03:29.978 --> 01:03:31.940
There is a whole lot going in there

1135
01:03:31.940 --> 01:03:35.690
that we have underappreciated for a long time

1136
01:03:36.688 --> 01:03:41.355
and these microbes, they rely on us and we rely on them.

1137
01:03:45.022 --> 01:03:48.029
It's a symbiotic relationship.

1138
01:03:48.029 --> 01:03:50.741
And you mentioned diversity

1139
01:03:50.741 --> 01:03:54.166
and the reason why diversity of plants

1140
01:03:54.166 --> 01:03:58.333
in the diet is important is that we have about 500

1141
01:03:59.784 --> 01:04:04.201
to 1000 different species within our gut of bacteria.

1142
01:04:06.501 --> 01:04:08.918
And a more diverse microbiome

1143
01:04:12.011 --> 01:04:14.226
is associated with better health.

1144
01:04:14.226 --> 01:04:16.952
We see healthy people have a more diverse microbiome.

1145
01:04:16.952 --> 01:04:20.254
Increasing the number of species of those microbes.

1146
01:04:20.254 --> 01:04:25.254
Yes, so, most people will have a sort of predefined number

1147
01:04:25.487 --> 01:04:28.398
of species between 500 to 1000

1148
01:04:28.398 --> 01:04:29.788
and that is usually defined

1149
01:04:29.788 --> 01:04:32.840
in the first three years of life.

1150
01:04:32.840 --> 01:04:35.090
And so increasing diversity

1151
01:04:38.721 --> 01:04:41.387
is really working with what you already have,

1152
01:04:41.387 --> 01:04:45.350
but it's increasing the prevalence of the healthy bacteria,

1153
01:04:45.350 --> 01:04:47.551
the numbers of them and suppressing

1154
01:04:47.551 --> 01:04:51.762
or reducing the numbers of the pathogenic bacteria.

1155
01:04:51.762 --> 01:04:54.835
I think perhaps a good analogy of this

1156
01:04:54.835 --> 01:04:57.782
is thinking about society.

1157
01:04:57.782 --> 01:04:59.798
When there's equal representation of people

1158
01:04:59.798 --> 01:05:01.040
from different cultures,

1159
01:05:01.040 --> 01:05:04.123
we have a better functioning society.

1160
01:05:05.919 --> 01:05:09.391
Each of these people from diverse backgrounds

1161
01:05:09.391 --> 01:05:13.295
are bringing different skills to the table.

1162
01:05:13.295 --> 01:05:16.163
And same thing in society,

1163
01:05:16.163 --> 01:05:19.007
there are of course a minority of people

1164
01:05:19.007 --> 01:05:21.157
who get up to no good and--

1165
01:05:21.157 --> 01:05:21.990
Pathogenic citizens.

1166
01:05:21.990 --> 01:05:25.907
The pathogenics and we wanna keep them at bay

1167
01:05:26.774 --> 01:05:29.569
and that's the same thing with the microbiome.

1168
01:05:29.569 --> 01:05:33.977
And so when you have better representation,

1169
01:05:33.977 --> 01:05:38.221
essentially if you think about a football team as well,

1170
01:05:38.221 --> 01:05:41.535
you could think about on any given day

1171
01:05:41.535 --> 01:05:43.917
if a couple of players aren't performing their best,

1172
01:05:43.917 --> 01:05:46.041
some of the other players can pick up the slack

1173
01:05:46.041 --> 01:05:48.401
and you can still win the game.

1174
01:05:48.401 --> 01:05:49.234
That's what it means,

1175
01:05:49.234 --> 01:05:52.272
when you have a more diverse microbiome, more robust,

1176
01:05:52.272 --> 01:05:53.794
you're more likely to be winning

1177
01:05:53.794 --> 01:05:55.891
in producing these compounds

1178
01:05:55.891 --> 01:05:59.642
that are protecting your gut lining.

1179
01:05:59.642 --> 01:06:02.038
And I'll go into that.

1180
01:06:02.038 --> 01:06:06.225
These different species essentially like you and I,

1181
01:06:06.225 --> 01:06:08.737
have different food preferences,

1182
01:06:08.737 --> 01:06:11.154
slightly different taste buds

1183
01:06:13.038 --> 01:06:16.871
and so the prebiotic fiber and the polyphenols

1184
01:06:18.961 --> 01:06:22.325
and the resistant starch in certain foods

1185
01:06:22.325 --> 01:06:24.794
will help promote the growth of certain species,

1186
01:06:24.794 --> 01:06:27.989
but then other foods will help promote the growth

1187
01:06:27.989 --> 01:06:29.752
of different species.

1188
01:06:29.752 --> 01:06:32.720
And so when you have a diverse range of plants in your diet,

1189
01:06:32.720 --> 01:06:35.949
you're keeping more of these guys happier.

1190
01:06:35.949 --> 01:06:40.282
And as you feed them, they're producing metabolites.

1191
01:06:41.797 --> 01:06:44.797
So, they ferment these food products

1192
01:06:47.673 --> 01:06:50.793
that pass through undigested into the large intestine

1193
01:06:50.793 --> 01:06:52.335
and as they're fermenting them,

1194
01:06:52.335 --> 01:06:54.709
they are producing these metabolites,

1195
01:06:54.709 --> 01:06:57.741
there are 1000s of these, but probably the most studied

1196
01:06:57.741 --> 01:07:01.119
and spoken about are short-chain fatty acids.

1197
01:07:01.119 --> 01:07:03.406
And of the short-chain fatty acids,

1198
01:07:03.406 --> 01:07:06.622
butyrate probably gets most of the attention

1199
01:07:06.622 --> 01:07:08.603
and it kinda rightly so.

1200
01:07:08.603 --> 01:07:13.520
Butyrate helps to keep the mucosal layer of the gut intact,

1201
01:07:16.048 --> 01:07:20.228
healthy, along with the endothelial cells

1202
01:07:20.228 --> 01:07:25.228
which sit right between it, just a very thin layer of cells

1203
01:07:25.277 --> 01:07:27.694
sit between all the microbes,

1204
01:07:28.569 --> 01:07:30.853
the 38 trillion microbes

1205
01:07:30.853 --> 01:07:33.652
and then 70% of your immune system.

1206
01:07:33.652 --> 01:07:36.865
Which is important because we're seeing this rise

1207
01:07:36.865 --> 01:07:39.108
in the incidence of conditions

1208
01:07:39.108 --> 01:07:41.978
like all sort of colitis, et cetera,

1209
01:07:41.978 --> 01:07:43.427
all of these gut digestion issues

1210
01:07:43.427 --> 01:07:47.344
which really relate to gut lining permeability.

1211
01:07:49.083 --> 01:07:51.508
Yeah, if you look at the surge

1212
01:07:51.508 --> 01:07:54.758
in autoimmune conditions and allergies,

1213
01:07:58.306 --> 01:07:59.862
they've increased far too rapidly

1214
01:07:59.862 --> 01:08:03.260
for that to be down to human genetics.

1215
01:08:03.260 --> 01:08:06.275
Human genetics take a lot longer than that to change.

1216
01:08:06.275 --> 01:08:09.966
We're talking hundreds of 1000s, millions of years usually.

1217
01:08:09.966 --> 01:08:12.549
So, something else has changed.

1218
01:08:13.798 --> 01:08:16.715
And when we look at the microbiome,

1219
01:08:19.361 --> 01:08:21.726
it's clear that people

1220
01:08:21.726 --> 01:08:26.726
who are experiencing these types of autoimmune conditions,

1221
01:08:27.305 --> 01:08:29.638
they tend to have dysbiosis.

1222
01:08:31.842 --> 01:08:34.965
Now, I think it would be far-fetched

1223
01:08:34.965 --> 01:08:37.495
to say that a causal relationship has been established

1224
01:08:37.495 --> 01:08:39.206
for some of these,

1225
01:08:39.206 --> 01:08:42.786
a lot of this is epidemiology and it's an association,

1226
01:08:42.786 --> 01:08:46.453
but there are mechanisms that do make sense.

1227
01:08:47.373 --> 01:08:51.123
And so the idea is that if you have dysbiosis

1228
01:08:51.970 --> 01:08:56.779
and you have less of these short-chain fatty acids

1229
01:08:56.779 --> 01:08:57.612
being produced--

1230
01:08:57.612 --> 01:08:58.613
And just to be clear,

1231
01:08:58.613 --> 01:09:01.008
dysbiosis being a dysregulation

1232
01:09:01.008 --> 01:09:03.508
of your gut health in general.

1233
01:09:06.250 --> 01:09:08.461
Yes, so you've lost that diversity

1234
01:09:08.461 --> 01:09:13.294
and you get a relative reduction in the healthful microbes

1235
01:09:15.311 --> 01:09:16.232
and you get an increase

1236
01:09:16.232 --> 01:09:19.565
in the pathogenic inflammatory microbes.

1237
01:09:20.524 --> 01:09:21.550
And when this happens,

1238
01:09:21.550 --> 01:09:23.477
you get breakdown of that mucosal layer,

1239
01:09:23.477 --> 01:09:25.952
you get separation of the endothelial cells,

1240
01:09:25.952 --> 01:09:27.751
so the endothelial cells are held together

1241
01:09:27.751 --> 01:09:30.667
by tight junctions and butyrate,

1242
01:09:30.667 --> 01:09:33.294
that short-chain fatty acid that I mentioned,

1243
01:09:33.294 --> 01:09:36.874
one of the functions that it has in the gut

1244
01:09:36.874 --> 01:09:40.750
is to help keep those tight junctions in good health.

1245
01:09:40.750 --> 01:09:45.500
And so as you get this more inflammatory state in the gut

1246
01:09:47.431 --> 01:09:51.348
and dysbiosis, you get breakdown of that layer.

1247
01:09:52.333 --> 01:09:57.311
This is important because it allows the flow of molecules

1248
01:09:57.311 --> 01:10:00.061
from the gut into the bloodstream

1249
01:10:01.269 --> 01:10:03.944
that shouldn't be getting through the gut lining.

1250
01:10:03.944 --> 01:10:08.258
And these are often referred to as bacterial endotoxins

1251
01:10:08.258 --> 01:10:12.738
and they essentially travel into the bloodstream

1252
01:10:12.738 --> 01:10:16.100
where they rev up the immune system.

1253
01:10:16.100 --> 01:10:19.433
And when you're driving up inflammation,

1254
01:10:20.663 --> 01:10:25.018
you're increasing oxidative stress for the body

1255
01:10:25.018 --> 01:10:28.536
and oxidative stress leads to DNA damage

1256
01:10:28.536 --> 01:10:30.883
and then you're like starting to accelerate

1257
01:10:30.883 --> 01:10:33.175
the aging process of tissues.

1258
01:10:33.175 --> 01:10:36.795
So, what are the foods that are most butyrate-promoting?

1259
01:10:36.795 --> 01:10:38.779
Great question.

1260
01:10:38.779 --> 01:10:41.530
Have you heard a professor Christopher Gardner?

1261
01:10:41.530 --> 01:10:42.742
I think so.

1262
01:10:42.742 --> 01:10:47.314
So, this brings us to a very interesting study

1263
01:10:47.314 --> 01:10:49.208
that just came out.

1264
01:10:49.208 --> 01:10:51.541
He's a professor at Stanford

1265
01:10:52.455 --> 01:10:55.451
and he was working with the Sonambergs.

1266
01:10:55.451 --> 01:10:58.351
The Sonamnbergs are arguably

1267
01:10:58.351 --> 01:11:03.351
some of the leading microbiome scientists in the world and--

1268
01:11:03.464 --> 01:11:04.297
Is it a a husband and a wife--

1269
01:11:04.297 --> 01:11:05.130
Yeah.

1270
01:11:05.130 --> 01:11:06.232
Yeah, I've heard of them.

1271
01:11:06.232 --> 01:11:11.232
And they just conducted a really eloquently designed study

1272
01:11:14.128 --> 01:11:18.295
that took 38 adults if my memory serves me correct

1273
01:11:19.258 --> 01:11:22.896
and randomized them into two groups.

1274
01:11:22.896 --> 01:11:25.396
And the idea was really to see

1275
01:11:27.644 --> 01:11:30.068
how fiber affects the microbiome

1276
01:11:30.068 --> 01:11:32.777
and how fermented foods do.

1277
01:11:32.777 --> 01:11:35.873
And of course fermented foods contain probiotics.

1278
01:11:35.873 --> 01:11:40.540
So, the hypothesis was that maybe they improve diversity

1279
01:11:41.388 --> 01:11:44.770
and the idea is to test and see if there's a difference

1280
01:11:44.770 --> 01:11:48.203
between fiber and fermented foods.

1281
01:11:48.203 --> 01:11:50.620
And this was a 10-week study.

1282
01:11:52.270 --> 01:11:56.283
The subjects that were told to increase fiber,

1283
01:11:56.283 --> 01:12:00.952
they increased their fiber from 20 grams to 40.

1284
01:12:00.952 --> 01:12:03.077
The subjects in the fermented food group,

1285
01:12:03.077 --> 01:12:08.077
they were told to eat six serves of fermented foods a day.

1286
01:12:08.444 --> 01:12:12.861
To give you an example, one combo jar was two serves.

1287
01:12:13.973 --> 01:12:16.664
There was sauerkraut, there was kefir,

1288
01:12:16.664 --> 01:12:18.614
there was some yogurt,

1289
01:12:18.614 --> 01:12:19.447
there was about five

1290
01:12:19.447 --> 01:12:23.447
or six different probiotic-rich, fermented foods

1291
01:12:24.924 --> 01:12:29.341
and the results of this study were really interesting

1292
01:12:30.302 --> 01:12:32.125
because there was some findings

1293
01:12:32.125 --> 01:12:34.322
that I guess challenged the idea

1294
01:12:34.322 --> 01:12:37.655
that fiber leads to increased diversity.

1295
01:12:41.853 --> 01:12:45.333
In the fermented food group, we'll start there,

1296
01:12:45.333 --> 01:12:48.333
they saw across the board, aggregate

1297
01:12:49.516 --> 01:12:52.183
increases in microbial diversity

1298
01:12:53.502 --> 01:12:56.250
and really impressive increases.

1299
01:12:56.250 --> 01:12:58.333
So, a real strong finding

1300
01:13:00.906 --> 01:13:05.906
and a good reason to add more fermented foods to your diet.

1301
01:13:05.985 --> 01:13:08.588
They also measured markers of inflammation

1302
01:13:08.588 --> 01:13:10.485
and they measured around 200 odds.

1303
01:13:10.485 --> 01:13:13.961
So, a very comprehensive look at inflammatory markers.

1304
01:13:13.961 --> 01:13:17.264
And they saw that the fermented foods

1305
01:13:17.264 --> 01:13:22.097
drove down inflammatory proteins, 19 of them specifically.

1306
01:13:23.024 --> 01:13:25.616
So, the introduction of fermented foods

1307
01:13:25.616 --> 01:13:28.866
was leading to a better immune response

1308
01:13:30.055 --> 01:13:34.141
and decreasing inflammation in the body.

1309
01:13:34.141 --> 01:13:38.079
Now, the fiber group, very interesting,

1310
01:13:38.079 --> 01:13:41.187
there was an individualized response.

1311
01:13:41.187 --> 01:13:45.437
So, if you look at the aggregate, just the average,

1312
01:13:46.646 --> 01:13:49.595
the fiber group did not see an increase

1313
01:13:49.595 --> 01:13:51.595
in microbiome diversity.

1314
01:13:52.901 --> 01:13:55.881
And that's an interesting finding

1315
01:13:55.881 --> 01:13:59.567
and you kind of have to dig into the study

1316
01:13:59.567 --> 01:14:01.657
to make sense of that--

1317
01:14:01.657 --> 01:14:02.522
Why would that be?

1318
01:14:02.522 --> 01:14:06.702
I mean, that up ends prior conclusions around that?

1319
01:14:06.702 --> 01:14:10.119
It does and when you dig into the study

1320
01:14:12.156 --> 01:14:15.823
and I've spoken with Dr. Gardner about this,

1321
01:14:17.574 --> 01:14:20.043
there were some individuals

1322
01:14:20.043 --> 01:14:23.546
who handled the increase in fiber really well

1323
01:14:23.546 --> 01:14:26.463
and they saw an increase in diversity.

1324
01:14:26.463 --> 01:14:28.594
And then there were some who didn't

1325
01:14:28.594 --> 01:14:31.596
and actually saw an increase in inflammation.

1326
01:14:31.596 --> 01:14:34.504
And they thought this was really interesting

1327
01:14:34.504 --> 01:14:35.479
because this finding

1328
01:14:35.479 --> 01:14:39.312
is kind of in spite of previous understanding.

1329
01:14:40.420 --> 01:14:43.671
And what they found was when they went back

1330
01:14:43.671 --> 01:14:46.421
and looked at baseline diversity,

1331
01:14:47.295 --> 01:14:49.980
the people who didn't respond so great

1332
01:14:49.980 --> 01:14:51.746
to increasing fiber,

1333
01:14:51.746 --> 01:14:56.364
they essentially had very poor diversity to begin with.

1334
01:14:56.364 --> 01:15:01.031
And so there are a couple of hypotheses from this study,

1335
01:15:02.934 --> 01:15:06.229
it's not to write off fiber at all.

1336
01:15:06.229 --> 01:15:10.145
It's just to say that maybe in certain circumstances,

1337
01:15:10.145 --> 01:15:14.400
jacking your fiber up from 20 grams to 40 grams overnight

1338
01:15:14.400 --> 01:15:16.617
is not ideal for some people

1339
01:15:16.617 --> 01:15:19.071
and perhaps they need tools and strategies

1340
01:15:19.071 --> 01:15:21.799
to increase their diversity first

1341
01:15:21.799 --> 01:15:23.783
before they're increasing.

1342
01:15:23.783 --> 01:15:26.694
And so one of the hypotheses is that

1343
01:15:26.694 --> 01:15:29.435
if your microbiome is really struggling

1344
01:15:29.435 --> 01:15:30.917
and you don't have diversity,

1345
01:15:30.917 --> 01:15:32.068
perhaps you have a long history

1346
01:15:32.068 --> 01:15:34.875
of using antibiotics for example

1347
01:15:34.875 --> 01:15:37.562
and you've been eating a lot of ultra-processed foods

1348
01:15:37.562 --> 01:15:39.727
and living a very high-stress life...

1349
01:15:39.727 --> 01:15:42.324
The stress affects the microbiome as well,

1350
01:15:42.324 --> 01:15:46.373
then perhaps it could be a better strategy

1351
01:15:46.373 --> 01:15:51.373
to introduce fermented foods first, increase some diversity

1352
01:15:51.913 --> 01:15:54.972
and then start to add more plant diversity

1353
01:15:54.972 --> 01:15:57.242
and more fiber into the diet

1354
01:15:57.242 --> 01:15:59.691
when your gut is in a better position

1355
01:15:59.691 --> 01:16:01.535
to be able to handle it.

1356
01:16:01.535 --> 01:16:03.386
How long was the study conducted for?

1357
01:16:03.386 --> 01:16:05.969
So, this was a 10-week study.

1358
01:16:06.984 --> 01:16:09.660
It would be interesting to see how that would play out

1359
01:16:09.660 --> 01:16:10.493
over six months

1360
01:16:10.493 --> 01:16:13.769
perhaps if you just continue to eat fiber at some point,

1361
01:16:13.769 --> 01:16:15.704
your microbiome locks in on that,

1362
01:16:15.704 --> 01:16:19.019
but that idea of fermented foods

1363
01:16:19.019 --> 01:16:20.917
kind of turbocharging things

1364
01:16:20.917 --> 01:16:22.199
and getting you prepared

1365
01:16:22.199 --> 01:16:25.948
to be in a position to take advantage of additional fiber

1366
01:16:25.948 --> 01:16:28.155
in your diet I think is super interesting.

1367
01:16:28.155 --> 01:16:32.339
Yeah, so I think they gonna go and test that

1368
01:16:32.339 --> 01:16:37.339
and they may even look at a probiotic supplement I believe

1369
01:16:37.370 --> 01:16:40.404
as well, but the other thing to consider here

1370
01:16:40.404 --> 01:16:41.676
and your point is a great one

1371
01:16:41.676 --> 01:16:44.305
because duration of this study is important.

1372
01:16:44.305 --> 01:16:46.267
And that was one thing they commented on.

1373
01:16:46.267 --> 01:16:48.749
Perhaps over longer exposure,

1374
01:16:48.749 --> 01:16:51.549
the microbiome would have adapted

1375
01:16:51.549 --> 01:16:55.258
and being better able to handle that increase in fiber

1376
01:16:55.258 --> 01:16:58.487
and then you would see proliferation of the bacteria,

1377
01:16:58.487 --> 01:17:02.244
but the other thing I think that's important here is that

1378
01:17:02.244 --> 01:17:05.451
it was an overnight jump from 20 to 40 grams.

1379
01:17:05.451 --> 01:17:07.130
Would it have been different

1380
01:17:07.130 --> 01:17:09.963
if it was a very slow progression?

1381
01:17:11.427 --> 01:17:14.927
So, look, some of that remains to be seen,

1382
01:17:16.956 --> 01:17:18.951
but that's the beautiful thing about science.

1383
01:17:18.951 --> 01:17:21.535
It's constantly evolving.

1384
01:17:21.535 --> 01:17:26.084
We know that from the gut microbiome project,

1385
01:17:26.084 --> 01:17:30.417
which is led by a microbiome researcher, Rob Knight,

1386
01:17:32.293 --> 01:17:37.142
he has this huge project and has been creating this database

1387
01:17:37.142 --> 01:17:40.475
where people send in their stool samples

1388
01:17:43.253 --> 01:17:46.273
and also send in information about what they eat

1389
01:17:46.273 --> 01:17:50.124
and he's been able to create a really cool library.

1390
01:17:50.124 --> 01:17:51.536
And we know from that,

1391
01:17:51.536 --> 01:17:53.482
that compared to people who are eating ten

1392
01:17:53.482 --> 01:17:55.178
or fewer plants per week,

1393
01:17:55.178 --> 01:17:58.741
those that are eating 30 or more have much more diversity

1394
01:17:58.741 --> 01:18:00.535
in their microbiome, so--

1395
01:18:00.535 --> 01:18:01.972
That's Dr. B's whole thing.

1396
01:18:01.972 --> 01:18:03.710
Like if you're gonna do one thing,

1397
01:18:03.710 --> 01:18:08.200
increase the diversity of plants on your plate.

1398
01:18:08.200 --> 01:18:12.806
Yes, so I think that recommendation still stands.

1399
01:18:12.806 --> 01:18:15.902
I guess the only thing that I would add there is that

1400
01:18:15.902 --> 01:18:19.190
the process to increasing that diversity

1401
01:18:19.190 --> 01:18:20.916
it might look a little different

1402
01:18:20.916 --> 01:18:25.579
depending on what someone's baseline diversity looks like.

1403
01:18:25.579 --> 01:18:28.356
Sure and the clickbait title version of that

1404
01:18:28.356 --> 01:18:32.197
would be forget about fiber, it's all about fermented foods.

1405
01:18:32.197 --> 01:18:33.683
A simplified--

1406
01:18:33.683 --> 01:18:35.924
And that happened.

1407
01:18:35.924 --> 01:18:38.507
So, that happened and Hannah...

1408
01:18:40.181 --> 01:18:41.497
I can't remember Hannah's surname,

1409
01:18:41.497 --> 01:18:44.414
but this study was part of her PhD.

1410
01:18:45.442 --> 01:18:47.118
And I've heard her speak

1411
01:18:47.118 --> 01:18:50.122
and she kind of had a bit of a laugh about that

1412
01:18:50.122 --> 01:18:54.194
because that's not what the study shows,

1413
01:18:54.194 --> 01:18:57.011
it is a lot more nuanced than that.

1414
01:18:57.011 --> 01:18:59.261
As the world tends to be.

1415
01:19:00.529 --> 01:19:01.372
That's fascinating.

1416
01:19:01.372 --> 01:19:02.205
Is there a way...

1417
01:19:02.205 --> 01:19:03.215
I wanna switch gears in a minute,

1418
01:19:03.215 --> 01:19:06.568
but to kind of put a pin in this,

1419
01:19:06.568 --> 01:19:08.962
what's the best way for somebody to figure out

1420
01:19:08.962 --> 01:19:12.325
what the diversity of their microbiome looks like,

1421
01:19:12.325 --> 01:19:13.288
are there tests?

1422
01:19:13.288 --> 01:19:16.085
Can you like get a stool sample or something like that?

1423
01:19:16.085 --> 01:19:17.179
How does that work?

1424
01:19:17.179 --> 01:19:20.666
There are a couple of different companies

1425
01:19:20.666 --> 01:19:24.561
that are doing this, I'm not sure how reliable they are yet,

1426
01:19:24.561 --> 01:19:27.040
I'm told that they're getting more and more reliable

1427
01:19:27.040 --> 01:19:30.551
so actually this a great question

1428
01:19:30.551 --> 01:19:34.448
because I think this could be a capha

1429
01:19:34.448 --> 01:19:39.448
in helping people work out what steps they should take.

1430
01:19:39.523 --> 01:19:41.260
But in the meantime,

1431
01:19:41.260 --> 01:19:45.187
a good rule of thumb is increase both your fiber

1432
01:19:45.187 --> 01:19:47.635
and your fermented foods?

1433
01:19:47.635 --> 01:19:52.552
Yep and you may need to experiment a little bit with that

1434
01:19:54.181 --> 01:19:57.654
to work with your body and listen to your body,

1435
01:19:57.654 --> 01:19:59.653
but I think certainly the key finding

1436
01:19:59.653 --> 01:20:03.521
from that study is that fermented foods are beneficial.

1437
01:20:03.521 --> 01:20:07.771
And all of us should be eating more kimchi and sauerkraut

1438
01:20:07.771 --> 01:20:11.885
and if you eat animal products and you eat dairy,

1439
01:20:11.885 --> 01:20:16.800
then I would argue that fermented dairy is probably the best

1440
01:20:16.800 --> 01:20:18.550
of the dairy options.

1441
01:20:21.976 --> 01:20:25.082
And I do think that some of that technology

1442
01:20:25.082 --> 01:20:29.417
that you just spoke to, it will become better over time,

1443
01:20:29.417 --> 01:20:32.244
I think Zoey is one that's out there.

1444
01:20:32.244 --> 01:20:35.318
I'm not sure whether they report so much on diversity

1445
01:20:35.318 --> 01:20:38.134
in terms of a diversity score for the person,

1446
01:20:38.134 --> 01:20:40.918
but they do take a stool sample

1447
01:20:40.918 --> 01:20:43.555
and they do recommend foods.

1448
01:20:43.555 --> 01:20:46.472
And that's being led by Tim Spector

1449
01:20:47.419 --> 01:20:52.200
who is certainly a sort of prominent expert in this space.

1450
01:20:52.200 --> 01:20:53.033
Zoe.

1451
01:20:53.033 --> 01:20:53.866
Zoe.

1452
01:20:53.866 --> 01:20:54.699
I suppose it's called Zoe.

1453
01:20:54.699 --> 01:20:55.907
Yeah.

1454
01:20:55.907 --> 01:20:58.913
Final question on this point,

1455
01:20:58.913 --> 01:20:59.746
what is your sense

1456
01:20:59.746 --> 01:21:04.480
of the efficacy of store-bought kombucha products?

1457
01:21:04.480 --> 01:21:08.980
You see them advertised with a certain number attached

1458
01:21:11.405 --> 01:21:14.374
to what that culture looks like,

1459
01:21:14.374 --> 01:21:15.207
but then I think,

1460
01:21:15.207 --> 01:21:19.457
"Well, these things are pasteurized on some level,"

1461
01:21:20.549 --> 01:21:23.444
like I could make kombucha at home and we've done that,

1462
01:21:23.444 --> 01:21:25.235
I'm sure the ones we make at home

1463
01:21:25.235 --> 01:21:26.785
are gonna be more efficacious

1464
01:21:26.785 --> 01:21:28.638
than what I'm buying at the store,

1465
01:21:28.638 --> 01:21:30.189
but I've often just thought like,

1466
01:21:30.189 --> 01:21:33.262
this is probably not doing anything,

1467
01:21:33.262 --> 01:21:36.555
it's more of a placebo type situation,

1468
01:21:36.555 --> 01:21:37.896
but I don't know if you look at that

1469
01:21:37.896 --> 01:21:38.916
or are there studies on this?

1470
01:21:38.916 --> 01:21:41.672
I'm with you, I don't think there are studies on that,

1471
01:21:41.672 --> 01:21:45.589
but I choose the raw, non-pasteurized kombucha,

1472
01:21:46.639 --> 01:21:47.967
you can find a few of those.

1473
01:21:47.967 --> 01:21:51.308
There are some here that are found in California

1474
01:21:51.308 --> 01:21:52.440
for that reason,

1475
01:21:52.440 --> 01:21:54.536
I think if you're pasteurizing them and heating them,

1476
01:21:54.536 --> 01:21:55.680
then you are--

1477
01:21:55.680 --> 01:21:58.044
Defeats the whole entire purpose of the whole thing.

1478
01:21:58.044 --> 01:22:00.903
Yeah, those microbes are likely being killed.

1479
01:22:00.903 --> 01:22:05.736
Now, in saying that, to add a layer of complexity to this,

1480
01:22:08.748 --> 01:22:13.748
there is a lot of research now looking at dead microbes

1481
01:22:13.804 --> 01:22:16.256
and these are often called post biotics.

1482
01:22:16.256 --> 01:22:19.598
And there are some studies showing benefit

1483
01:22:19.598 --> 01:22:24.027
even through the consumption of dead probiotics.

1484
01:22:24.027 --> 01:22:26.434
So, that's interesting,

1485
01:22:26.434 --> 01:22:31.184
I don't think we fully understand is pasteurized kombucha

1486
01:22:32.328 --> 01:22:34.726
and perhaps the dead cultures that are in there

1487
01:22:34.726 --> 01:22:39.403
is that exerting benefit within our gut and downstream?

1488
01:22:39.403 --> 01:22:42.560
So, I think my advice would be to either try

1489
01:22:42.560 --> 01:22:46.400
and make your own or to look for one that is a raw,

1490
01:22:46.400 --> 01:22:48.155
non-pasteurized kombucha.

1491
01:22:48.155 --> 01:22:51.096
Get your scoby going at home.

1492
01:22:51.096 --> 01:22:54.354
Switching gears, I wanna talk about saturated fat.

1493
01:22:54.354 --> 01:22:56.412
When you articulated your thesis

1494
01:22:56.412 --> 01:22:57.934
at the beginning of the show,

1495
01:22:57.934 --> 01:23:01.429
a big piece in that was the reduction of saturated fat

1496
01:23:01.429 --> 01:23:05.512
and when we talk about saturated fat, this is a hot button,

1497
01:23:05.512 --> 01:23:08.795
hotly debated thing particularly on social media

1498
01:23:08.795 --> 01:23:12.872
depending upon your particular dietary tribe of choice.

1499
01:23:12.872 --> 01:23:16.622
The overwhelming evidence from my perspective

1500
01:23:18.165 --> 01:23:21.717
of valid, objective science is pretty clear

1501
01:23:21.717 --> 01:23:25.913
that saturated fat, no bueno, contributing to heart disease

1502
01:23:25.913 --> 01:23:29.722
and other not so good health outcomes.

1503
01:23:29.722 --> 01:23:32.607
So, walk us through your sense of the science

1504
01:23:32.607 --> 01:23:36.676
with respect to dietary saturated fat intake,

1505
01:23:36.676 --> 01:23:39.992
the impact that it has and why we should be reducing it.

1506
01:23:39.992 --> 01:23:41.490
How far back should we go?

1507
01:23:41.490 --> 01:23:42.467
I don't know.

1508
01:23:42.467 --> 01:23:44.701
We go as long as you want.

1509
01:23:44.701 --> 01:23:47.534
There's a great study from 1908.

1510
01:23:49.233 --> 01:23:50.671
That still holds up?

1511
01:23:50.671 --> 01:23:52.213
Yeah, well, it's interesting

1512
01:23:52.213 --> 01:23:55.213
because pre-1908, atherosclerosis...

1513
01:23:56.867 --> 01:23:57.700
A lot of what we're talking about

1514
01:23:57.700 --> 01:24:01.002
when we're talking about saturated fat is its effect

1515
01:24:01.002 --> 01:24:02.502
on LDL cholesterol

1516
01:24:03.523 --> 01:24:05.481
and then the effect that that has

1517
01:24:05.481 --> 01:24:08.101
on the accumulation of fat in your artery,

1518
01:24:08.101 --> 01:24:10.268
the development of plaque,

1519
01:24:11.407 --> 01:24:13.658
in science it's called atherosclerosis,

1520
01:24:13.658 --> 01:24:15.358
which then puts you at higher risk

1521
01:24:15.358 --> 01:24:18.572
of having heart attack or stroke.

1522
01:24:18.572 --> 01:24:20.739
And prior to 1908 however,

1523
01:24:22.616 --> 01:24:26.732
the prevailing sort of idea around cardiovascular disease

1524
01:24:26.732 --> 01:24:29.713
was that it was just a process of aging

1525
01:24:29.713 --> 01:24:34.671
and it wasn't necessarily driven through a dietary exposure.

1526
01:24:34.671 --> 01:24:38.338
And this early research goes back to Russia.

1527
01:24:39.470 --> 01:24:42.887
These Russian scientists were in the lab,

1528
01:24:44.046 --> 01:24:46.948
the first one's really doing work in this space

1529
01:24:46.948 --> 01:24:50.948
and there was a researcher, last name Ignatowski

1530
01:24:51.911 --> 01:24:55.744
and he fed meat and dairy products to a rabbit

1531
01:25:00.690 --> 01:25:05.690
and was able to see that that rabbit developed fatty streaks

1532
01:25:06.713 --> 01:25:08.487
in its arteries.

1533
01:25:08.487 --> 01:25:11.820
And that was really the very first study

1534
01:25:12.977 --> 01:25:13.953
that was like,

1535
01:25:13.953 --> 01:25:17.951
"Hang on, maybe the development of fatty streaks,

1536
01:25:17.951 --> 01:25:19.996
atherosclerosis that we're seeing in humans

1537
01:25:19.996 --> 01:25:21.579
is not just aging."

1538
01:25:22.654 --> 01:25:24.790
However, from that study,

1539
01:25:24.790 --> 01:25:28.672
the hypothesis was that it was animal protein.

1540
01:25:28.672 --> 01:25:33.589
And about four or five years later, 1913, Nikolai Anichkov,

1541
01:25:36.936 --> 01:25:40.157
quite a famous name in the sort of science world

1542
01:25:40.157 --> 01:25:43.926
for cardiovascular disease, another Russian,

1543
01:25:43.926 --> 01:25:47.117
he was looking at the fatty streaks in arteries

1544
01:25:47.117 --> 01:25:50.436
and could see that there was a lot of cholesterol in there

1545
01:25:50.436 --> 01:25:54.532
and so he had a hypothesis that maybe in that 1908 study,

1546
01:25:54.532 --> 01:25:57.505
maybe it was cholesterol that was in those foods

1547
01:25:57.505 --> 01:25:59.137
and it wasn't protein

1548
01:25:59.137 --> 01:26:02.970
that was causing this arterial plaque to form.

1549
01:26:04.742 --> 01:26:08.728
So, he ran a study again with a rabbit

1550
01:26:08.728 --> 01:26:12.311
and one group were fed a sunflower seed oil

1551
01:26:13.946 --> 01:26:18.797
and one were fed sunflower seed oil with cholesterol in it.

1552
01:26:18.797 --> 01:26:22.297
And he was able to see that quite clearly,

1553
01:26:24.234 --> 01:26:26.629
the rabbits that were fed the sunflower oil

1554
01:26:26.629 --> 01:26:28.863
with cholesterol dissolved into it

1555
01:26:28.863 --> 01:26:30.993
developed the fatty streaks

1556
01:26:30.993 --> 01:26:33.410
and the sort of the pathology

1557
01:26:37.308 --> 01:26:41.048
that goes on to become atherosclerosis.

1558
01:26:41.048 --> 01:26:45.215
Now, that research then was really groundbreaking.

1559
01:26:47.704 --> 01:26:51.249
But what happened was immediately after that,

1560
01:26:51.249 --> 01:26:52.931
some other researches

1561
01:26:52.931 --> 01:26:57.931
and he decided to look at replicating that study in rats

1562
01:26:57.970 --> 01:26:59.691
and in dogs.

1563
01:26:59.691 --> 01:27:03.947
So, again feeding dietary cholesterol to these animals

1564
01:27:03.947 --> 01:27:08.549
and looking to see if there was any buildup of this fat

1565
01:27:08.549 --> 01:27:10.364
in the arteries.

1566
01:27:10.364 --> 01:27:13.447
And what they found was there wasn't.

1567
01:27:14.398 --> 01:27:17.858
And so they thought this was a bit of an anomaly,

1568
01:27:17.858 --> 01:27:19.978
maybe it's just something that happens in a rabbit,

1569
01:27:19.978 --> 01:27:21.550
which is a herbivore

1570
01:27:21.550 --> 01:27:26.021
and there was no further science done for quite some time.

1571
01:27:26.021 --> 01:27:28.961
Now, the key thing that was overlooked there was that

1572
01:27:28.961 --> 01:27:32.336
in the rabbit model, the rabbit was fed something,

1573
01:27:32.336 --> 01:27:36.381
it increased the LDL cholesterol in that rabbit,

1574
01:27:36.381 --> 01:27:41.354
which then had led to the buildup of the fatty streak.

1575
01:27:41.354 --> 01:27:44.021
The rat and the dog, the models,

1576
01:27:46.265 --> 01:27:49.674
the reason there was no fatty buildup in the artery

1577
01:27:49.674 --> 01:27:53.396
was because they metabolize cholesterol differently

1578
01:27:53.396 --> 01:27:57.073
and so the consumption of dietary cholesterol

1579
01:27:57.073 --> 01:28:01.361
didn't increase their cholesterol levels in their blood.

1580
01:28:01.361 --> 01:28:04.444
And so having sort of overlooked that

1581
01:28:05.368 --> 01:28:09.826
and also the fact that all of this was published in Russian,

1582
01:28:09.826 --> 01:28:14.081
it was kind of parked by the Russians there

1583
01:28:14.081 --> 01:28:16.171
and wasn't seen by the rest of the world

1584
01:28:16.171 --> 01:28:18.588
and then 20 or 30 years later

1585
01:28:19.985 --> 01:28:24.402
is when some American researchers, Kin Sell and keys,

1586
01:28:27.475 --> 01:28:30.438
they were looking at metabolic ward studies

1587
01:28:30.438 --> 01:28:32.049
where they're bringing people in

1588
01:28:32.049 --> 01:28:35.940
and they wanted to see if they could identify

1589
01:28:35.940 --> 01:28:38.283
what components of the diet

1590
01:28:38.283 --> 01:28:41.885
would increase cholesterol levels in the blood

1591
01:28:41.885 --> 01:28:45.970
because the idea at that point in time was that

1592
01:28:45.970 --> 01:28:50.053
high serum cholesterol was driving heart disease.

1593
01:28:51.504 --> 01:28:54.712
And you've got to remember cardiovascular disease peaked

1594
01:28:54.712 --> 01:28:55.795
in the 1950s.

1595
01:28:58.130 --> 01:29:00.092
There was essentially a national inquiry

1596
01:29:00.092 --> 01:29:04.396
following the president of America, president Roosevelt,

1597
01:29:04.396 --> 01:29:07.146
he died of heart disease in 1944.

1598
01:29:08.056 --> 01:29:12.659
And so these guys were running these metabolic ward studies

1599
01:29:12.659 --> 01:29:16.826
and they were able to very clearly show in humans,

1600
01:29:17.977 --> 01:29:21.001
when you feed someone saturated fat,

1601
01:29:21.001 --> 01:29:26.001
you increase the levels of LDL cholesterol in their blood.

1602
01:29:26.478 --> 01:29:27.585
On the other hand,

1603
01:29:27.585 --> 01:29:30.397
when you feed someone polyunsaturated fats,

1604
01:29:30.397 --> 01:29:33.835
you will drive down their cholesterol.

1605
01:29:33.835 --> 01:29:35.473
In fact in their calculation,

1606
01:29:35.473 --> 01:29:38.788
what they were able to deduce was that

1607
01:29:38.788 --> 01:29:42.038
saturated fat will increase cholesterol

1608
01:29:45.595 --> 01:29:49.225
at a rate of around twice as much as polyunsaturated fat

1609
01:29:49.225 --> 01:29:50.392
will lower it.

1610
01:29:52.185 --> 01:29:54.058
They were also able to tease out

1611
01:29:54.058 --> 01:29:57.320
that dietary cholesterol in humans,

1612
01:29:57.320 --> 01:30:00.042
while it doesn't have as significant effect

1613
01:30:00.042 --> 01:30:05.042
as saturated fat, it still does affect cholesterol levels.

1614
01:30:05.167 --> 01:30:07.546
And they came up with a calculation

1615
01:30:07.546 --> 01:30:10.061
that still stands to this day.

1616
01:30:10.061 --> 01:30:12.129
It still stands to this day.

1617
01:30:12.129 --> 01:30:14.113
The more saturated fats in the diet

1618
01:30:14.113 --> 01:30:16.706
and the less polyunsaturated fats,

1619
01:30:16.706 --> 01:30:21.451
the higher someone's LDL cholesterol levels are.

1620
01:30:21.451 --> 01:30:25.491
And the incidence of atherosclerosis.

1621
01:30:25.491 --> 01:30:27.991
Yes, so what we know is that

1622
01:30:30.313 --> 01:30:32.615
the higher your LDL cholesterol

1623
01:30:32.615 --> 01:30:35.438
and the longer you're exposed to that over a lifetime,

1624
01:30:35.438 --> 01:30:39.249
the higher your risk is to develop atherosclerosis.

1625
01:30:39.249 --> 01:30:43.082
And there is an interesting study out of Spain

1626
01:30:44.679 --> 01:30:46.692
called the PISA study

1627
01:30:46.692 --> 01:30:51.025
and this study essentially took 4,000 healthy adults

1628
01:30:54.044 --> 01:30:58.693
and they used ultrasound to look at the plaque

1629
01:30:58.693 --> 01:31:00.462
in their arteries.

1630
01:31:00.462 --> 01:31:02.141
And these were healthy adults

1631
01:31:02.141 --> 01:31:04.615
without cardiovascular disease.

1632
01:31:04.615 --> 01:31:06.790
And the recommendations in this country

1633
01:31:06.790 --> 01:31:09.416
and around the world are LDL cholesterol

1634
01:31:09.416 --> 01:31:13.252
should be around 100 milligrams per deciliter.

1635
01:31:13.252 --> 01:31:18.252
And so they ultrasounded all of these different participants

1636
01:31:19.275 --> 01:31:22.729
who had varying LDL cholesterol levels.

1637
01:31:22.729 --> 01:31:24.411
And what they found was that

1638
01:31:24.411 --> 01:31:27.559
even at that 100 milligrams per deciliter level,

1639
01:31:27.559 --> 01:31:30.142
which we consider to be normal,

1640
01:31:31.195 --> 01:31:33.168
but we consider it to be normal

1641
01:31:33.168 --> 01:31:35.859
in quite an unhealthy population.

1642
01:31:35.859 --> 01:31:38.428
What they found was that at that level,

1643
01:31:38.428 --> 01:31:41.641
40% of people had atherosclerosis.

1644
01:31:41.641 --> 01:31:44.164
And it wasn't until you got down

1645
01:31:44.164 --> 01:31:48.562
to around 50, 60, 70 milligrams per deciliter,

1646
01:31:48.562 --> 01:31:52.828
did you see people without atherosclerosis.

1647
01:31:52.828 --> 01:31:54.841
So, a couple observations.

1648
01:31:54.841 --> 01:31:59.008
First, wasn't there a study done on young soldiers

1649
01:32:01.382 --> 01:32:03.987
who had died in World War I where they autopsy them

1650
01:32:03.987 --> 01:32:06.852
and they realized perhaps for the first time

1651
01:32:06.852 --> 01:32:10.219
that young people had these fatty streaks

1652
01:32:10.219 --> 01:32:13.345
in their cardiovascular system?

1653
01:32:13.345 --> 01:32:16.195
It was the Korean War I believe.

1654
01:32:16.195 --> 01:32:17.286
Oh, Korean War--

1655
01:32:17.286 --> 01:32:21.119
And around 70% of the American soldiers

1656
01:32:22.570 --> 01:32:24.799
that were killed from gunfire--

1657
01:32:24.799 --> 01:32:27.966
These are like 18 to 24 or whatever.

1658
01:32:29.164 --> 01:32:32.997
Around 70% of them had atherosclerosis.

1659
