01:34:09.033 --> 01:34:13.136
And then the second thing that I want to bring up,

1695
01:34:13.136 --> 01:34:14.968
you mentioned Ancel Keys.

1696
01:34:14.968 --> 01:34:17.518
I mean, this is a guy who is legendary

1697
01:34:17.518 --> 01:34:19.168
for his breakthrough science,

1698
01:34:19.168 --> 01:34:22.744
but also somebody who has a scarlet letter attached to him,

1699
01:34:22.744 --> 01:34:26.782
there's a whole narrative around this guy being a quack

1700
01:34:26.782 --> 01:34:28.596
and why we shouldn't pay any attention

1701
01:34:28.596 --> 01:34:31.222
to anything that he discovered

1702
01:34:31.222 --> 01:34:33.275
and this narrative is being propagated

1703
01:34:33.275 --> 01:34:36.816
by a different dietary drive like the sort of carnivore

1704
01:34:36.816 --> 01:34:39.512
and low-carb community of people.

1705
01:34:39.512 --> 01:34:41.815
So, walk us through truth and fiction

1706
01:34:41.815 --> 01:34:43.339
when it comes to Ancel Keys,

1707
01:34:43.339 --> 01:34:48.039
where he sits in the canon of legitimate, verifiable science

1708
01:34:48.039 --> 01:34:51.610
that's trustworthy versus this idea

1709
01:34:51.610 --> 01:34:55.860
that he should be dismissed for reasons X, Y and Z.

1710
01:34:59.760 --> 01:35:04.760
If you take a position that carbohydrates are to blame

1711
01:35:04.819 --> 01:35:08.986
for all disease and you blame the 1980 guidelines,

1712
01:35:10.528 --> 01:35:12.695
which are quite notorious,

1713
01:35:14.211 --> 01:35:16.388
everyone refers to them as the guidelines that came out

1714
01:35:16.388 --> 01:35:20.146
and told people to eat low fat and look what happened,

1715
01:35:20.146 --> 01:35:22.616
population got sicker and sicker.

1716
01:35:22.616 --> 01:35:25.480
If you take the position that--

1717
01:35:25.480 --> 01:35:28.182
Never mind that people didn't follow those guidelines,

1718
01:35:28.182 --> 01:35:29.015
but anyway--

1719
01:35:29.015 --> 01:35:33.908
And we should get into that, that's really important.

1720
01:35:33.908 --> 01:35:37.309
If you take that position, then you need a scapegoat.

1721
01:35:37.309 --> 01:35:40.788
You need someone to blame who's responsible for this.

1722
01:35:40.788 --> 01:35:44.205
And unfortunately, Ancel Keys is that guy

1723
01:35:45.456 --> 01:35:47.854
for a lot of people and they blame him

1724
01:35:47.854 --> 01:35:51.279
for the development of the low-fat guidelines

1725
01:35:51.279 --> 01:35:53.112
that came out in 1980.

1726
01:35:54.796 --> 01:35:58.912
Now, if you go back and look at Key's work,

1727
01:35:58.912 --> 01:36:03.745
I would say arguably he has made the greatest contribution

1728
01:36:06.630 --> 01:36:10.246
to nutrition science in the last 100 years.

1729
01:36:10.246 --> 01:36:13.544
This relationship between saturated fat and cholesterol

1730
01:36:13.544 --> 01:36:16.690
and atherosclerosis is the most solid relationship

1731
01:36:16.690 --> 01:36:18.198
that exists today.

1732
01:36:18.198 --> 01:36:19.244
If that's wrong,

1733
01:36:19.244 --> 01:36:22.505
then we need to throw out all of nutrition science.

1734
01:36:22.505 --> 01:36:25.755
And people think that he just performed

1735
01:36:27.180 --> 01:36:29.196
this Seven Countries Study.

1736
01:36:29.196 --> 01:36:31.334
But as I just mentioned then,

1737
01:36:31.334 --> 01:36:34.762
he started before that in the metabolic wards.

1738
01:36:34.762 --> 01:36:37.149
He started in metabolic ward trials,

1739
01:36:37.149 --> 01:36:40.566
very controlled settings, bringing people in,

1740
01:36:40.566 --> 01:36:43.583
feeding them different fats, exposing them to animal fats,

1741
01:36:43.583 --> 01:36:44.700
to vegetable oils

1742
01:36:44.700 --> 01:36:48.804
and looking what happens to their cholesterol.

1743
01:36:48.804 --> 01:36:52.721
From there, he was able to create a hypothesis.

1744
01:36:54.492 --> 01:36:57.421
He could see that the saturated fats, the animal fats

1745
01:36:57.421 --> 01:36:59.466
were driving up cholesterol.

1746
01:36:59.466 --> 01:37:02.277
Now he wanted to go out in the real world

1747
01:37:02.277 --> 01:37:05.457
and look at populations that exist

1748
01:37:05.457 --> 01:37:08.942
and compare those populations eating lots of animal fats

1749
01:37:08.942 --> 01:37:11.173
with high cholesterol,

1750
01:37:11.173 --> 01:37:15.340
what's their cardiovascular disease incidents like

1751
01:37:16.335 --> 01:37:19.787
compared to other populations eating less animal fats

1752
01:37:19.787 --> 01:37:21.831
with lower cholesterol?

1753
01:37:21.831 --> 01:37:25.528
And prior to the Seven Countries Study

1754
01:37:25.528 --> 01:37:27.861
is what he's most known for.

1755
01:37:29.473 --> 01:37:33.640
And prior to that, he did a speech at a conference

1756
01:37:37.572 --> 01:37:41.739
and on a chart, he put up the saturated fat intake

1757
01:37:43.554 --> 01:37:48.303
and level of cardiovascular disease from six countries.

1758
01:37:48.303 --> 01:37:51.470
And people today look at that and say,

1759
01:37:53.389 --> 01:37:56.130
"Well, he cherry-picked those six countries,

1760
01:37:56.130 --> 01:37:58.047
there was actually 22."

1761
01:37:59.047 --> 01:38:02.994
Now, in Keys' defense, he chose those six countries

1762
01:38:02.994 --> 01:38:05.827
because that's where the best data was.

1763
01:38:05.827 --> 01:38:09.224
He wasn't happy with the data from the other countries.

1764
01:38:09.224 --> 01:38:12.474
You remember this is back in the 1950s.

1765
01:38:14.169 --> 01:38:17.086
So, some of the population data wasn't that great.

1766
01:38:17.086 --> 01:38:21.753
Now, the main point here is that some folks at that time

1767
01:38:23.957 --> 01:38:28.491
who were arguing with Ancel Keys said that in actual fact

1768
01:38:28.491 --> 01:38:30.589
there's 22 countries.

1769
01:38:30.589 --> 01:38:33.292
And they produced a graph which is very famous now,

1770
01:38:33.292 --> 01:38:36.177
you'll see the six versus the 22.

1771
01:38:36.177 --> 01:38:39.696
And the 22 didn't actually refute

1772
01:38:39.696 --> 01:38:41.028
what Ancel Keys was saying.

1773
01:38:41.028 --> 01:38:44.887
It just made the association a little weaker, now--

1774
01:38:44.887 --> 01:38:47.383
And the reason that he did not focus

1775
01:38:47.383 --> 01:38:52.161
on those other countries was that the data was error-ridden

1776
01:38:52.161 --> 01:38:55.743
or incomplete because of reporting inconsistencies

1777
01:38:55.743 --> 01:39:00.504
or just issues related to creating verifiable data sets.

1778
01:39:00.504 --> 01:39:03.186
Yeah, how verifiable it is,

1779
01:39:03.186 --> 01:39:06.893
there are problems with diagnosis for example in countries

1780
01:39:06.893 --> 01:39:10.310
and how they were categorizing the deaths

1781
01:39:11.888 --> 01:39:13.471
for certain people.

1782
01:39:14.646 --> 01:39:16.981
But I think what's really important is that

1783
01:39:16.981 --> 01:39:20.932
when he went to set up the Seven Countries Study,

1784
01:39:20.932 --> 01:39:25.200
he invited many, many countries and this is overlooked.

1785
01:39:25.200 --> 01:39:27.457
He's written about this.

1786
01:39:27.457 --> 01:39:31.423
He wanted to work with established nutrition scientists

1787
01:39:31.423 --> 01:39:32.738
in these countries

1788
01:39:32.738 --> 01:39:36.320
and essentially, he even invited France

1789
01:39:36.320 --> 01:39:39.242
which often gets brought up and they declined.

1790
01:39:39.242 --> 01:39:44.190
So, he was working with those that accepted the invitation

1791
01:39:44.190 --> 01:39:48.194
and had established nutrition science centers

1792
01:39:48.194 --> 01:39:49.946
who could conduct the research with him,

1793
01:39:49.946 --> 01:39:51.915
it was a collaboration.

1794
01:39:51.915 --> 01:39:55.248
And so there were seven countries chosen

1795
01:39:56.911 --> 01:40:00.078
and within that there were 16 cohorts.

1796
01:40:01.219 --> 01:40:05.708
These countries were the USA, there was Finland,

1797
01:40:05.708 --> 01:40:08.958
there was Greece, there was Yugoslavia,

1798
01:40:10.431 --> 01:40:14.348
which today has been separated, there was Japan

1799
01:40:16.652 --> 01:40:20.317
and there was the Netherlands, that's the seven countries

1800
01:40:20.317 --> 01:40:21.725
and then within those,

1801
01:40:21.725 --> 01:40:24.458
some of those countries had multiple cohorts,

1802
01:40:24.458 --> 01:40:27.426
there ended up being 16 different cohorts.

1803
01:40:27.426 --> 01:40:32.356
And the really nice thing about this study was that

1804
01:40:32.356 --> 01:40:37.356
there was great differences in the amount of saturated fat

1805
01:40:38.002 --> 01:40:39.317
between these cohorts.

1806
01:40:39.317 --> 01:40:41.060
You need that contrast.

1807
01:40:41.060 --> 01:40:43.037
If you're just looking at lots of populations

1808
01:40:43.037 --> 01:40:45.686
and their saturated fat consumption is very similar,

1809
01:40:45.686 --> 01:40:49.103
then you're not providing enough contrast

1810
01:40:49.952 --> 01:40:52.556
to see whether high versus low saturated fat consumption

1811
01:40:52.556 --> 01:40:54.806
is affecting heart disease.

1812
01:40:56.039 --> 01:40:58.539
So, he conducted this research

1813
01:40:59.658 --> 01:41:04.390
and it was able to establish relatively quickly

1814
01:41:04.390 --> 01:41:07.109
that countries like Greece and Japan

1815
01:41:07.109 --> 01:41:10.020
who had low saturated fat intake,

1816
01:41:10.020 --> 01:41:12.473
around 6 to 8% of calories

1817
01:41:12.473 --> 01:41:15.273
had significantly lower incidence of heart disease

1818
01:41:15.273 --> 01:41:17.712
compared to countries like the Netherlands

1819
01:41:17.712 --> 01:41:21.926
and the USA and Finland who had saturated fat intakes

1820
01:41:21.926 --> 01:41:24.759
around 16 to 20% of calories.

1821
01:41:26.147 --> 01:41:31.064
Wow, so what we glean from that is a pretty clear picture

1822
01:41:33.239 --> 01:41:36.726
of the relationship between saturated fat intake

1823
01:41:36.726 --> 01:41:41.143
and heart disease incidents that holds up to this day

1824
01:41:42.042 --> 01:41:47.042
and the research around this relationship has only continued

1825
01:41:47.764 --> 01:41:50.882
to kind of build on that premise.

1826
01:41:50.882 --> 01:41:53.965
Yeah, like that was just the start.

1827
01:41:57.328 --> 01:42:00.540
There's been extensive research since then.

1828
01:42:00.540 --> 01:42:03.790
And I should add that another criticism

1829
01:42:05.868 --> 01:42:09.954
that Keys often gets is that he was not paying attention

1830
01:42:09.954 --> 01:42:12.360
to refined carbohydrates.

1831
01:42:12.360 --> 01:42:14.027
And that's not true.

1832
01:42:15.100 --> 01:42:17.721
His own study show that there is a relationship

1833
01:42:17.721 --> 01:42:21.248
between refined carbohydrates and cardiovascular disease,

1834
01:42:21.248 --> 01:42:23.097
but that association is weaker.

1835
01:42:23.097 --> 01:42:26.742
It is weaker than saturated fat and cardiovascular disease.

1836
01:42:26.742 --> 01:42:28.325
And in fact in 1968

1837
01:42:29.553 --> 01:42:32.332
when Keys was providing recommendations

1838
01:42:32.332 --> 01:42:35.893
for Scandinavian dietary guidelines,

1839
01:42:35.893 --> 01:42:40.886
limiting refined sugars was one of his recommendations.

1840
01:42:40.886 --> 01:42:43.316
And he wasn't just this guy that was obsessed

1841
01:42:43.316 --> 01:42:45.369
just with saturated fat.

1842
01:42:45.369 --> 01:42:48.066
That was very much the start of his work

1843
01:42:48.066 --> 01:42:50.733
and over the course of his work,

1844
01:42:52.906 --> 01:42:55.114
he's most well known for being an advocate

1845
01:42:55.114 --> 01:42:57.539
of the Mediterranean dietary pattern.

1846
01:42:57.539 --> 01:42:59.465
And he's the one that went on

1847
01:42:59.465 --> 01:43:03.300
to describe that dietary pattern as I referred to earlier

1848
01:43:03.300 --> 01:43:06.941
as sort of largely vegetarian diet

1849
01:43:06.941 --> 01:43:10.108
with modest amounts of fish and dairy.

1850
01:43:11.098 --> 01:43:14.781
So, when we see the carnivore diet proponents out there,

1851
01:43:14.781 --> 01:43:17.802
the Paul Saladinos and the Shawn Bakers

1852
01:43:17.802 --> 01:43:19.592
advocating for this notion

1853
01:43:19.592 --> 01:43:21.127
that everything you ever thought you knew

1854
01:43:21.127 --> 01:43:25.485
about saturated fat is wrong, saturated fat is your friend,

1855
01:43:25.485 --> 01:43:27.497
what are they hanging their hat on

1856
01:43:27.497 --> 01:43:31.664
in terms of establishing that this is not harmful?

1857
01:43:33.664 --> 01:43:35.268
Compared to what Rich?

1858
01:43:35.268 --> 01:43:36.664
I don't know, you tell me.

1859
01:43:36.664 --> 01:43:39.518
That's the million-dollar question here.

1860
01:43:39.518 --> 01:43:42.698
There are a bunch of meta analyses

1861
01:43:42.698 --> 01:43:45.972
that seem to make saturated fat look okay.

1862
01:43:45.972 --> 01:43:49.797
And you can do that depending on what you compare it to.

1863
01:43:49.797 --> 01:43:53.918
And I said before there's been mountains of evidence

1864
01:43:53.918 --> 01:43:56.619
that have come out since the Seven Countries Study

1865
01:43:56.619 --> 01:44:00.877
and part of that has been teasing out exactly this.

1866
01:44:00.877 --> 01:44:04.774
If you swap calories from saturated fat

1867
01:44:04.774 --> 01:44:08.141
for calories in refined carbohydrates,

1868
01:44:08.141 --> 01:44:10.688
then that's a lateral move.

1869
01:44:10.688 --> 01:44:13.306
It could even increase risk of cardiovascular disease.

1870
01:44:13.306 --> 01:44:16.724
Yeah and you're kind of alluding to...

1871
01:44:16.724 --> 01:44:20.557
There was a study that garnered a lot of press

1872
01:44:21.723 --> 01:44:23.834
that said saturated fat isn't related to heart disease--

1873
01:44:23.834 --> 01:44:25.025
The PURE study.

1874
01:44:25.025 --> 01:44:25.866
It was really...

1875
01:44:25.866 --> 01:44:26.900
Yeah, exactly.

1876
01:44:26.900 --> 01:44:29.099
But what it really said was

1877
01:44:29.099 --> 01:44:32.167
maybe it's not quite as bad as we originally thought,

1878
01:44:32.167 --> 01:44:33.750
but it's still bad.

1879
01:44:35.120 --> 01:44:37.563
How do you interpret that?

1880
01:44:37.563 --> 01:44:42.421
The way I interpret the PURE study in particular

1881
01:44:42.421 --> 01:44:43.448
is that...

1882
01:44:43.448 --> 01:44:46.036
This was a multi-country study

1883
01:44:46.036 --> 01:44:49.119
and there are always some limitations

1884
01:44:50.293 --> 01:44:53.816
when you're comparing different socio-economic classes

1885
01:44:53.816 --> 01:44:55.316
and in that study,

1886
01:44:56.430 --> 01:44:58.555
the people that were eating less saturated fat

1887
01:44:58.555 --> 01:45:01.870
were tending to eat diets below the poverty line.

1888
01:45:01.870 --> 01:45:03.969
And they were diets

1889
01:45:03.969 --> 01:45:06.326
that contained a lot of refined carbohydrates,

1890
01:45:06.326 --> 01:45:09.885
very little diversity and sure, in a diet like that,

1891
01:45:09.885 --> 01:45:13.351
the addition of some animal fats

1892
01:45:13.351 --> 01:45:15.144
may improve their health.

1893
01:45:15.144 --> 01:45:17.100
I wouldn't argue against that.

1894
01:45:17.100 --> 01:45:20.562
But consistently if you look at what happens

1895
01:45:20.562 --> 01:45:23.263
if you swap calories from saturated fat

1896
01:45:23.263 --> 01:45:26.829
for polyunsaturated fats like vegetable oils

1897
01:45:26.829 --> 01:45:29.986
or nuts and seeds or even fatty fish

1898
01:45:29.986 --> 01:45:33.905
or for monounsaturated fats or for whole grains,

1899
01:45:33.905 --> 01:45:36.943
you see that you drive down heart disease risk.

1900
01:45:36.943 --> 01:45:41.113
So, I think the key takeaway point here is

1901
01:45:41.113 --> 01:45:43.506
you can make saturated fat look good,

1902
01:45:43.506 --> 01:45:45.853
it depends what you're comparing it with

1903
01:45:45.853 --> 01:45:49.407
and whether removing saturated fat from your diet

1904
01:45:49.407 --> 01:45:52.811
is healthy or not depends what you're replacing it with.

1905
01:45:52.811 --> 01:45:57.532
If you are replacing it with nuts, seeds, fatty fish

1906
01:45:57.532 --> 01:46:00.534
if you eat seafood or whole grains,

1907
01:46:00.534 --> 01:46:02.484
then you are going to be lowering your risk

1908
01:46:02.484 --> 01:46:03.823
of heart disease.

1909
01:46:03.823 --> 01:46:05.858
Another talking point that gets thrown around

1910
01:46:05.858 --> 01:46:07.008
from those communities,

1911
01:46:07.008 --> 01:46:09.188
I feel like they've pivoted a little bit away

1912
01:46:09.188 --> 01:46:11.192
from the saturated fat thing

1913
01:46:11.192 --> 01:46:14.849
and now it's all about vilifying seed oils.

1914
01:46:14.849 --> 01:46:18.133
Like that's become like the evil culprit in this whole thing

1915
01:46:18.133 --> 01:46:19.730
in a very reductionist way.

1916
01:46:19.730 --> 01:46:22.627
So, walk me through that argument

1917
01:46:22.627 --> 01:46:25.283
and kind of where things stand

1918
01:46:25.283 --> 01:46:27.542
in terms of not just seed oils,

1919
01:46:27.542 --> 01:46:30.521
but also the whole conversation

1920
01:46:30.521 --> 01:46:34.733
around the health implications of oil in the diet,

1921
01:46:34.733 --> 01:46:39.733
whether we're talking about Esselstyn's super no to low oil,

1922
01:46:41.022 --> 01:46:42.929
plant-based diet

1923
01:46:42.929 --> 01:46:47.762
to people who are very kind of pro-olive oil and all that.

1924
01:46:48.618 --> 01:46:50.955
I mean, that's a large, big question--

1925
01:46:50.955 --> 01:46:51.788
Maybe we start

1926
01:46:51.788 --> 01:46:56.288
with the sort of omega-6-rich vegetable oil, seed oils

1927
01:46:57.735 --> 01:47:01.143
and then we'll go into olive oil and the vegan diet,

1928
01:47:01.143 --> 01:47:03.060
the low-fat vegan diet.

1929
01:47:06.816 --> 01:47:11.355
That rhetoric around omega-6 oils being to blame,

1930
01:47:11.355 --> 01:47:14.438
seed oils being to blame for disease,

1931
01:47:15.395 --> 01:47:17.783
it's something I've studied super closely

1932
01:47:17.783 --> 01:47:21.004
because it really intrigues me

1933
01:47:21.004 --> 01:47:23.040
because I find it incredibly hard

1934
01:47:23.040 --> 01:47:25.271
to see how someone can take that position

1935
01:47:25.271 --> 01:47:29.028
from an evidence-based point of view.

1936
01:47:29.028 --> 01:47:31.481
But I think this is a prime example where emotions

1937
01:47:31.481 --> 01:47:34.165
and facts don't mix very well.

1938
01:47:34.165 --> 01:47:37.991
And when you're really affected by emotions,

1939
01:47:37.991 --> 01:47:40.791
it can affect objectivity.

1940
01:47:40.791 --> 01:47:44.874
And so I said before the 1980 guidelines came out

1941
01:47:46.059 --> 01:47:49.257
and a lot of people will say that those guidelines

1942
01:47:49.257 --> 01:47:51.634
pushed people off of animal fats

1943
01:47:51.634 --> 01:47:55.674
and pushed them towards seed oils and vegetable oils.

1944
01:47:55.674 --> 01:47:57.765
Right, being a primary ingredient

1945
01:47:57.765 --> 01:47:58.972
in ultra-processed foods.

1946
01:47:58.972 --> 01:48:02.101
Sure and I would be the first person to put my hand up

1947
01:48:02.101 --> 01:48:06.441
and say that seed oils within ultra-processed foods,

1948
01:48:06.441 --> 01:48:07.557
I don't think anyone should be eating

1949
01:48:07.557 --> 01:48:09.215
more ultra-processed foods.

1950
01:48:09.215 --> 01:48:10.803
But we need to remember there is much more

1951
01:48:10.803 --> 01:48:15.174
than just seed oils in those ultra-processed foods,

1952
01:48:15.174 --> 01:48:17.794
there's added sugar and there's added salt

1953
01:48:17.794 --> 01:48:21.374
and a whole lot of other additives.

1954
01:48:21.374 --> 01:48:22.408
Refined grains too.

1955
01:48:22.408 --> 01:48:23.557
That's right.

1956
01:48:23.557 --> 01:48:27.557
So, where does this idea that omega-6s seed oils

1957
01:48:30.726 --> 01:48:34.395
are inflammatory or causing disease come from?

1958
01:48:34.395 --> 01:48:36.750
Let's go back to the evidence hierarchy.

1959
01:48:36.750 --> 01:48:41.083
So, I think firstly from an anecdotal point of view,

1960
01:48:43.957 --> 01:48:47.957
a lot of people who believe that saito is a bad,

1961
01:48:48.996 --> 01:48:51.541
they've removed ultra-processed foods from their diet

1962
01:48:51.541 --> 01:48:52.685
and they feel better.

1963
01:48:52.685 --> 01:48:56.768
So, I understand that, that's a lived experience,

1964
01:48:57.675 --> 01:49:02.008
but I think they're wrongly attributing that benefit

1965
01:49:02.993 --> 01:49:04.373
to the reduction of seed oils

1966
01:49:04.373 --> 01:49:07.518
when in fact it's just the removal of ultra-processed foods

1967
01:49:07.518 --> 01:49:08.851
from their diet.

1968
01:49:09.859 --> 01:49:13.107
If you look at the strongest kind of arguments

1969
01:49:13.107 --> 01:49:15.279
for omega-6s being inflammatory,

1970
01:49:15.279 --> 01:49:19.029
all of this is in vitro animal study science.

1971
01:49:20.274 --> 01:49:22.010
And as we mentioned earlier,

1972
01:49:22.010 --> 01:49:24.250
not everything that happens at that level

1973
01:49:24.250 --> 01:49:25.917
plays out in humans.

1974
01:49:27.440 --> 01:49:30.455
And we know from significant amounts

1975
01:49:30.455 --> 01:49:32.680
of randomized controlled trials,

1976
01:49:32.680 --> 01:49:34.295
randomized controlled trials,

1977
01:49:34.295 --> 01:49:38.899
if people substitute saturated fat for vegetable oils

1978
01:49:38.899 --> 01:49:42.982
or seed oils, they reduce their risk of heart disease.

1979
01:49:42.982 --> 01:49:44.387
That's the first thing.

1980
01:49:44.387 --> 01:49:48.554
The second is that omega-6s when you consume them,

1981
01:49:51.528 --> 01:49:55.080
the body desaturates and elongates them

1982
01:49:55.080 --> 01:49:58.175
into a biologically active form,

1983
01:49:58.175 --> 01:49:59.801
a bit like what happens to omega-3s,

1984
01:49:59.801 --> 01:50:03.599
people have probably heard that plant-based omega-3s

1985
01:50:03.599 --> 01:50:04.820
go through a similar pathway

1986
01:50:04.820 --> 01:50:07.292
where they are converted to DHA and EPA.

1987
01:50:07.292 --> 01:50:09.782
Same thing happens with omega-6s.

1988
01:50:09.782 --> 01:50:13.782
And along this pathway when you consume omega-6s

1989
01:50:15.787 --> 01:50:17.196
which is linoleic acid,

1990
01:50:17.196 --> 01:50:21.696
this is a fat that is quite concentrated in seed oils,

1991
01:50:22.957 --> 01:50:24.540
along that pathway,

1992
01:50:26.007 --> 01:50:30.000
a fat called arachidonic acid is produced.

1993
01:50:30.000 --> 01:50:32.855
And the idea from a mechanism point of view is that

1994
01:50:32.855 --> 01:50:36.688
this is a precursor to inflammatory compounds.

1995
01:50:38.013 --> 01:50:42.412
But when we look at human intervention studies,

1996
01:50:42.412 --> 01:50:44.829
if you feed people seed oils,

1997
01:50:45.798 --> 01:50:48.350
you don't get an increase in arachidonic acid,

1998
01:50:48.350 --> 01:50:49.767
you don't see it.

1999
01:50:50.790 --> 01:50:55.778
Secondly, if you feed people omega-6 linoleic acid,

2000
01:50:55.778 --> 01:50:59.414
you do not get an increase in inflammatory biomarkers.

2001
01:50:59.414 --> 01:51:01.146
That's probably the most important thing

2002
01:51:01.146 --> 01:51:02.727
for us to think about here.

2003
01:51:02.727 --> 01:51:04.118
It just doesn't happen.

2004
01:51:04.118 --> 01:51:06.204
Despite the putative mechanism

2005
01:51:06.204 --> 01:51:09.338
and what you see in in vitro or animal studies,

2006
01:51:09.338 --> 01:51:12.891
it doesn't play out in human studies.

2007
01:51:12.891 --> 01:51:15.254
And then if you zoom right out

2008
01:51:15.254 --> 01:51:18.868
and you look at large population studies

2009
01:51:18.868 --> 01:51:22.616
and you look at people consuming more linoleic acid

2010
01:51:22.616 --> 01:51:24.755
or who have higher amounts of linoleic acid

2011
01:51:24.755 --> 01:51:26.277
in their tissues,

2012
01:51:26.277 --> 01:51:29.019
they have lower risk of cardiovascular disease

2013
01:51:29.019 --> 01:51:31.591
and lower risk of total mortality.

2014
01:51:31.591 --> 01:51:34.104
But would that not be to play devil's advocate,

2015
01:51:34.104 --> 01:51:37.418
another example of compared to what?

2016
01:51:37.418 --> 01:51:39.296
Is not the conventional wisdom still?

2017
01:51:39.296 --> 01:51:42.279
Like what I've heard for years is

2018
01:51:42.279 --> 01:51:45.071
most people are consuming way too much omega-6

2019
01:51:45.071 --> 01:51:46.422
and it's all about the ratio

2020
01:51:46.422 --> 01:51:50.222
or proportion of omega-6 intake to Omega-3

2021
01:51:50.222 --> 01:51:53.841
and we're being told to increase our omega-3s

2022
01:51:53.841 --> 01:51:55.786
and reduce our omega-6

2023
01:51:55.786 --> 01:51:59.164
to bring that into kind of proper relationship.

2024
01:51:59.164 --> 01:52:03.164
Is that still the scientific sort of perspective

2025
01:52:04.500 --> 01:52:08.402
or is this new studies that have changed that?

2026
01:52:08.402 --> 01:52:11.985
I don't think that is the position today.

2027
01:52:13.033 --> 01:52:16.616
I think that there is overwhelming evidence

2028
01:52:17.491 --> 01:52:22.491
to show that that ratio where I do think it is important

2029
01:52:22.613 --> 01:52:24.863
is that the omega-6 pathway

2030
01:52:25.768 --> 01:52:28.797
and the omega-3 pathway share enzymes.

2031
01:52:28.797 --> 01:52:30.853
And so there is a possibility

2032
01:52:30.853 --> 01:52:34.069
if you have a lot of omega-6s in your diet,

2033
01:52:34.069 --> 01:52:36.612
then you're using up all those enzymes

2034
01:52:36.612 --> 01:52:40.586
and you may struggle to convert the Omega-3s from ALA

2035
01:52:40.586 --> 01:52:41.836
to DHA and EPA.

2036
01:52:42.764 --> 01:52:47.571
So, I don't think that's been fully borne out in the science

2037
01:52:47.571 --> 01:52:51.530
other than to say I think there is some evidence

2038
01:52:51.530 --> 01:52:54.275
particularly people with chronic inflammation

2039
01:52:54.275 --> 01:52:56.113
like rheumatoid arthritis

2040
01:52:56.113 --> 01:53:00.556
that does seem to be a benefit for them to lower omega-6s

2041
01:53:00.556 --> 01:53:01.622
in their diet.

2042
01:53:01.622 --> 01:53:06.039
But overall, if you look at human health outcome data

2043
01:53:08.463 --> 01:53:12.046
when you're feeding healthy adults omega-6s

2044
01:53:13.272 --> 01:53:16.839
in quite large volumes, you're not dialing up inflammation

2045
01:53:16.839 --> 01:53:18.578
and when we look at a population level,

2046
01:53:18.578 --> 01:53:21.920
people that are consuming a lot of these omega-6s

2047
01:53:21.920 --> 01:53:24.896
as I said they do have lower risk of cardiovascular disease,

2048
01:53:24.896 --> 01:53:27.267
lower risk of premature death.

2049
01:53:27.267 --> 01:53:32.116
Is that provided those omega-6s are not pre-packaged

2050
01:53:32.116 --> 01:53:34.082
in an ultra-processed food

2051
01:53:34.082 --> 01:53:37.894
where there's all these other factors to consider?

2052
01:53:37.894 --> 01:53:39.308
Yeah, I think so.

2053
01:53:39.308 --> 01:53:42.641
And that's kind of where I land on this.

2054
01:53:43.916 --> 01:53:47.574
I mean, firstly, I'm not sort of pro-seed

2055
01:53:47.574 --> 01:53:50.576
or pro-vegetable oils, you don't have to weigh them--

2056
01:53:50.576 --> 01:53:52.616
Let's make that clear because it does sound like,

2057
01:53:52.616 --> 01:53:55.187
"Hey, like no problem with omega-6s,

2058
01:53:55.187 --> 01:53:56.765
knock yourself out with seed oil."

2059
01:53:56.765 --> 01:54:01.752
What I'm saying is I just don't think they're the poison

2060
01:54:01.752 --> 01:54:04.362
that they're made out to be.

2061
01:54:04.362 --> 01:54:05.456
I certainly don't think

2062
01:54:05.456 --> 01:54:06.871
that we should be increasing our exposure

2063
01:54:06.871 --> 01:54:09.199
to ultra-processed foods,

2064
01:54:09.199 --> 01:54:11.582
but I don't think we need to generate the fear

2065
01:54:11.582 --> 01:54:12.901
over vegetable oils

2066
01:54:12.901 --> 01:54:17.401
and certainly don't need to blame the increased burden

2067
01:54:18.762 --> 01:54:21.991
of chronic disease since 1980.

2068
01:54:21.991 --> 01:54:24.943
That's not from people swapping butter with a vegetable oil

2069
01:54:24.943 --> 01:54:26.360
in their cooking.

2070
01:54:29.136 --> 01:54:31.280
It's a lot more complex than that,

2071
01:54:31.280 --> 01:54:35.929
since 1980 the consumption of these ultra-processed foods

2072
01:54:35.929 --> 01:54:37.596
has increased hugely

2073
01:54:41.066 --> 01:54:45.816
and as I said, I'm not a huge sort of pro-seed oil person

2074
01:54:47.056 --> 01:54:50.986
and particularly someone's watching their weight,

2075
01:54:50.986 --> 01:54:54.195
I think all oils are very calorie-dense

2076
01:54:54.195 --> 01:54:57.028
and we need to be mindful of that,

2077
01:54:58.381 --> 01:55:01.456
they're bringing about 120 calories per tablespoon,

2078
01:55:01.456 --> 01:55:05.184
so they're quite easy to over consume.

2079
01:55:05.184 --> 01:55:07.907
You mentioned olive oil and the vegan diet.

2080
01:55:07.907 --> 01:55:12.827
Yeah, I mean, within the plant-based vegan world,

2081
01:55:12.827 --> 01:55:15.910
there are sub communities within that

2082
01:55:18.552 --> 01:55:20.004
that are at odds with each other

2083
01:55:20.004 --> 01:55:22.669
over the health implications

2084
01:55:22.669 --> 01:55:24.829
of including something like olive oil in the diet.

2085
01:55:24.829 --> 01:55:29.327
On the one hand, you have Caldwell Esselstyn and his camp

2086
01:55:29.327 --> 01:55:31.775
that is very anti-oil.

2087
01:55:31.775 --> 01:55:33.299
That's a contingent of people

2088
01:55:33.299 --> 01:55:36.549
who are primarily focused on preventing

2089
01:55:37.998 --> 01:55:39.350
and reversing heart disease

2090
01:55:39.350 --> 01:55:41.503
and Caldwell is somebody who's treating people

2091
01:55:41.503 --> 01:55:44.336
for the most part who are very ill

2092
01:55:45.437 --> 01:55:47.526
and he's very adamant in that perspective

2093
01:55:47.526 --> 01:55:50.859
and has done his studies and written books about this

2094
01:55:50.859 --> 01:55:52.117
and then on the other end,

2095
01:55:52.117 --> 01:55:55.534
you have a different contingent of people

2096
01:55:56.629 --> 01:55:59.646
that include like Danielle Bilardo and some other folks

2097
01:55:59.646 --> 01:56:02.703
who are saying the science doesn't bear out

2098
01:56:02.703 --> 01:56:04.952
the negative health implications of olive oil

2099
01:56:04.952 --> 01:56:08.951
on a plant-based diet, we vilified this.

2100
01:56:08.951 --> 01:56:12.546
There is space and room within a healthy plant-based diet

2101
01:56:12.546 --> 01:56:15.379
to include this, what say you sir?

2102
01:56:17.730 --> 01:56:21.530
So, I guess firstly, the likes of Dean Ornish

2103
01:56:21.530 --> 01:56:24.122
and Dr. Esselstyn, these guys are giants--

2104
01:56:24.122 --> 01:56:25.716
Yes, legends.

2105
01:56:25.716 --> 01:56:29.198
And they have done so much good for health

2106
01:56:29.198 --> 01:56:32.053
across the globe, reached millions and millions of people

2107
01:56:32.053 --> 01:56:33.803
and their diets work.

2108
01:56:37.680 --> 01:56:42.141
I am of the view that there is no definitive evidence

2109
01:56:42.141 --> 01:56:44.683
that the addition of olive oil for example

2110
01:56:44.683 --> 01:56:47.837
to a plant-based diet makes it inferior

2111
01:56:47.837 --> 01:56:52.731
and perhaps it could make it superior in some ways

2112
01:56:52.731 --> 01:56:56.898
and I think if you look at say Dean Ornish's study

2113
01:56:59.979 --> 01:57:03.696
for example, I think people look at that and say...

2114
01:57:03.696 --> 01:57:05.561
Or even Esselstyn's study,

2115
01:57:05.561 --> 01:57:08.385
that there was no oil in those studies

2116
01:57:08.385 --> 01:57:10.722
and therefore that must be why they got the results

2117
01:57:10.722 --> 01:57:11.889
that they did.

2118
01:57:13.231 --> 01:57:16.522
But in the case of Dean Ornish,

2119
01:57:16.522 --> 01:57:19.376
you have to realize that compared to the control group,

2120
01:57:19.376 --> 01:57:22.695
there was many changes, it wasn't just--

2121
01:57:22.695 --> 01:57:24.489
There were a lot of lifestyle changes that they made

2122
01:57:24.489 --> 01:57:25.322
that were part of that

2123
01:57:25.322 --> 01:57:28.002
and he's never tried to say otherwise.

2124
01:57:28.002 --> 01:57:30.852
Exactly and I mean just from a dietary point of view,

2125
01:57:30.852 --> 01:57:33.004
there were many changes though.

2126
01:57:33.004 --> 01:57:36.837
People increasing their fruit and vegetable consumption

2127
01:57:36.837 --> 01:57:40.380
and decreasing their animal product consumption

2128
01:57:40.380 --> 01:57:44.893
and the removal of oil was just one change of many.

2129
01:57:44.893 --> 01:57:48.386
So, you would really need a third arm in that study

2130
01:57:48.386 --> 01:57:52.886
to try and independently tease out the effects of oil.

2131
01:57:54.525 --> 01:57:55.358
And in fact,

2132
01:57:55.358 --> 01:57:57.061
you would need to change the study design a little bit

2133
01:57:57.061 --> 01:57:59.905
to remove the exercise and the stress component

2134
01:57:59.905 --> 01:58:01.053
and all these other variables

2135
01:58:01.053 --> 01:58:04.134
that could be affecting the outcome.

2136
01:58:04.134 --> 01:58:06.879
So, I think that there's a little bit

2137
01:58:06.879 --> 01:58:10.546
of kind of overextrapolation from that study

2138
01:58:12.050 --> 01:58:16.883
and also Essylstn's study as well that's an oil-free diet.

2139
01:58:19.523 --> 01:58:21.164
There are some nuances with that study

2140
01:58:21.164 --> 01:58:22.935
that are still debated,

2141
01:58:22.935 --> 01:58:27.102
but I don't think we need to sort of go into that.

2142
01:58:28.031 --> 01:58:29.331
If you then zoom out

2143
01:58:29.331 --> 01:58:32.735
and look at other studies like PREDIMED

2144
01:58:32.735 --> 01:58:37.119
and PREDIMED was a study of around 7,000 people,

2145
01:58:37.119 --> 01:58:40.816
primary prevention, so they didn't have heart disease

2146
01:58:40.816 --> 01:58:45.733
and they had a control group, standard care, standard diet,

2147
01:58:46.960 --> 01:58:48.655
they had two intervention groups.

2148
01:58:48.655 --> 01:58:51.928
These were Mediterranean diets, one with oil

2149
01:58:51.928 --> 01:58:55.594
and one with nuts, olive oil I should say.

2150
01:58:55.594 --> 01:58:58.677
And they saw over a five-year period,

2151
01:59:00.663 --> 01:59:04.407
those in the Mediterranean groups had around 30% reduction

2152
01:59:04.407 --> 01:59:07.074
in cardiovascular disease events

2153
01:59:08.345 --> 01:59:11.736
and there was no difference between the Mediterranean group

2154
01:59:11.736 --> 01:59:13.221
that had nuts added

2155
01:59:13.221 --> 01:59:16.345
or the Mediterranean group that had olive oil added.

2156
01:59:16.345 --> 01:59:19.678
So, that kind of speaks to the fact that

2157
01:59:24.516 --> 01:59:28.183
olive oil can be included in a healthy diet.

2158
01:59:29.082 --> 01:59:31.675
It's not to say that there's not going to be a future study

2159
01:59:31.675 --> 01:59:35.687
that does look at the independent effects of olive oil

2160
01:59:35.687 --> 01:59:39.695
in a completely plant-based diet and shows a difference,

2161
01:59:39.695 --> 01:59:41.195
but at this stage,

2162
01:59:42.111 --> 01:59:46.861
I think it's kind of the least of most people's concerns,

2163
01:59:49.161 --> 01:59:52.840
the most important things are driving saturated fat down.

2164
01:59:52.840 --> 01:59:56.430
We know that the inclusion of polyunsaturated fats

2165
01:59:56.430 --> 01:59:59.182
as I spoke to earlier from those metabolic ward studies

2166
01:59:59.182 --> 02:00:00.908
is actually very important.

2167
02:00:00.908 --> 02:00:04.586
These polyunsaturated fats are inherently beneficial,

2168
02:00:04.586 --> 02:00:07.434
they will help to lower cholesterol.

2169
02:00:07.434 --> 02:00:12.028
So, their inclusion through nuts and seeds or olive oil

2170
02:00:12.028 --> 02:00:15.725
if that's right for you I think is a good idea.

2171
02:00:15.725 --> 02:00:18.001
So, when someone hits you up and says,

2172
02:00:18.001 --> 02:00:20.255
"Simon, I hear everything you're saying,

2173
02:00:20.255 --> 02:00:23.655
you make a lot of sense, but I've been on a low-carb diet

2174
02:00:23.655 --> 02:00:26.641
or I've been on a keto diet and I'm having great results.

2175
02:00:26.641 --> 02:00:29.031
I feel great I've lost a bunch of weight,"

2176
02:00:29.031 --> 02:00:30.856
what is your response to that?

2177
02:00:30.856 --> 02:00:33.801
Like how do you think about low-carb diets,

2178
02:00:33.801 --> 02:00:38.301
the keto diet, et cetera in contrast to a higher-carb,

2179
02:00:39.254 --> 02:00:40.671
plant-based diet?

2180
02:00:41.622 --> 02:00:43.701
I might just add one thing to the oil.

2181
02:00:43.701 --> 02:00:45.856
Well, hold that thought because I just remembered something

2182
02:00:45.856 --> 02:00:48.023
that I think is important.

2183
02:00:49.529 --> 02:00:50.601
There are some studies

2184
02:00:50.601 --> 02:00:53.918
showing that oil impairs endothelial cell function

2185
02:00:53.918 --> 02:00:56.505
and you may have heard that before.

2186
02:00:56.505 --> 02:00:59.181
And these are acute studies

2187
02:00:59.181 --> 02:01:01.470
looking at when you feed someone oil,

2188
02:01:01.470 --> 02:01:04.605
what happens in the postprandial period

2189
02:01:04.605 --> 02:01:05.960
after they consume that oil

2190
02:01:05.960 --> 02:01:08.312
to their endothelial cell function?

2191
02:01:08.312 --> 02:01:10.979
And you see that it is impaired.

2192
02:01:13.165 --> 02:01:16.028
And that is often science that people point to to say,

2193
02:01:16.028 --> 02:01:17.307
"Hey, look."

2194
02:01:17.307 --> 02:01:19.051
Impaired of what, what does that mean?

2195
02:01:19.051 --> 02:01:22.301
Well, you get a change in blood flow,

2196
02:01:25.016 --> 02:01:27.759
a negative change in blood flow.

2197
02:01:27.759 --> 02:01:32.009
And so the idea here is that the consumption of oil

2198
02:01:36.091 --> 02:01:40.465
is negatively affecting the endothelial cell function,

2199
02:01:40.465 --> 02:01:42.912
endothelial cells line the arteries

2200
02:01:42.912 --> 02:01:44.927
and if this is happening acutely,

2201
02:01:44.927 --> 02:01:49.270
maybe it explains what's happening chronically.

2202
02:01:49.270 --> 02:01:54.187
But there is I guess some issues with this train of thought

2203
02:01:55.114 --> 02:01:59.796
exercise also acutely impairs endothelial cell function

2204
02:01:59.796 --> 02:02:01.866
and so does sleep.

2205
02:02:01.866 --> 02:02:03.616
So, it's interesting,

2206
02:02:06.269 --> 02:02:08.234
but I guess we have to consider

2207
02:02:08.234 --> 02:02:10.430
that not everything that happens acutely

2208
02:02:10.430 --> 02:02:13.907
is representative of what happens chronically.

2209
02:02:13.907 --> 02:02:15.514
And sometimes an acute stress

2210
02:02:15.514 --> 02:02:20.116
can actually lead to an adaptation and a benefit

2211
02:02:20.116 --> 02:02:21.177
in the long-term.

2212
02:02:21.177 --> 02:02:24.260
So, I think that's worthwhile adding.

2213
02:02:26.395 --> 02:02:29.895
So, onto the next thing that I asked you about.

2214
02:02:29.895 --> 02:02:32.996
Low carb versus high carb?

2215
02:02:32.996 --> 02:02:34.579
Basically.

2216
02:02:36.043 --> 02:02:39.387
From a weight loss point of view,

2217
02:02:39.387 --> 02:02:41.887
there is a plethora of studies

2218
02:02:43.012 --> 02:02:45.331
that have essentially shown

2219
02:02:45.331 --> 02:02:49.352
there is no significant benefit to either.

2220
02:02:49.352 --> 02:02:53.719
And some of probably the best design studies

2221
02:02:53.719 --> 02:02:56.382
come from professor Christopher Gardner

2222
02:02:56.382 --> 02:02:57.715
out of Stanford.

2223
02:02:59.288 --> 02:03:01.553
He initially ran a study in the early 2000s

2224
02:03:01.553 --> 02:03:05.636
called the A to Z study and this featured Atkins,

2225
02:03:07.469 --> 02:03:10.886
it featured the Zone diet, the Learn diet

2226
02:03:11.797 --> 02:03:13.612
and the Ornish diet.

2227
02:03:13.612 --> 02:03:16.255
All of these have a different ratio of macronutrients,

2228
02:03:16.255 --> 02:03:20.433
so some of them are low-carb, some are high-carb

2229
02:03:20.433 --> 02:03:22.100
and 12-months trial.

2230
02:03:23.596 --> 02:03:26.822
By the end of that 12 months,

2231
02:03:26.822 --> 02:03:28.796
there was no significant difference

2232
02:03:28.796 --> 02:03:32.343
between Atkins and Ornish and Atkins is the lowest-carb

2233
02:03:32.343 --> 02:03:34.061
and Ornish is the highest-carb.

2234
02:03:34.061 --> 02:03:36.446
Strictly through the rubric of weight loss.

2235
02:03:36.446 --> 02:03:39.084
This is strictly through the rubric of weight loss

2236
02:03:39.084 --> 02:03:43.465
and the idea with this was they had dieticians

2237
02:03:43.465 --> 02:03:48.401
who would use the most prominent books, so the Atkins book

2238
02:03:48.401 --> 02:03:50.304
and the Ornish book at the time

2239
02:03:50.304 --> 02:03:54.701
to educate people on how to adopt these diets.

2240
02:03:54.701 --> 02:03:55.534
And that's what--

2241
02:03:55.534 --> 02:03:56.942
So, this was done a while ago then?

2242
02:03:56.942 --> 02:03:58.575
Those books like the "Zone Diet"

2243
02:03:58.575 --> 02:04:00.392
was like in the early 90s, right?

2244
02:04:00.392 --> 02:04:04.516
Yes, so this was done I think in 2003.

2245
02:04:04.516 --> 02:04:07.885
And there was a very interesting finding though Rich

2246
02:04:07.885 --> 02:04:08.865
from this study.

2247
02:04:08.865 --> 02:04:13.865
And that was that within the groups, so within the low-carb

2248
02:04:14.016 --> 02:04:17.898
and within the high-carb, some people did really well

2249
02:04:17.898 --> 02:04:20.415
and some people did really poorly.

2250
02:04:20.415 --> 02:04:24.598
So, there was this sort of wide variation.

2251
02:04:24.598 --> 02:04:27.933
And Christopher Gardner was left thinking,

2252
02:04:27.933 --> 02:04:31.187
"Well, I wonder what explains that?

2253
02:04:31.187 --> 02:04:33.389
Why would some people do really well on low-carb,

2254
02:04:33.389 --> 02:04:36.443
some do really poorly and the same for high-carb?"

2255
02:04:36.443 --> 02:04:38.563
And he thought maybe it's genetics

2256
02:04:38.563 --> 02:04:40.659
or perhaps it's insulin resistance,

2257
02:04:40.659 --> 02:04:45.159
how well your body can utilize glucose, carbohydrates.

2258
02:04:46.513 --> 02:04:51.346
And so he went away and got funding and ran another study,

2259
02:04:52.301 --> 02:04:55.740
a larger study, 600 people, again 12 months

2260
02:04:55.740 --> 02:04:57.815
called DIETFITS.

2261
02:04:57.815 --> 02:05:00.565
And this study only had two arms.

2262
02:05:02.130 --> 02:05:06.713
The two arms were a low-carb diet and a high-carb diet.

2263
02:05:07.655 --> 02:05:11.655
And he knew that what he would see in each group

2264
02:05:14.480 --> 02:05:17.141
was some people do really well and some really poorly

2265
02:05:17.141 --> 02:05:19.796
and he wanted to see if perhaps these genetic markers...

2266
02:05:19.796 --> 02:05:23.208
He was looking at three different genetic variations,

2267
02:05:23.208 --> 02:05:26.453
he wanted to see if they would affect the outcome

2268
02:05:26.453 --> 02:05:29.940
or if insulin resistance would affect the outcome.

2269
02:05:29.940 --> 02:05:31.857
And very interestingly,

2270
02:05:32.750 --> 02:05:36.470
none of that predicted who was successful

2271
02:05:36.470 --> 02:05:38.611
and who was unsuccessful in weight loss.

2272
02:05:38.611 --> 02:05:41.514
So, we're left in this position today

2273
02:05:41.514 --> 02:05:44.264
where that is largely unanswered.

2274
02:05:45.481 --> 02:05:50.339
For some reason some people do better on high-carb

2275
02:05:50.339 --> 02:05:52.447
and there's certain people that are doing better

2276
02:05:52.447 --> 02:05:53.447
on low-carb.

2277
02:05:54.292 --> 02:05:58.875
And perhaps future research will tease out why that is,

2278
02:06:00.175 --> 02:06:01.477
but at this stage,

2279
02:06:01.477 --> 02:06:05.397
I've kind of changed my view a little bit I'd have to say

2280
02:06:05.397 --> 02:06:09.510
on low-carb and I think I've softened my approach a bit.

2281
02:06:09.510 --> 02:06:11.269
If that works for someone,

2282
02:06:11.269 --> 02:06:15.019
then I think that meeting them where they are

2283
02:06:16.281 --> 02:06:17.827
is the better approach

2284
02:06:17.827 --> 02:06:20.806
and that's not to say you can't do a low-carb diet

2285
02:06:20.806 --> 02:06:22.163
in a healthy way.

2286
02:06:22.163 --> 02:06:24.368
I mean, it's what kind of low-carb diet,

2287
02:06:24.368 --> 02:06:25.875
if you're doing a low-carb diet

2288
02:06:25.875 --> 02:06:28.000
that's rife with saturated fat

2289
02:06:28.000 --> 02:06:30.561
and all these other things that we've been talking about,

2290
02:06:30.561 --> 02:06:31.394
not so good,

2291
02:06:31.394 --> 02:06:34.599
but there are ways of doing it in a healthy manner.

2292
02:06:34.599 --> 02:06:36.449
Yeah, so you can...

2293
02:06:36.449 --> 02:06:39.240
Going back to that theme, the plant-predominant theme,

2294
02:06:39.240 --> 02:06:42.704
you can do a low-carb diet within that theme.

2295
02:06:42.704 --> 02:06:47.704
And instead of loading up on the red meats and the dairy,

2296
02:06:48.015 --> 02:06:52.759
you're going to be more emphasizing the nuts and the seeds

2297
02:06:52.759 --> 02:06:54.952
and the plant protein.

2298
02:06:54.952 --> 02:06:57.202
And since writing this book

2299
02:06:59.899 --> 02:07:04.899
and I'm not sure exactly what communities have been speaking

2300
02:07:05.204 --> 02:07:06.037
about the book,

2301
02:07:06.037 --> 02:07:09.832
but I've had a number of people from the low-carb community

2302
02:07:09.832 --> 02:07:11.608
get in contact with me.

2303
02:07:11.608 --> 02:07:16.216
And what I've realized Rich is there are a lot of people

2304
02:07:16.216 --> 02:07:20.883
in the low-carb community who despite what Paul Saladino

2305
02:07:22.108 --> 02:07:26.257
and Shawn Baker might be saying about cholesterol,

2306
02:07:26.257 --> 02:07:29.580
there are a lot of people who do believe the science,

2307
02:07:29.580 --> 02:07:32.278
low-carb's working for them, they're losing weight,

2308
02:07:32.278 --> 02:07:34.353
but they do believe the science

2309
02:07:34.353 --> 02:07:38.070
when it comes to heart disease and they are fearful.

2310
02:07:38.070 --> 02:07:41.653
These are young parents, people 40s and 50s

2311
02:07:43.678 --> 02:07:46.178
who they found a way of eating

2312
02:07:47.105 --> 02:07:49.108
that does help them lose weight,

2313
02:07:49.108 --> 02:07:50.734
but at the same time they go to their doctor

2314
02:07:50.734 --> 02:07:54.388
and their doctor's like, "What are you doing?

2315
02:07:54.388 --> 02:07:56.787
We need to put you on a statin."

2316
02:07:56.787 --> 02:07:58.204
And that's scary.

2317
02:08:00.003 --> 02:08:03.844
So, what I found is that there are a large number of people

2318
02:08:03.844 --> 02:08:06.567
who are really receptive to this idea of,

2319
02:08:06.567 --> 02:08:11.464
"Hang on, you don't have to abandon this approach

2320
02:08:11.464 --> 02:08:12.703
that's working for you.

2321
02:08:12.703 --> 02:08:16.014
You can actually do it in a way that is evidence-based,

2322
02:08:16.014 --> 02:08:19.265
we can keep you on this path of weight loss

2323
02:08:19.265 --> 02:08:22.190
if that's right for you and at the same time,

2324
02:08:22.190 --> 02:08:26.134
we can modify the foods that are in that diet

2325
02:08:26.134 --> 02:08:29.907
in a way that will drive your cholesterol levels down,

2326
02:08:29.907 --> 02:08:32.740
drive your insulin resistance down

2327
02:08:33.797 --> 02:08:37.347
and overall not just deliver on weight loss,

2328
02:08:37.347 --> 02:08:40.575
but also put you at lower risk of these diseases."

2329
02:08:40.575 --> 02:08:44.460
And what would some of those cornerstone foods be?

2330
02:08:44.460 --> 02:08:47.598
So, it's all about driving the saturated fat down

2331
02:08:47.598 --> 02:08:50.404
and increasing polyunsaturated fats.

2332
02:08:50.404 --> 02:08:55.168
You're trying to cut back on meats and dairy in particular

2333
02:08:55.168 --> 02:08:58.918
and you're wanting to increase nuts and seeds

2334
02:09:00.255 --> 02:09:03.047
and seafood if you eat seafood

2335
02:09:03.047 --> 02:09:07.208
is a much better source of polyunsaturated fats,

2336
02:09:07.208 --> 02:09:11.472
much lower in saturated fats compared to meat.

2337
02:09:11.472 --> 02:09:15.335
So, those are the main little tweaks,

2338
02:09:15.335 --> 02:09:18.585
but also finding ways within their diet

2339
02:09:19.486 --> 02:09:22.653
to increase fiber as well is important

2340
02:09:23.999 --> 02:09:26.781
and you can do that in a low-carb manner

2341
02:09:26.781 --> 02:09:29.316
where instead of it being leaning heavily

2342
02:09:29.316 --> 02:09:31.620
on the sort of starchy foods,

2343
02:09:31.620 --> 02:09:34.926
you're really trying to increase the volume

2344
02:09:34.926 --> 02:09:39.509
of sort of low calorie, very nutrient-dense vegetables.

2345
02:09:40.391 --> 02:09:42.576
Like super low glycemic vegetables.

2346
02:09:42.576 --> 02:09:45.948
Yes, so lots of cruciferous vegetables

2347
02:09:45.948 --> 02:09:48.447
and dark, leafy greens.

2348
02:09:48.447 --> 02:09:53.447
And so you can easily shift this sort of animal-heavy,

2349
02:09:53.648 --> 02:09:58.648
low-carb diet to a more Mediterranean style, low-carb diet

2350
02:09:59.236 --> 02:10:02.653
and you see very quick, huge improvements

2351
02:10:03.491 --> 02:10:07.359
in their blood lipids, improvements in blood pressure

2352
02:10:07.359 --> 02:10:08.886
and they feel better.

2353
02:10:08.886 --> 02:10:13.184
But you personally eat around 50, 30, 20, right?

2354
02:10:13.184 --> 02:10:17.017
50% carb, 30% protein, 20% fat.

2355
02:10:19.693 --> 02:10:20.602
Yeah, if I was to count it out--

2356
02:10:20.602 --> 02:10:22.122
That's a lot of protein.

2357
02:10:22.122 --> 02:10:24.717
I think actually somewhere around there,

2358
02:10:24.717 --> 02:10:26.209
maybe or perhaps a little more fat

2359
02:10:26.209 --> 02:10:29.263
and a little less protein than that,

2360
02:10:29.263 --> 02:10:32.508
but that would be kind of around the mark

2361
02:10:32.508 --> 02:10:36.091
and that's what leaves me feeling the best.

2362
02:10:37.703 --> 02:10:41.627
I'm big on diet quality being more important

2363
02:10:41.627 --> 02:10:44.297
than focusing too much on the macronutrients,

2364
02:10:44.297 --> 02:10:47.242
the macronutrients can be important

2365
02:10:47.242 --> 02:10:52.113
if you have a very specific goal for example athletes

2366
02:10:52.113 --> 02:10:54.174
or bodybuilders, et cetera,

2367
02:10:54.174 --> 02:10:57.424
but I think what's more important first

2368
02:10:58.646 --> 02:11:02.035
is the quality of those macronutrients,

2369
02:11:02.035 --> 02:11:06.589
the quality of the fat, the quality of the carbohydrates,

2370
02:11:06.589 --> 02:11:10.117
it's less jelly beans, more black beans,

2371
02:11:10.117 --> 02:11:11.775
the quality of the protein--
That's my favorite quote

2372
02:11:11.775 --> 02:11:13.241
of yours by the way.

2373
02:11:13.241 --> 02:11:15.824
So, there are a lot of people

2374
02:11:18.905 --> 02:11:21.838
that are very anti-carbohydrates,

2375
02:11:21.838 --> 02:11:25.005
but carbohydrates is an umbrella term.

2376
02:11:26.028 --> 02:11:27.562
And what matters is

2377
02:11:27.562 --> 02:11:30.537
where you're getting those carbohydrates from.

2378
02:11:30.537 --> 02:11:31.602
If they're coming from jelly beans

2379
02:11:31.602 --> 02:11:33.774
then you're going to run into problems.

2380
02:11:33.774 --> 02:11:35.691
This idea in terms of

2381
02:11:37.696 --> 02:11:39.653
kind of falsified narratives out there

2382
02:11:39.653 --> 02:11:42.082
vilifying all carbohydrates,

2383
02:11:42.082 --> 02:11:44.797
lumping in the black beans

2384
02:11:44.797 --> 02:11:46.678
with the jelly beans is preposterous

2385
02:11:46.678 --> 02:11:51.178
or analogizing fruit to diet soda or soda, sugar soda.

2386
02:11:54.171 --> 02:11:57.371
We've talked extensively on this podcast about protein,

2387
02:11:57.371 --> 02:11:59.154
meeting your protein needs on a plant-based diet.

2388
02:11:59.154 --> 02:12:00.476
So, I don't wanna belabor the point,

2389
02:12:00.476 --> 02:12:02.007
but I haven't had you here to do it

2390
02:12:02.007 --> 02:12:05.840
and you being a very strong physical specimen,

2391
02:12:07.262 --> 02:12:10.605
I think it's worthy if somebody is tuning into this

2392
02:12:10.605 --> 02:12:12.371
and isn't familiar with either of us

2393
02:12:12.371 --> 02:12:14.472
and perhaps this is their first introduction

2394
02:12:14.472 --> 02:12:16.641
to some of the ideas we're talking about

2395
02:12:16.641 --> 02:12:17.957
to at least spend a couple of minutes

2396
02:12:17.957 --> 02:12:20.539
talking about the big protein question

2397
02:12:20.539 --> 02:12:23.393
because it still propagates

2398
02:12:23.393 --> 02:12:25.119
in terms of people who are interested

2399
02:12:25.119 --> 02:12:27.494
in moving towards a more plant-centric diet,

2400
02:12:27.494 --> 02:12:30.828
but are fearful of what that implies

2401
02:12:30.828 --> 02:12:32.623
in terms of meeting their protein needs

2402
02:12:32.623 --> 02:12:37.040
especially for the active people amongst that cohort.

2403
02:12:38.361 --> 02:12:41.694
So, this was a very large fear of mine

2404
02:12:43.533 --> 02:12:46.753
when I was initially making the changes to my diet.

2405
02:12:46.753 --> 02:12:49.290
And I always remind myself of that

2406
02:12:49.290 --> 02:12:53.273
because it's easy to kind of dismiss it

2407
02:12:53.273 --> 02:12:57.690
and forget that the sort of general school of thought

2408
02:12:59.465 --> 02:13:02.138
is that you get your protein from animal foods

2409
02:13:02.138 --> 02:13:04.982
and that plants are missing protein.

2410
02:13:04.982 --> 02:13:07.297
To this day, when you go to a restaurant

2411
02:13:07.297 --> 02:13:09.531
or you go to Chipotle, they say,

2412
02:13:09.531 --> 02:13:12.508
"What protein do you want on this?"

2413
02:13:12.508 --> 02:13:16.675
And very rarely does that include plant protein.

2414
02:13:18.410 --> 02:13:20.631
Although it's starting to change,

2415
02:13:20.631 --> 02:13:25.326
I think the food environment is starting to modify

2416
02:13:25.326 --> 02:13:29.503
and we're seeing increases in plant protein options

2417
02:13:29.503 --> 02:13:33.134
and I was chatting with Paul Shapiro

2418
02:13:33.134 --> 02:13:34.138
couple weeks ago now

2419
02:13:34.138 --> 02:13:36.196
and he's doing exciting things with microbe proteins--

2420
02:13:36.196 --> 02:13:37.029
It's pretty wild

2421
02:13:37.029 --> 02:13:40.657
what his company is doing right now with mushrooms.

2422
02:13:40.657 --> 02:13:41.765
Yeah, it's crazy,

2423
02:13:41.765 --> 02:13:45.682
feeding these fungi microbes potato and sorghum

2424
02:13:47.854 --> 02:13:52.309
and then producing this super protein-rich food,

2425
02:13:52.309 --> 02:13:55.561
I tried some as well and it's incredible, so--

2426
02:13:55.561 --> 02:13:58.477
Me I haven't tried it yet.

2427
02:13:58.477 --> 02:14:01.885
That's certainly the future of food.

2428
02:14:01.885 --> 02:14:06.885
But overall, I think there are a few aspects of protein

2429
02:14:07.175 --> 02:14:09.758
that are somewhat misunderstood

2430
02:14:11.043 --> 02:14:15.635
and we can perhaps clear up some of that confusion.

2431
02:14:15.635 --> 02:14:18.497
The first would be that plant protein

2432
02:14:18.497 --> 02:14:22.545
is missing certain essential amino acids.

2433
02:14:22.545 --> 02:14:26.508
And protein is made up of amino acids,

2434
02:14:26.508 --> 02:14:30.077
they are the little building blocks

2435
02:14:30.077 --> 02:14:33.397
and our body can make 11 of them

2436
02:14:33.397 --> 02:14:36.846
and the other nine are considered essential amino acids,

2437
02:14:36.846 --> 02:14:38.885
we have to get them through our food.

2438
02:14:38.885 --> 02:14:41.302
And the idea for many decades

2439
02:14:43.756 --> 02:14:48.673
has been that plants are missing some of those amino acids.

2440
02:14:49.543 --> 02:14:53.187
And you hear this idea of complete protein

2441
02:14:53.187 --> 02:14:56.520
and often people speak to quinoa and soy

2442
02:14:58.105 --> 02:15:02.173
and I think definition here is really important.

2443
02:15:02.173 --> 02:15:03.759
It's critical to this

2444
02:15:03.759 --> 02:15:05.402
because I think there's a misunderstanding

2445
02:15:05.402 --> 02:15:07.728
of what complete protein means.

2446
02:15:07.728 --> 02:15:09.746
I think most people think,

2447
02:15:09.746 --> 02:15:12.383
"Well, if quinoa and soy are complete proteins,

2448
02:15:12.383 --> 02:15:16.568
that means that these other plants are missing amino acids."

2449
02:15:16.568 --> 02:15:18.039
They're inferior.

2450
02:15:18.039 --> 02:15:21.539
Yeah or that they're just totally missing something.

2451
02:15:21.539 --> 02:15:23.054
And that's not true.

2452
02:15:23.054 --> 02:15:27.971
So, all plants contain all nine essential amino acids.

2453
02:15:27.971 --> 02:15:29.269
They're not missing,

2454
02:15:29.269 --> 02:15:31.133
they're just some of those plants

2455
02:15:31.133 --> 02:15:34.801
where certain amino acids are in lower amounts.

2456
02:15:34.801 --> 02:15:38.466
And so the definition of incomplete

2457
02:15:38.466 --> 02:15:40.920
actually by definition within science

2458
02:15:40.920 --> 02:15:44.447
although it's not used this way in general conversation

2459
02:15:44.447 --> 02:15:47.409
is not that an amino acid is missing,

2460
02:15:47.409 --> 02:15:51.076
it's just that it is in a smaller percentage

2461
02:15:52.207 --> 02:15:56.015
and if you were to eat only that food

2462
02:15:56.015 --> 02:15:57.746
for all of your calories,

2463
02:15:57.746 --> 02:15:59.771
then you might run into some trouble.

2464
02:15:59.771 --> 02:16:03.560
You might not consume enough of a certain amino acid

2465
02:16:03.560 --> 02:16:06.607
to meet your body's daily requirement.

2466
02:16:06.607 --> 02:16:11.607
So, you can imagine that that's kind of a useful definition

2467
02:16:11.649 --> 02:16:13.837
for developing countries,

2468
02:16:13.837 --> 02:16:17.454
places where there is food security problems

2469
02:16:17.454 --> 02:16:21.117
and if someone's living on one or two foods,

2470
02:16:21.117 --> 02:16:22.559
then that's important,

2471
02:16:22.559 --> 02:16:23.740
but if you're eating a diet

2472
02:16:23.740 --> 02:16:27.407
where you have an abundance of food options,

2473
02:16:28.370 --> 02:16:31.722
then really it's not that helpful.

2474
02:16:31.722 --> 02:16:35.055
And so if you are eating with diversity,

2475
02:16:37.951 --> 02:16:41.921
you might be consuming for example grains

2476
02:16:41.921 --> 02:16:43.798
that are low in lysine.

2477
02:16:43.798 --> 02:16:47.826
And look, it's true if you were to eat certain grains

2478
02:16:47.826 --> 02:16:49.547
and that was providing your 3,000,

2479
02:16:49.547 --> 02:16:51.432
two and a half thousand calories,

2480
02:16:51.432 --> 02:16:55.154
then you might fall short on lysine.

2481
02:16:55.154 --> 02:16:57.721
And the same could be said about certain beans.

2482
02:16:57.721 --> 02:17:00.653
If you eat a certain bean for all of your calories,

2483
02:17:00.653 --> 02:17:02.824
you might fall short on the thynine,

2484
02:17:02.824 --> 02:17:05.290
but beans are very rich in lysine

2485
02:17:05.290 --> 02:17:09.044
and when you start adding all these foods into your diet,

2486
02:17:09.044 --> 02:17:10.898
some might be low in certain amino acid,

2487
02:17:10.898 --> 02:17:12.906
but the other food is really high.

2488
02:17:12.906 --> 02:17:14.772
It's really an academic exercise

2489
02:17:14.772 --> 02:17:16.958
because almost no one is only eating one food

2490
02:17:16.958 --> 02:17:18.993
unless you're like the spud fit guy

2491
02:17:18.993 --> 02:17:20.784
who is eating just potatoes for a year

2492
02:17:20.784 --> 02:17:22.782
or you are in a developing country

2493
02:17:22.782 --> 02:17:25.908
and there's a legitimate food scarcity issue,

2494
02:17:25.908 --> 02:17:30.544
but almost everybody is eating at least two, three, five,

2495
02:17:30.544 --> 02:17:33.742
six, seven, 10 different foods

2496
02:17:33.742 --> 02:17:36.067
throughout the course of a day or two.

2497
02:17:36.067 --> 02:17:37.294
And if you're doing that,

2498
02:17:37.294 --> 02:17:38.635
you're eating with that diversity

2499
02:17:38.635 --> 02:17:41.206
and you're eating enough calories,

2500
02:17:41.206 --> 02:17:42.809
you will not run into problems.

2501
02:17:42.809 --> 02:17:45.772
You will get all of the essential amino acids

2502
02:17:45.772 --> 02:17:46.953
that your body needs.

2503
02:17:46.953 --> 02:17:51.032
And anyone can run a simple exercise, don't believe me,

2504
02:17:51.032 --> 02:17:54.319
download the chronometer app, it's a free app,

2505
02:17:54.319 --> 02:17:56.768
plug in a day of eating

2506
02:17:56.768 --> 02:18:01.218
and you will quickly see that you will exceed

2507
02:18:01.218 --> 02:18:03.155
the 100% recommended amount

2508
02:18:03.155 --> 02:18:06.072
for all nine essential amino acids.

2509
02:18:06.917 --> 02:18:10.512
And you'll see it's very hard not to exceed,

2510
02:18:10.512 --> 02:18:12.352
it would be more challenging to fall short on them

2511
02:18:12.352 --> 02:18:15.077
than it would be to consume enough of them.

2512
02:18:15.077 --> 02:18:19.430
Is there any validity to the argument of high quality

2513
02:18:19.430 --> 02:18:21.181
versus low quality proteins?

2514
02:18:21.181 --> 02:18:25.396
If we're meeting all of our essential amino acid needs,

2515
02:18:25.396 --> 02:18:28.659
is there a difference in plant protein X

2516
02:18:28.659 --> 02:18:30.982
versus animal protein Y

2517
02:18:30.982 --> 02:18:34.884
beyond just the narrow lens of amino acid intake?

2518
02:18:34.884 --> 02:18:38.356
I mean like IGF-1 and all these other things

2519
02:18:38.356 --> 02:18:42.869
that come into play in terms of the impact on our ability

2520
02:18:42.869 --> 02:18:45.536
to build and repair our muscles.

2521
02:18:47.475 --> 02:18:50.475
So, let's step back one step here.

2522
02:18:54.658 --> 02:18:57.654
I think it's important to acknowledge

2523
02:18:57.654 --> 02:19:02.654
that if you look at populations who are vegetarian

2524
02:19:02.654 --> 02:19:05.423
or are vegan across the board,

2525
02:19:05.423 --> 02:19:08.334
whether you're looking at populations in North America

2526
02:19:08.334 --> 02:19:10.025
or in Europe,

2527
02:19:10.025 --> 02:19:13.706
people are consuming more than enough total protein.

2528
02:19:13.706 --> 02:19:15.084
And that's really important.

2529
02:19:15.084 --> 02:19:16.894
So, there's a paper just out

2530
02:19:16.894 --> 02:19:20.183
from Slovenia health-conscious vegans

2531
02:19:20.183 --> 02:19:21.754
and these people were consuming

2532
02:19:21.754 --> 02:19:24.671
about 1.5 grams of protein per kilo

2533
02:19:25.573 --> 02:19:27.151
and the recommendation

2534
02:19:27.151 --> 02:19:30.318
is to consume 0.83 grams per kilogram,

2535
02:19:31.885 --> 02:19:33.945
that's just for everyday functioning,

2536
02:19:33.945 --> 02:19:34.971
not if you're an athlete,

2537
02:19:34.971 --> 02:19:37.246
if you're an athlete it's gonna be higher

2538
02:19:37.246 --> 02:19:39.682
in the realm of 1.2 grams per kilo

2539
02:19:39.682 --> 02:19:42.823
up to sort of 1.6 grams per kilo

2540
02:19:42.823 --> 02:19:44.342
But first and foremost,

2541
02:19:44.342 --> 02:19:47.511
these populations of people eating only plants

2542
02:19:47.511 --> 02:19:49.261
or almost only plants

2543
02:19:50.252 --> 02:19:54.079
are getting more than enough total protein.

2544
02:19:54.079 --> 02:19:57.532
And then the question is around protein quality

2545
02:19:57.532 --> 02:19:59.662
and often people wonder,

2546
02:19:59.662 --> 02:20:03.970
"Well, is there a difference in bioavailability as well?"

2547
02:20:03.970 --> 02:20:07.989
And you hear about animal protein being much more superior

2548
02:20:07.989 --> 02:20:09.972
in terms of absorption

2549
02:20:09.972 --> 02:20:13.049
and your body's ability to utilize it.

2550
02:20:13.049 --> 02:20:17.632
And of course the RDA and when they did the guidelines,

2551
02:20:19.903 --> 02:20:21.440
they had to consider this

2552
02:20:21.440 --> 02:20:24.449
because if there was a really, really big difference,

2553
02:20:24.449 --> 02:20:27.251
they would need separate guidelines.

2554
02:20:27.251 --> 02:20:31.732
0.83 grams per kilo would not be for everyone,

2555
02:20:31.732 --> 02:20:33.055
it would be for omnivores

2556
02:20:33.055 --> 02:20:34.503
and then for vegetarians and vegans,

2557
02:20:34.503 --> 02:20:36.281
there would need to be a separate guideline--

2558
02:20:36.281 --> 02:20:38.687
If there was a 20% reduction

2559
02:20:38.687 --> 02:20:41.687
in bioavailability of plant protein,

2560
02:20:43.307 --> 02:20:47.453
then you would have to increase that up from 0.83.

2561
02:20:47.453 --> 02:20:48.876
Yeah, which there's not

2562
02:20:48.876 --> 02:20:51.879
and that's based on nitrogen balance studies,

2563
02:20:51.879 --> 02:20:55.046
which essentially can look at how well

2564
02:20:55.946 --> 02:20:58.529
your body is absorbing protein.

2565
02:20:59.364 --> 02:21:02.643
And these studies have been able to compare animal protein

2566
02:21:02.643 --> 02:21:04.884
versus plant protein

2567
02:21:04.884 --> 02:21:08.639
and see that there is not a big enough difference

2568
02:21:08.639 --> 02:21:11.913
to justify different recommendations

2569
02:21:11.913 --> 02:21:14.568
for protein requirements for different populations,

2570
02:21:14.568 --> 02:21:17.601
omnivores, vegetarians, vegans.

2571
02:21:17.601 --> 02:21:21.684
Now, I will say that there have been some studies

2572
02:21:23.059 --> 02:21:27.809
where animal protein has been made to look quite superior

2573
02:21:31.359 --> 02:21:32.751
to plant protein.

2574
02:21:32.751 --> 02:21:36.668
And there are a few problems with this research

2575
02:21:38.157 --> 02:21:40.437
or limitations I should say,

2576
02:21:40.437 --> 02:21:44.060
there's not a problem with the studies themselves,

2577
02:21:44.060 --> 02:21:48.548
but limitations in terms of how we can interpret them.

2578
02:21:48.548 --> 02:21:50.393
A lot of this knowledge early on

2579
02:21:50.393 --> 02:21:54.965
was built on animal studies, feeding them different proteins

2580
02:21:54.965 --> 02:21:56.965
and measuring absorption

2581
02:21:58.657 --> 02:22:01.138
and so their physiology is different to us,

2582
02:22:01.138 --> 02:22:03.832
but they can act as a useful model particularly pigs,

2583
02:22:03.832 --> 02:22:08.832
which have a closer digestive system to us compared to rats.

2584
02:22:10.575 --> 02:22:14.452
But one of the biggest problems with this research is that

2585
02:22:14.452 --> 02:22:15.852
by and large across the board

2586
02:22:15.852 --> 02:22:17.663
when it came to plant protein,

2587
02:22:17.663 --> 02:22:20.330
they fed them raw plant protein.

2588
02:22:21.431 --> 02:22:26.050
And we know that cooking beans and grains for example,

2589
02:22:26.050 --> 02:22:29.196
it helps make the nutrients more bioavailable.

2590
02:22:29.196 --> 02:22:33.613
And you and I wouldn't eat a raw bean for that reason

2591
02:22:34.513 --> 02:22:37.930
and so what happened is in those studies,

2592
02:22:39.384 --> 02:22:42.977
the results sort of overstate the differences

2593
02:22:42.977 --> 02:22:46.992
between the animal protein and the plant protein.

2594
02:22:46.992 --> 02:22:50.527
Now, in more recent years, there are now human studies

2595
02:22:50.527 --> 02:22:54.299
that have looked at properly prepared plant proteins

2596
02:22:54.299 --> 02:22:57.160
and in the plant proteins that they've looked at,

2597
02:22:57.160 --> 02:22:59.532
there seems to be only a few percent difference

2598
02:22:59.532 --> 02:23:01.217
in bioavailability

2599
02:23:01.217 --> 02:23:04.717
and there's a paper by Mary Arty from 2019

2600
02:23:05.984 --> 02:23:08.131
that summarizes all of that.

2601
02:23:08.131 --> 02:23:11.644
And so the current position is that

2602
02:23:11.644 --> 02:23:15.439
there might be a slightly lower bioavailability

2603
02:23:15.439 --> 02:23:16.781
of plant protein,

2604
02:23:16.781 --> 02:23:21.039
but it's much less than what had been thought in the past

2605
02:23:21.039 --> 02:23:23.383
and I'll add the caveat there

2606
02:23:23.383 --> 02:23:27.182
that there is probably only around six

2607
02:23:27.182 --> 02:23:30.299
or seven plant proteins that have been tested,

2608
02:23:30.299 --> 02:23:31.776
so I'd love to see more research

2609
02:23:31.776 --> 02:23:36.776
and look at hundreds of different sources of plant proteins,

2610
02:23:36.841 --> 02:23:41.674
but it does seem like there's not a significant difference

2611
02:23:42.682 --> 02:23:45.309
or at this stage reason to be worried

2612
02:23:45.309 --> 02:23:48.142
about bioavailability differences.

2613
02:23:49.609 --> 02:23:52.325
But perhaps more important than any of this

2614
02:23:52.325 --> 02:23:53.636
because let's be honest,

2615
02:23:53.636 --> 02:23:56.211
people listening and you and I,

2616
02:23:56.211 --> 02:23:59.397
bioavailability and percentages around absorption,

2617
02:23:59.397 --> 02:24:00.772
that's not what matters.

2618
02:24:00.772 --> 02:24:05.772
What matters is what happens to various health outcomes

2619
02:24:05.968 --> 02:24:09.308
or short-term outcomes in terms of strength

2620
02:24:09.308 --> 02:24:11.010
and building muscle.

2621
02:24:11.010 --> 02:24:13.658
And I spoke before to the clear benefits

2622
02:24:13.658 --> 02:24:15.831
of swapping animal protein for plant protein

2623
02:24:15.831 --> 02:24:18.403
when it comes to our long-term health.

2624
02:24:18.403 --> 02:24:21.746
That there's no doubt about that.

2625
02:24:21.746 --> 02:24:25.857
It's absolutely consistent in the literature.

2626
02:24:25.857 --> 02:24:29.274
In terms of strength and building muscle,

2627
02:24:30.133 --> 02:24:32.898
this is a really interesting area

2628
02:24:32.898 --> 02:24:35.273
and there's a lot of emerging science here

2629
02:24:35.273 --> 02:24:40.273
and I'm starting to see nutrition scientists in this space

2630
02:24:40.453 --> 02:24:44.828
who have been very adamant that animal protein is king

2631
02:24:44.828 --> 02:24:47.828
starting to change their tune a bit.

2632
02:24:49.016 --> 02:24:53.277
And I don't think it's all sort of said and done,

2633
02:24:53.277 --> 02:24:56.517
but there is some early clues

2634
02:24:56.517 --> 02:25:00.421
that you can still get the same benefits

2635
02:25:00.421 --> 02:25:02.719
in terms of strength and hypertrophy

2636
02:25:02.719 --> 02:25:05.136
from consuming plant protein.

2637
02:25:06.039 --> 02:25:09.415
And we need more research looking at different populations,

2638
02:25:09.415 --> 02:25:11.165
but there's one study

2639
02:25:12.049 --> 02:25:15.717
that came out early this year actually

2640
02:25:15.717 --> 02:25:18.856
and this was a randomized controlled trial

2641
02:25:18.856 --> 02:25:20.917
and really the first of its kind.

2642
02:25:20.917 --> 02:25:22.856
A lot of studies in the past Rich

2643
02:25:22.856 --> 02:25:25.752
that have looked at plant protein

2644
02:25:25.752 --> 02:25:27.425
and looking at resistance training

2645
02:25:27.425 --> 02:25:30.592
and measuring strength and hypertrophy

2646
02:25:32.205 --> 02:25:35.632
have looked at the addition of say a soy protein shake

2647
02:25:35.632 --> 02:25:37.382
to an omnivorous diet

2648
02:25:39.112 --> 02:25:42.956
and versus another group who are eating an omnivorous diet

2649
02:25:42.956 --> 02:25:44.859
with a whey protein shake.

2650
02:25:44.859 --> 02:25:49.859
So, it's very hard to from that study sort of extrapolate

2651
02:25:50.297 --> 02:25:52.470
to a completely plant-exclusive diet

2652
02:25:52.470 --> 02:25:55.540
because their baseline diet was including animal foods--

2653
02:25:55.540 --> 02:25:57.660
They were already meeting their protein needs.

2654
02:25:57.660 --> 02:26:02.577
Yeah and they were not a a person consuming a vegan diet.

2655
02:26:05.043 --> 02:26:07.960
So, it's just a different question.

2656
02:26:10.457 --> 02:26:13.708
Those studies are essentially just comparing

2657
02:26:13.708 --> 02:26:15.973
whey versus soy protein

2658
02:26:15.973 --> 02:26:18.368
in the context of the two different people

2659
02:26:18.368 --> 02:26:19.790
eating the same diet.

2660
02:26:19.790 --> 02:26:20.623
I got it.

2661
02:26:20.623 --> 02:26:24.040
Whereas with this latest study, it was,

2662
02:26:25.516 --> 02:26:29.099
"Let's actually look at healthy adult males

2663
02:26:30.017 --> 02:26:32.858
who are consuming a completely plant-based diet,"

2664
02:26:32.858 --> 02:26:35.709
same sort of diet you and I would consume,

2665
02:26:35.709 --> 02:26:38.709
"with a soy protein shake supplement

2666
02:26:39.644 --> 02:26:43.361
against a group consuming an omnivorous diet

2667
02:26:43.361 --> 02:26:45.358
with a whey protein supplement."

2668
02:26:45.358 --> 02:26:49.300
So, now we have completely plant against an omnivorous diet,

2669
02:26:49.300 --> 02:26:50.557
how most people

2670
02:26:50.557 --> 02:26:54.974
in the sort of resistance training industry would eat

2671
02:26:56.194 --> 02:27:00.444
and we get to see is there a difference in strength

2672
02:27:01.975 --> 02:27:05.945
and in terms of hypertrophy, lean muscle?

2673
02:27:05.945 --> 02:27:09.906
And I should note these were healthy adults who were active,

2674
02:27:09.906 --> 02:27:11.823
but they weren't people

2675
02:27:13.053 --> 02:27:15.234
that had been doing resistance training for years.

2676
02:27:15.234 --> 02:27:19.050
And there was a very important reason for that selection.

2677
02:27:19.050 --> 02:27:21.792
Once you've been training for a very long time,

2678
02:27:21.792 --> 02:27:22.873
it's hard to--

2679
02:27:22.873 --> 02:27:23.806
Yeah, you plateau.

2680
02:27:23.806 --> 02:27:26.889
You plateau, so in a 12 week-trial,

2681
02:27:29.012 --> 02:27:31.325
you're not likely to see enough differences

2682
02:27:31.325 --> 02:27:33.459
to be able to measure

2683
02:27:33.459 --> 02:27:35.282
whether one diet is superior to the other,

2684
02:27:35.282 --> 02:27:38.746
so what they wanted is almost these healthy fit people

2685
02:27:38.746 --> 02:27:43.434
who are going to get those early, newbie gains.

2686
02:27:43.434 --> 02:27:45.573
And I'd see like in this period of rapid growth,

2687
02:27:45.573 --> 02:27:49.637
is there a difference between these different diets?

2688
02:27:49.637 --> 02:27:53.050
So, they brought these folks in,

2689
02:27:53.050 --> 02:27:55.311
they did I think it was 10 or 12 weeks

2690
02:27:55.311 --> 02:27:58.755
of resistance training, this is a study out of Brazil.

2691
02:27:58.755 --> 02:28:01.838
Hamilton Michels, the lead researcher

2692
02:28:02.839 --> 02:28:07.645
and what they found was there was no significant difference

2693
02:28:07.645 --> 02:28:11.072
at all in strength or hypertrophy.

2694
02:28:11.072 --> 02:28:14.272
And both groups had great improvements in both.

2695
02:28:14.272 --> 02:28:17.526
So, look, what can we say from that study?

2696
02:28:17.526 --> 02:28:21.392
Well, we can say in healthy adult males

2697
02:28:21.392 --> 02:28:24.874
that did not have a history of resistance training,

2698
02:28:24.874 --> 02:28:26.818
there seems to be no difference

2699
02:28:26.818 --> 02:28:30.283
in terms of whether it's plant protein or animal protein.

2700
02:28:30.283 --> 02:28:33.962
Yeah, no difference in terms of your ability to make gains

2701
02:28:33.962 --> 02:28:36.222
in a certain period of time.

2702
02:28:36.222 --> 02:28:37.055
That's right.

2703
02:28:37.055 --> 02:28:40.888
And is that the same for an elderly population

2704
02:28:43.164 --> 02:28:46.713
who as you get older, you develop anabolic resistance

2705
02:28:46.713 --> 02:28:49.963
and so your ability to generate muscle,

2706
02:28:52.539 --> 02:28:55.956
to promote lean muscle growth is harder.?

2707
02:28:58.183 --> 02:29:00.569
There are certain ideas out there for example

2708
02:29:00.569 --> 02:29:04.111
that animal proteins might be better in that population

2709
02:29:04.111 --> 02:29:07.020
because of differences in amino acids

2710
02:29:07.020 --> 02:29:11.179
particularly being more concentrated in leucine,

2711
02:29:11.179 --> 02:29:13.420
but these were the same ideas

2712
02:29:13.420 --> 02:29:18.420
that were held not too long ago about healthy adult males.

2713
02:29:18.437 --> 02:29:20.372
And what we're starting to see here

2714
02:29:20.372 --> 02:29:22.667
and I didn't mention this about that study,

2715
02:29:22.667 --> 02:29:25.792
they set both groups at 1.6 grams per kilogram

2716
02:29:25.792 --> 02:29:27.349
of protein intake.

2717
02:29:27.349 --> 02:29:30.928
That seems to be the sort of threshold.

2718
02:29:30.928 --> 02:29:35.894
If you want to absolutely maximize muscle protein synthesis,

2719
02:29:35.894 --> 02:29:37.808
that's the level you need to hit.

2720
02:29:37.808 --> 02:29:38.872
Once you go above that,

2721
02:29:38.872 --> 02:29:42.455
you don't really see any extra improvement.

2722
02:29:43.467 --> 02:29:48.097
So, that's kind of where science is up to today.

2723
02:29:48.097 --> 02:29:49.171
Yeah, the current state of affairs.

2724
02:29:49.171 --> 02:29:50.392
I mean, that's encouraging

2725
02:29:50.392 --> 02:29:51.779
and I would like to see that study

2726
02:29:51.779 --> 02:29:54.263
with elderly people as well because I have heard that,

2727
02:29:54.263 --> 02:29:56.127
like once you reach a certain age

2728
02:29:56.127 --> 02:29:58.877
where your body becomes less able

2729
02:30:00.122 --> 02:30:04.720
to hold onto that muscle mass and make those gains

2730
02:30:04.720 --> 02:30:08.845
that protein then becomes a much more important component

2731
02:30:08.845 --> 02:30:09.928
of your diet.

2732
02:30:11.290 --> 02:30:12.123
And that's coming,

2733
02:30:12.123 --> 02:30:14.379
those researchers are going to do it,

2734
02:30:14.379 --> 02:30:18.060
but having spoken to the researchers

2735
02:30:18.060 --> 02:30:19.367
and I think they were a bit surprised

2736
02:30:19.367 --> 02:30:21.784
by the results of that study,

2737
02:30:22.765 --> 02:30:25.088
I'm not so sure there is confident

2738
02:30:25.088 --> 02:30:28.088
going in to studying the elderly now

2739
02:30:29.268 --> 02:30:33.927
in terms of animal protein being vastly superior

2740
02:30:33.927 --> 02:30:38.260
because in this study of healthy, young adult males,

2741
02:30:39.193 --> 02:30:43.439
even though the protein was matched at 1.6 grams per kilo

2742
02:30:43.439 --> 02:30:47.514
and they had no differences in strength or in lean muscle,

2743
02:30:47.514 --> 02:30:49.176
the plant-based protein group

2744
02:30:49.176 --> 02:30:52.426
did consume significantly less leucine.

2745
02:30:53.527 --> 02:30:55.831
So, they were still able

2746
02:30:55.831 --> 02:30:58.134
to achieve the same health outcomes

2747
02:30:58.134 --> 02:31:02.459
despite the lower concentration of leucine in their diet

2748
02:31:02.459 --> 02:31:04.667
and I think that's an important thing to consider--

2749
02:31:04.667 --> 02:31:06.316
So, what does that tell you?

2750
02:31:06.316 --> 02:31:08.133
What do you extrapolate from that?

2751
02:31:08.133 --> 02:31:12.716
Well, I extrapolate that there may be an overemphasis

2752
02:31:15.054 --> 02:31:18.554
on the importance of dialing into leucine,

2753
02:31:19.794 --> 02:31:22.306
I think it's no doubt is important,

2754
02:31:22.306 --> 02:31:24.870
but most important is total protein.

2755
02:31:24.870 --> 02:31:29.171
And if you're hitting that 1.6 grams per kilogram mark,

2756
02:31:29.171 --> 02:31:31.025
I don't think there is added benefit

2757
02:31:31.025 --> 02:31:36.025
of just continuing to dial up leucine further and further

2758
02:31:36.066 --> 02:31:40.032
and in fact if you speak to longevity scientists,

2759
02:31:40.032 --> 02:31:43.546
the likes of Valter Longo that these guys are gonna say

2760
02:31:43.546 --> 02:31:46.132
one of the inherent benefits of plant protein

2761
02:31:46.132 --> 02:31:47.423
is that it's exposing you

2762
02:31:47.423 --> 02:31:50.590
to lower amounts of leucine and methionine,

2763
02:31:50.590 --> 02:31:54.866
these amino acids that are thought to activate

2764
02:31:54.866 --> 02:31:59.866
not just muscle growth pathways, but also aging pathways.

2765
02:32:00.209 --> 02:32:01.426
It's so interesting.

2766
02:32:01.426 --> 02:32:02.604
It's also fascinating

2767
02:32:02.604 --> 02:32:07.271
to see traditional kind of gym rat bodybuilder type guys

2768
02:32:08.164 --> 02:32:11.831
pivoting towards plant-based protein powders

2769
02:32:13.325 --> 02:32:14.885
instead of the traditional way.

2770
02:32:14.885 --> 02:32:16.685
Like these are not plant-based people,

2771
02:32:16.685 --> 02:32:17.575
they're not vegan people,

2772
02:32:17.575 --> 02:32:19.325
they're probably eating their chicken and their broccoli

2773
02:32:19.325 --> 02:32:21.751
and doing the standard routine,

2774
02:32:21.751 --> 02:32:24.873
yet they're still going to the pea and the brown rice

2775
02:32:24.873 --> 02:32:28.623
and the various new products that are coming onboard

2776
02:32:28.623 --> 02:32:30.521
as opposed to just doing the whey thing

2777
02:32:30.521 --> 02:32:33.805
like traditionally they always have.

2778
02:32:33.805 --> 02:32:37.133
And some of that is probably down to the way

2779
02:32:37.133 --> 02:32:39.925
that whey makes certain people feel

2780
02:32:39.925 --> 02:32:43.686
and there's a lot of lactose intolerance out there.

2781
02:32:43.686 --> 02:32:47.769
So, it's nice to see that change in the community

2782
02:32:51.766 --> 02:32:55.027
and I think this study in particular

2783
02:32:55.027 --> 02:32:58.296
is giving people more and more confidence

2784
02:32:58.296 --> 02:33:00.612
because people need the data.

2785
02:33:00.612 --> 02:33:02.335
There are a lot of great anecdotes out there

2786
02:33:02.335 --> 02:33:05.610
and you can see a lot of people like yourself

2787
02:33:05.610 --> 02:33:09.310
who have had very, very successful careers

2788
02:33:09.310 --> 02:33:13.810
in various forms of performance, the performance world

2789
02:33:14.761 --> 02:33:17.580
be it insurance or be it resistance training,

2790
02:33:17.580 --> 02:33:20.247
but now to have some actual data

2791
02:33:21.550 --> 02:33:24.833
is giving people more confidence to lean into this

2792
02:33:24.833 --> 02:33:26.976
and explore a bit further.

2793
02:33:26.976 --> 02:33:28.586
So, Simon, I wanna switch gears

2794
02:33:28.586 --> 02:33:30.087
and cover some other terrain,

2795
02:33:30.087 --> 02:33:33.337
but before we move off of human health,

2796
02:33:34.231 --> 02:33:38.574
perhaps some final thoughts on LDL and the carnivore diet

2797
02:33:38.574 --> 02:33:40.123
because there's a lot of people out there

2798
02:33:40.123 --> 02:33:41.427
who are being influenced

2799
02:33:41.427 --> 02:33:45.108
by certain quote, unquote influencers

2800
02:33:45.108 --> 02:33:48.691
with respect to this meat heels perspective

2801
02:33:50.647 --> 02:33:52.237
of diet and nutrition,

2802
02:33:52.237 --> 02:33:55.791
so let's just kind of put this to bed.

2803
02:33:55.791 --> 02:33:58.374
So, I am extremely concerned,

2804
02:34:00.882 --> 02:34:04.632
I think it would be a nice way of summarizing

2805
02:34:06.183 --> 02:34:11.119
what we're seeing with people adopting the old mate diets.

2806
02:34:11.119 --> 02:34:13.672
Certainly and I mentioned before that a number of people

2807
02:34:13.672 --> 02:34:16.432
in low-carb community have reached out to me

2808
02:34:16.432 --> 02:34:17.970
and some of these have been people

2809
02:34:17.970 --> 02:34:21.516
that have been eating a completely carnivorous diet

2810
02:34:21.516 --> 02:34:23.778
and it cannot be denied

2811
02:34:23.778 --> 02:34:27.172
that you can get good short-term results.

2812
02:34:27.172 --> 02:34:28.918
Especially if you're moving off

2813
02:34:28.918 --> 02:34:30.292
of a standard American diet,

2814
02:34:30.292 --> 02:34:34.226
you are gonna experience some pretty immediate changes

2815
02:34:34.226 --> 02:34:35.077
in how you feel.

2816
02:34:35.077 --> 02:34:38.045
Right and speaking back to that Stanford study

2817
02:34:38.045 --> 02:34:39.628
and the microbiome,

2818
02:34:41.151 --> 02:34:44.651
it's not sort of far-fetched to understand

2819
02:34:49.424 --> 02:34:52.930
that perhaps there is a certain amount

2820
02:34:52.930 --> 02:34:57.709
of microbiome disruption where the addition of plant foods

2821
02:34:57.709 --> 02:35:00.190
is uncomfortable for people.

2822
02:35:00.190 --> 02:35:01.857
And a carnivore diet

2823
02:35:02.726 --> 02:35:05.009
essentially it's a strict elimination diet.

2824
02:35:05.009 --> 02:35:06.840
And so if these people

2825
02:35:06.840 --> 02:35:09.058
even if they're trying to eat healthfully

2826
02:35:09.058 --> 02:35:11.014
are introducing certain plant foods

2827
02:35:11.014 --> 02:35:14.596
and it's revving up their inflammation and making--

2828
02:35:14.596 --> 02:35:15.429
Sorry to interrupt,

2829
02:35:15.429 --> 02:35:19.358
but if you've been eating strictly carnivorously,

2830
02:35:19.358 --> 02:35:20.647
you're seeding your microbiome

2831
02:35:20.647 --> 02:35:23.397
with a certain type of microbiota

2832
02:35:24.500 --> 02:35:27.000
that feed on that type of food

2833
02:35:28.637 --> 02:35:30.957
and you're lacking the diversity

2834
02:35:30.957 --> 02:35:34.457
to be able to create a healthy environment

2835
02:35:35.675 --> 02:35:38.191
for the plant microbiomes to propagate

2836
02:35:38.191 --> 02:35:41.042
and you'll have digestive issues, you'll have inflammation

2837
02:35:41.042 --> 02:35:42.385
and all the like.

2838
02:35:42.385 --> 02:35:45.635
Yes, I see it as a bit of a band-aid.

2839
02:35:47.279 --> 02:35:51.541
If someone is in a state of chronic inflammation

2840
02:35:51.541 --> 02:35:54.847
and their gut is very inflamed,

2841
02:35:54.847 --> 02:35:57.023
removing a lot of foods does work,

2842
02:35:57.023 --> 02:35:59.940
we see it the low-FODMAP diet for example,

2843
02:35:59.940 --> 02:36:03.381
which is a specific diet for people with IBS,

2844
02:36:03.381 --> 02:36:04.654
you're removing the triggers.

2845
02:36:04.654 --> 02:36:08.737
Now, the problem is the food that you're removing

2846
02:36:10.143 --> 02:36:10.976
is not unhealthy,

2847
02:36:10.976 --> 02:36:13.258
it's just that your microbiome is not set up

2848
02:36:13.258 --> 02:36:14.449
in a way that allows you

2849
02:36:14.449 --> 02:36:17.221
to derive the benefits of that food.

2850
02:36:17.221 --> 02:36:19.938
So, completely removing those foods from your diet

2851
02:36:19.938 --> 02:36:22.908
and doing something like adopting a carnivorous diet,

2852
02:36:22.908 --> 02:36:27.204
it could in the short-term make someone feel a bit better.

2853
02:36:27.204 --> 02:36:29.157
The problem is because you're removing

2854
02:36:29.157 --> 02:36:31.647
all of this prebiotic substrate,

2855
02:36:31.647 --> 02:36:34.491
you're starving those bacteria

2856
02:36:34.491 --> 02:36:37.538
so you're going to as you say over time,

2857
02:36:37.538 --> 02:36:40.207
lose more and more diversity

2858
02:36:40.207 --> 02:36:41.827
and you're not getting all of those benefits

2859
02:36:41.827 --> 02:36:43.922
that we spoke about before

2860
02:36:43.922 --> 02:36:46.560
with regards to the production

2861
02:36:46.560 --> 02:36:50.022
of these short-chain fatty acids, these postbiotics

2862
02:36:50.022 --> 02:36:53.022
that then keep the gut lining intact

2863
02:36:54.426 --> 02:36:58.706
and provide huge benefits both locally in the gut

2864
02:36:58.706 --> 02:37:00.373
and also downstream,

2865
02:37:01.687 --> 02:37:04.056
these metabolites are lowering the risk

2866
02:37:04.056 --> 02:37:05.806
of colorectal cancer.

2867
02:37:06.705 --> 02:37:10.156
They're driving down inflammation throughout the body

2868
02:37:10.156 --> 02:37:11.677
and protecting us

2869
02:37:11.677 --> 02:37:15.343
against things like neurodegenerative diseases.

2870
02:37:15.343 --> 02:37:18.343
So, there's a band-aid solution here

2871
02:37:19.944 --> 02:37:21.944
where you get this short-term benefit.

2872
02:37:21.944 --> 02:37:24.210
What I think is being overlooked is that

2873
02:37:24.210 --> 02:37:27.932
chronic diseases have a long latency period.

2874
02:37:27.932 --> 02:37:32.575
They bubble away under the surface for a while

2875
02:37:32.575 --> 02:37:35.908
and you might experience chronic disease

2876
02:37:36.932 --> 02:37:40.986
in your 40s or 50s or 60s, but by and large,

2877
02:37:40.986 --> 02:37:45.491
the lifestyle that you were leading 10, 20, 30 years ago

2878
02:37:45.491 --> 02:37:48.377
has had a lot to do with the development

2879
02:37:48.377 --> 02:37:50.265
of that chronic disease.

2880
02:37:50.265 --> 02:37:53.294
You spoke to the soldiers from the Korean War,

2881
02:37:53.294 --> 02:37:54.216
they were young,

2882
02:37:54.216 --> 02:37:59.121
they were laying down the pathology of atherosclerosis.

2883
02:37:59.121 --> 02:38:04.121
So, my fear is this sort of short-term band-aid solution

2884
02:38:04.282 --> 02:38:09.130
and my biggest fear with it is that it sends LDL cholesterol

2885
02:38:09.130 --> 02:38:10.547
through the roof.

2886
02:38:11.407 --> 02:38:13.800
I said before 100 milligrams per deciliter

2887
02:38:13.800 --> 02:38:17.838
is like it's considered the kind of recommendation

2888
02:38:17.838 --> 02:38:20.521
here in America and in Australia.

2889
02:38:20.521 --> 02:38:22.434
But as I said, really you got to get down to 60

2890
02:38:22.434 --> 02:38:24.144
or 70 milligrams per deciliter

2891
02:38:24.144 --> 02:38:27.790
where you don't see atherosclerosis.

2892
02:38:27.790 --> 02:38:32.207
And here's the really, really interesting thing Rich.

2893
02:38:34.496 --> 02:38:38.204
Loren Cordain, he wrote "The Paleo Diet" book.

2894
02:38:38.204 --> 02:38:40.834
He also published a paper

2895
02:38:40.834 --> 02:38:45.417
that goes into what is a healthy LDL cholesterol level,

2896
02:38:46.773 --> 02:38:49.817
this is coming from the father of the Paleo diet.

2897
02:38:49.817 --> 02:38:51.178
And he went through

2898
02:38:51.178 --> 02:38:53.849
and he looked at LDL cholesterols of primates

2899
02:38:53.849 --> 02:38:57.070
and of hunter, gatherer tribes

2900
02:38:57.070 --> 02:39:01.248
where there is low incidence of atherosclerosis

2901
02:39:01.248 --> 02:39:04.489
and his conclusion in this paper that he wrote,

2902
02:39:04.489 --> 02:39:06.628
the father of the Paleo diet

2903
02:39:06.628 --> 02:39:11.359
was that human LDL cholesterol needs to be at 50

2904
02:39:11.359 --> 02:39:13.731
to 70 milligrams per deciliter

2905
02:39:13.731 --> 02:39:16.310
in order to avoid atherosclerosis.

2906
02:39:16.310 --> 02:39:18.435
So, this is coming from him.

2907
02:39:18.435 --> 02:39:21.662
And you might think, "Well, hang on,

2908
02:39:21.662 --> 02:39:23.492
he's advocating for the Paleo diet,

2909
02:39:23.492 --> 02:39:25.792
then how can he be also saying

2910
02:39:25.792 --> 02:39:28.217
that LDL cholesterol should be low?"

2911
02:39:28.217 --> 02:39:31.806
And a lot of this comes back down to what the Paleo diet is

2912
02:39:31.806 --> 02:39:35.018
versus how people are doing it and so--

2913
02:39:35.018 --> 02:39:36.117
That's a very good point

2914
02:39:36.117 --> 02:39:39.235
because it's not exactly the way

2915
02:39:39.235 --> 02:39:41.585
that it's been massively adopted.

2916
02:39:41.585 --> 02:39:43.128
That's right and the Paleo diet

2917
02:39:43.128 --> 02:39:46.634
if you look at how Loren Cordain...

2918
02:39:46.634 --> 02:39:48.217
What he recommends,

2919
02:39:50.157 --> 02:39:54.148
it's talking about meats that are very low in saturated fat.

2920
02:39:54.148 --> 02:39:56.681
If you went back and looked in the Paleolithic era

2921
02:39:56.681 --> 02:39:58.288
and you look at woolly mammoth

2922
02:39:58.288 --> 02:40:02.427
or these types of meats that would have been eaten then,

2923
02:40:02.427 --> 02:40:05.167
they are more equivalent to venison in antelope,

2924
02:40:05.167 --> 02:40:08.107
which are very low in saturated fat

2925
02:40:08.107 --> 02:40:11.147
and higher in polyunsaturated fats.

2926
02:40:11.147 --> 02:40:12.760
That's actually ironic in itself

2927
02:40:12.760 --> 02:40:17.593
because those people that are blaming polyunsaturated fats

2928
02:40:19.193 --> 02:40:23.091
in said oils are overlooking the fact that meat

2929
02:40:23.091 --> 02:40:25.144
from the Paleolithic era was very rich

2930
02:40:25.144 --> 02:40:27.144
in polyunsaturated fats.

2931
02:40:28.188 --> 02:40:30.438
But overall, Loren Cordain,

2932
02:40:32.567 --> 02:40:35.359
his recommendations is for a diet

2933
02:40:35.359 --> 02:40:36.901
that's low in saturated fat.

2934
02:40:36.901 --> 02:40:41.657
Even though he speaks about the inclusion of animal foods,

2935
02:40:41.657 --> 02:40:43.697
he talks about that being more consistent

2936
02:40:43.697 --> 02:40:47.112
with the quality of meat that people were eating back then

2937
02:40:47.112 --> 02:40:51.010
and he talks about the importance of a very high fiber diet.

2938
02:40:51.010 --> 02:40:52.724
In fact he mentions that many of these people

2939
02:40:52.724 --> 02:40:56.535
were eating over 100 grams of fiber a day.

2940
02:40:56.535 --> 02:41:01.368
So, I think that's very important for people to understand

2941
02:41:02.557 --> 02:41:05.577
that the Paleo diet was not developed

2942
02:41:05.577 --> 02:41:09.983
as this high saturated fat, animal-rich diet.

2943
02:41:09.983 --> 02:41:12.962
But the carnivore diet is something altogether different,

2944
02:41:12.962 --> 02:41:14.220
it has its roots

2945
02:41:14.220 --> 02:41:18.176
in this kind of ancestral ideology I suppose,

2946
02:41:18.176 --> 02:41:23.093
but part and parcel of the philosophy of the carnivore diet

2947
02:41:25.044 --> 02:41:29.961
is that saturated fat is your friend, we talked about that,

2948
02:41:30.956 --> 02:41:33.721
but also these high LDL markers

2949
02:41:33.721 --> 02:41:36.195
are something that you should not concern yourself with

2950
02:41:36.195 --> 02:41:37.387
because you see a lot of that

2951
02:41:37.387 --> 02:41:39.767
because people are having these elevated numbers,

2952
02:41:39.767 --> 02:41:43.783
including Shawn Baker who shared it on Rogan et cetera

2953
02:41:43.783 --> 02:41:45.338
and in response, they just say,

2954
02:41:45.338 --> 02:41:48.505
"Well, that high number isn't relevant

2955
02:41:49.570 --> 02:41:51.903
in the traditional way that we've considered it

2956
02:41:51.903 --> 02:41:52.827
in the past.

2957
02:41:52.827 --> 02:41:55.988
Because I'm doing my diet in this way,

2958
02:41:55.988 --> 02:41:57.738
it's somehow buffered

2959
02:41:58.795 --> 02:42:01.203
or ameliorated by dint of the fact

2960
02:42:01.203 --> 02:42:04.188
that I'm only eating meat," so help me understand

2961
02:42:04.188 --> 02:42:06.596
at least like if you were them,

2962
02:42:06.596 --> 02:42:08.785
like how you would articulate that

2963
02:42:08.785 --> 02:42:11.952
and then let's kind of deconstruct it.

2964
02:42:13.271 --> 02:42:16.236
So, I mentioned before that saturated fat,

2965
02:42:16.236 --> 02:42:18.049
increasing LDL cholesterol,

2966
02:42:18.049 --> 02:42:21.585
increasing risk of atherosclerosis

2967
02:42:21.585 --> 02:42:24.357
and coronary heart disease

2968
02:42:24.357 --> 02:42:27.951
is the most substantiated relationship in science,

2969
02:42:27.951 --> 02:42:31.805
in nutrition science today, without a doubt.

2970
02:42:31.805 --> 02:42:34.138
And so much so that in 2017,

2971
02:42:35.822 --> 02:42:38.443
a cardiologist by the name of Brian Ferentz,

2972
02:42:38.443 --> 02:42:40.971
he co-authored a paper,

2973
02:42:40.971 --> 02:42:45.174
which very clearly states that LDL is causal

2974
02:42:45.174 --> 02:42:49.674
in the development and progression of atherosclerosis.

2975
02:42:51.034 --> 02:42:53.879
There are some very interesting things in that paper

2976
02:42:53.879 --> 02:42:57.631
that he walks through and let's quickly go through those.

2977
02:42:57.631 --> 02:43:00.214
There is some genetic mutations

2978
02:43:01.529 --> 02:43:06.029
that see people with genetically high LDL cholesterol,

2979
02:43:09.403 --> 02:43:13.488
heterozygous, familial hyper, cholesterolemia

2980
02:43:13.488 --> 02:43:14.738
and homozygous,

2981
02:43:17.601 --> 02:43:20.526
hetero if you inherit one of those genes from one parent

2982
02:43:20.526 --> 02:43:23.152
and homo if you inherit both,

2983
02:43:23.152 --> 02:43:25.684
the latter unfortunately leading

2984
02:43:25.684 --> 02:43:28.626
to much more rapid development of disease.

2985
02:43:28.626 --> 02:43:32.126
But if you have FH as it's called in short

2986
02:43:34.647 --> 02:43:37.176
particularly if it's the homozygous form,

2987
02:43:37.176 --> 02:43:38.390
if you're dealt that genetic code

2988
02:43:38.390 --> 02:43:41.458
and you have high LDL cholesterol from birth,

2989
02:43:41.458 --> 02:43:43.586
these are people that have LDL cholesterol

2990
02:43:43.586 --> 02:43:45.336
between 500 and 1000,

2991
02:43:46.385 --> 02:43:48.034
which is where we're seeing people

2992
02:43:48.034 --> 02:43:50.036
in the carnivore community.

2993
02:43:50.036 --> 02:43:50.869
If you are--

2994
02:43:50.869 --> 02:43:54.786
Just literally five to 10 to 15 times higher.

2995
02:43:55.636 --> 02:43:57.993
I think Paul Saladino is just 700.

2996
02:43:57.993 --> 02:44:01.198
700, where Cordain is saying--

2997
02:44:01.198 --> 02:44:03.787
50 to 70.
50 to 70.

2998
02:44:03.787 --> 02:44:06.870
And these people that inherit this,

2999
02:44:09.218 --> 02:44:11.877
if they're not treated with statins,

3000
02:44:11.877 --> 02:44:13.824
it's very, very common for them

3001
02:44:13.824 --> 02:44:17.652
to develop cardiovascular disease in their teenage years

3002
02:44:17.652 --> 02:44:19.985
and die in their 20s or 30s.

3003
02:44:21.553 --> 02:44:24.429
And that's obviously very, very sad today.

3004
02:44:24.429 --> 02:44:27.705
With the progression of statins,

3005
02:44:27.705 --> 02:44:31.440
these people are living much longer.

3006
02:44:31.440 --> 02:44:35.549
So, that's the first thing that I think is interesting.

3007
02:44:35.549 --> 02:44:39.485
The second is that there is a type of science

3008
02:44:39.485 --> 02:44:43.317
we didn't discuss it before called Mendelian randomization.

3009
02:44:43.317 --> 02:44:48.150
And this is science that really has become I guess popular

3010
02:44:51.112 --> 02:44:54.974
or more commonly used in the last five to 10 years.

3011
02:44:54.974 --> 02:44:57.095
And thanks to improvements

3012
02:44:57.095 --> 02:45:00.104
in our understanding of genetics and--

3013
02:45:00.104 --> 02:45:03.131
Mendelian in the Gregor Mendel sense?

3014
02:45:03.131 --> 02:45:03.964
I'm not sure--

3015
02:45:03.964 --> 02:45:04.889
Do you know Gregory Mendel?

3016
02:45:04.889 --> 02:45:05.996
I'm not sure of the origin.

3017
02:45:05.996 --> 02:45:08.075
Like the original guy doing plant hybrids

3018
02:45:08.075 --> 02:45:10.158
and working in genetics?

3019
02:45:10.158 --> 02:45:14.108
Well, it could well be, I'm not sure of the origin,

3020
02:45:14.108 --> 02:45:16.679
but what it speaks to is

3021
02:45:16.679 --> 02:45:20.043
these studies are like nature's randomized controlled trial.

3022
02:45:20.043 --> 02:45:22.716
There are over 50 genetic mutations,

3023
02:45:22.716 --> 02:45:25.049
which lower LDL cholesterol.

3024
02:45:26.470 --> 02:45:28.887
And if you're dealt that card

3025
02:45:29.856 --> 02:45:32.443
and you have lower LDL cholesterol,

3026
02:45:32.443 --> 02:45:34.228
you have significantly lower risk

3027
02:45:34.228 --> 02:45:37.220
of developing coronary heart disease through your life.

3028
02:45:37.220 --> 02:45:39.637
And because this is occurring

3029
02:45:42.102 --> 02:45:45.847
as you're being made by your mother,

3030
02:45:45.847 --> 02:45:48.720
it is nature's randomized controlled trial.

3031
02:45:48.720 --> 02:45:52.214
And so we can see very clearly...

3032
02:45:52.214 --> 02:45:54.193
What's really interesting about this is that

3033
02:45:54.193 --> 02:45:57.276
these single nucleotide polymorphisms

3034
02:45:58.852 --> 02:46:02.496
is what they're called, there's 50 of these or more

3035
02:46:02.496 --> 02:46:07.496
is that they all lower LDL cholesterol by different amounts.

3036
02:46:08.780 --> 02:46:13.530
But if you are to standardize how much they lower LDL by,

3037
02:46:15.015 --> 02:46:18.284
they all have the same order of effect

3038
02:46:18.284 --> 02:46:20.158
on coronary heart disease,

3039
02:46:20.158 --> 02:46:23.460
which means that it's highly likely

3040
02:46:23.460 --> 02:46:25.710
that this is a causal link.

3041
02:46:27.309 --> 02:46:31.427
We're talking about true cause and effect.

3042
02:46:31.427 --> 02:46:34.705
Between elevated LDL and atherosclerosis?

3043
02:46:34.705 --> 02:46:38.373
Yes and it's a linear relationship.

3044
02:46:38.373 --> 02:46:41.480
The lower you get LDL cholesterol down

3045
02:46:41.480 --> 02:46:44.816
based on whichever genetic variant someone has,

3046
02:46:44.816 --> 02:46:48.079
the lower their risk of coronary heart disease.

3047
02:46:48.079 --> 02:46:51.079
Now, to add more evidence onto that,

3048
02:46:54.718 --> 02:46:58.682
then we have lots and lots of statin trials.

3049
02:46:58.682 --> 02:47:02.682
And there's some anti-statin rhetoric out there,

3050
02:47:03.537 --> 02:47:06.370
but it's so clear in these studies

3051
02:47:09.551 --> 02:47:12.468
that when you lower LDL cholesterol

3052
02:47:13.670 --> 02:47:17.078
through the use of either a statin, ezetimibe

3053
02:47:17.078 --> 02:47:20.911
or PCSK9 inhibitors, these are different drugs

3054
02:47:22.849 --> 02:47:26.660
acting on different pathways that all lower LDL cholesterol.

3055
02:47:26.660 --> 02:47:29.550
No matter which one you look at,

3056
02:47:29.550 --> 02:47:33.713
they all significantly lower coronary heart disease.

3057
02:47:33.713 --> 02:47:36.881
And once you get someone's LDL cholesterol down

3058
02:47:36.881 --> 02:47:39.588
to about 70 milligrams per deciliter,

3059
02:47:39.588 --> 02:47:43.160
which is 1.8 millimoles per liter with statins

3060
02:47:43.160 --> 02:47:45.804
or with PCSK9 inhibitors,

3061
02:47:45.804 --> 02:47:50.221
that's where the progression of atherosclerosis stops

3062
02:47:51.179 --> 02:47:54.280
and you can start to see some regression.

3063
02:47:54.280 --> 02:47:56.947
So, we have evidence from people

3064
02:47:58.265 --> 02:48:01.991
who have genetically high LDL cholesterol,

3065
02:48:01.991 --> 02:48:03.850
these people if they're untreated

3066
02:48:03.850 --> 02:48:07.936
will develop atherosclerosis in their teens,

3067
02:48:07.936 --> 02:48:12.079
we have evidence from people who have genetically low LDL,

3068
02:48:12.079 --> 02:48:14.601
they're significantly protected against heart disease,

3069
02:48:14.601 --> 02:48:16.883
we have the statin trials,

3070
02:48:16.883 --> 02:48:20.950
which you see as they lower LDL cholesterol,

3071
02:48:20.950 --> 02:48:24.389
you drive coronary heart disease risk down

3072
02:48:24.389 --> 02:48:25.222
and then on top of that,

3073
02:48:25.222 --> 02:48:28.151
we have all of the observational research.

3074
02:48:28.151 --> 02:48:30.258
There's a meta analysis,

3075
02:48:30.258 --> 02:48:32.506
the prospective studies collaboration

3076
02:48:32.506 --> 02:48:35.222
that included 50 plus studies,

3077
02:48:35.222 --> 02:48:39.384
12 million plus person follow up in that study

3078
02:48:39.384 --> 02:48:43.387
and it clearly shows in these cohort populations

3079
02:48:43.387 --> 02:48:46.552
lower LDL cholesterol lower risk of coronary heart disease.

3080
02:48:46.552 --> 02:48:51.510
So, when Paul Saladino with his insanely high LDL says,

3081
02:48:51.510 --> 02:48:53.057
"Don't sweat it, don't worry about it.

3082
02:48:53.057 --> 02:48:57.448
You don't need to concern yourself with this."

3083
02:48:57.448 --> 02:49:01.198
How is he scientifically substantiating that?

3084
02:49:03.403 --> 02:49:04.861
I don't think he is.

3085
02:49:04.861 --> 02:49:08.028
I think that it's inconvenient for him

3086
02:49:11.872 --> 02:49:15.026
that LDL has been shown to be causal

3087
02:49:15.026 --> 02:49:17.406
in the development of heart disease

3088
02:49:17.406 --> 02:49:22.406
and I've never seen him be able to substantiate that

3089
02:49:22.434 --> 02:49:23.874
other than the fact he will say that

3090
02:49:23.874 --> 02:49:27.073
his coronary artery calcium score is zero.

3091
02:49:27.073 --> 02:49:27.906
But we know

3092
02:49:27.906 --> 02:49:30.734
that people with a coronary artery calcium score of zero

3093
02:49:30.734 --> 02:49:32.436
can still have atherosclerosis

3094
02:49:32.436 --> 02:49:34.967
and can still suffer from a heart attack.

3095
02:49:34.967 --> 02:49:38.300
So, for me the evidence is overwhelming.

3096
02:49:39.584 --> 02:49:42.056
The lower your LDL cholesterol

3097
02:49:42.056 --> 02:49:44.763
and the more years you're exposed

3098
02:49:44.763 --> 02:49:49.638
to that low level of LDL cholesterol, the better.

3099
02:49:49.638 --> 02:49:51.438
Enough said on that.

3100
02:49:51.438 --> 02:49:55.656
I wanna switch gears and explore a couple other things,

3101
02:49:55.656 --> 02:49:58.153
we've been going on for a while here.

3102
02:49:58.153 --> 02:50:03.052
Obviously there are different motivations and on-ramps

3103
02:50:03.052 --> 02:50:06.526
for people who are interested in a plant-based diet

3104
02:50:06.526 --> 02:50:09.681
or becoming more plant-predominant in their lifestyle,

3105
02:50:09.681 --> 02:50:12.189
we've explored the human health piece

3106
02:50:12.189 --> 02:50:13.228
quite extensively today,

3107
02:50:13.228 --> 02:50:16.656
but of course there are other motivators,

3108
02:50:16.656 --> 02:50:19.242
the ethics of our food choices

3109
02:50:19.242 --> 02:50:23.483
and how that implicates planetary health more largely,

3110
02:50:23.483 --> 02:50:28.400
which of course also brings up the issue of the environment

3111
02:50:29.469 --> 02:50:31.895
and the health implications planetarily

3112
02:50:31.895 --> 02:50:33.257
for the choices that we're making

3113
02:50:33.257 --> 02:50:34.921
about the foods that we eat

3114
02:50:34.921 --> 02:50:39.151
and the food products that we choose to purchase

3115
02:50:39.151 --> 02:50:40.567
with our buying power.

3116
02:50:40.567 --> 02:50:43.817
So, let's talk a little bit about that.

3117
02:50:46.963 --> 02:50:48.536
Maybe start more broadly

3118
02:50:48.536 --> 02:50:53.130
with the idea of how these dietary choices are affecting,

3119
02:50:53.130 --> 02:50:56.273
like we're in a climate crisis right now.

3120
02:50:56.273 --> 02:50:58.835
It's incumbent upon all of us to take greater responsibility

3121
02:50:58.835 --> 02:51:01.529
for the choices that we make daily.

3122
02:51:01.529 --> 02:51:05.693
How does the plant-based diet figure into this equation

3123
02:51:05.693 --> 02:51:08.943
of being more ecologically responsible?

3124
02:51:09.947 --> 02:51:14.530
Well, we've turned this place into a giant farm and--

3125
02:51:16.251 --> 02:51:19.336
That's my second favorite quote of yours by the way.

3126
02:51:19.336 --> 02:51:24.169
And that's had huge effects on greenhouse gas emissions.

3127
02:51:28.753 --> 02:51:30.463
Currently agriculture is responsible

3128
02:51:30.463 --> 02:51:35.180
for 25% of the world's greenhouse gas emissions.

3129
02:51:35.180 --> 02:51:36.672
It's had a huge effects

3130
02:51:36.672 --> 02:51:39.922
on biodiversity loss and deforestation.

3131
02:51:40.991 --> 02:51:43.799
Agriculture is the number one cause of deforestation

3132
02:51:43.799 --> 02:51:45.136
throughout the world

3133
02:51:45.136 --> 02:51:49.053
and with that we're losing these precious parts

3134
02:51:51.189 --> 02:51:53.465
of the natural world that really are our ally

3135
02:51:53.465 --> 02:51:55.715
in fighting climate change.

3136
02:51:57.117 --> 02:52:01.117
And it's the single greatest user of freshwater.

3137
02:52:03.857 --> 02:52:07.857
Agriculture is resulting in ocean acidification,

3138
02:52:11.015 --> 02:52:15.932
it's resulting in the eutrophication and hypoxic conditions

3139
02:52:17.268 --> 02:52:22.101
in certain bodies of water that result in the algal blooms

3140
02:52:23.111 --> 02:52:26.944
and overall if you are to look at agriculture,

3141
02:52:31.892 --> 02:52:36.278
I think one of the most powerful statistics is

3142
02:52:36.278 --> 02:52:39.660
50% of the habitable land on earth

3143
02:52:39.660 --> 02:52:42.160
is used today for agriculture.

3144
02:52:43.317 --> 02:52:48.317
And of that, 83% of that is used for animal agriculture

3145
02:52:50.536 --> 02:52:55.243
yet it only returns us with 18% of our calories

3146
02:52:55.243 --> 02:52:58.163
and is responsible for two thirds

3147
02:52:58.163 --> 02:53:00.043
of agricultural-related emissions.

3148
02:53:00.043 --> 02:53:02.611
Yeah, so purely from an academic perspective,

3149
02:53:02.611 --> 02:53:06.181
this is a highly inefficient farm that we're running

3150
02:53:06.181 --> 02:53:07.541
if the planet is indeed a farm.

3151
02:53:07.541 --> 02:53:11.958
Yeah, so we have kind of I guess two parts to this,

3152
02:53:13.429 --> 02:53:16.849
the factory farming of animals is very inefficient

3153
02:53:16.849 --> 02:53:20.892
because it requires large amounts of land to grow crops

3154
02:53:20.892 --> 02:53:24.475
and the feed conversion ratio is not great.

3155
02:53:29.557 --> 02:53:32.891
To produce animal products, you have to grow these animals.

3156
02:53:32.891 --> 02:53:34.211
And the larger the animal,

3157
02:53:34.211 --> 02:53:37.135
the higher the basal metabolic rate.

3158
02:53:37.135 --> 02:53:41.258
It's the energy that that animal's burning just to be alive.

3159
02:53:41.258 --> 02:53:42.652
And so with that,

3160
02:53:42.652 --> 02:53:44.867
what happens is we lose a lot of the calories

3161
02:53:44.867 --> 02:53:46.965
that we feed in to the system.

3162
02:53:46.965 --> 02:53:51.953
So, if you use like a combustion engine as an analogy,

3163
02:53:51.953 --> 02:53:53.800
you put gasoline in

3164
02:53:53.800 --> 02:53:57.597
and not all of that energy gets converted

3165
02:53:57.597 --> 02:53:59.049
into forward motion of the car,

3166
02:53:59.049 --> 02:54:02.639
some of it is cast off by heat, et cetera.

3167
02:54:02.639 --> 02:54:06.028
Similarly, when we're taking all of these crops,

3168
02:54:06.028 --> 02:54:07.729
we're feeding them to animals,

3169
02:54:07.729 --> 02:54:11.285
not all of that is converted into potential energy

3170
02:54:11.285 --> 02:54:13.505
that's consumed by humans, a lot of it is lost

3171
02:54:13.505 --> 02:54:15.024
like it's just highly high...

3172
02:54:15.024 --> 02:54:17.803
If you're an alien who came to earth and and said,

3173
02:54:17.803 --> 02:54:19.228
"Show me how you make your food"

3174
02:54:19.228 --> 02:54:23.294
and we showed them, they would say this is total insanity.

3175
02:54:23.294 --> 02:54:26.057
And food waste is important

3176
02:54:26.057 --> 02:54:29.062
and 78% of the world's calories are wasted

3177
02:54:29.062 --> 02:54:31.347
and we absolutely have to focus on that

3178
02:54:31.347 --> 02:54:34.348
because that's calories that could be ending up in bellies

3179
02:54:34.348 --> 02:54:38.281
and it's also a big contributor to greenhouse gas emissions.

3180
02:54:38.281 --> 02:54:43.281
But what's overlooked is that the biggest area of food waste

3181
02:54:43.479 --> 02:54:46.726
is within animal agriculture, it's the waste of the calories

3182
02:54:46.726 --> 02:54:49.848
that we're feeding into this inefficient system.

3183
02:54:49.848 --> 02:54:53.569
And to give you an idea of the efficiency

3184
02:54:53.569 --> 02:54:58.569
or lack of efficiency, if you feed 100 calories into a cow,

3185
02:54:59.277 --> 02:55:01.776
you'll get 40 calories of milk out the other side.

3186
02:55:01.776 --> 02:55:03.692
So, you'll lose 60%.

3187
02:55:03.692 --> 02:55:07.025
If you feed 100 calories into a chicken,

3188
02:55:09.070 --> 02:55:12.955
you'll get 20 calories of eggs out the other side.

3189
02:55:12.955 --> 02:55:14.792
So, you're losing 80%.

3190
02:55:14.792 --> 02:55:18.875
If you feed 100 calories into a pig or a chicken,

3191
02:55:19.967 --> 02:55:23.159
you'll get about 10 calories of meat out the other side.

3192
02:55:23.159 --> 02:55:25.909
So, in that example you can think

3193
02:55:29.079 --> 02:55:30.996
for one chicken burger,

3194
02:55:32.806 --> 02:55:36.690
you could essentially make 10 black bean burgers.

3195
02:55:36.690 --> 02:55:38.559
That's the difference we're talking about here

3196
02:55:38.559 --> 02:55:42.392
in terms of efficiency and what we're wasting.

3197
02:55:43.602 --> 02:55:47.859
And then beef, red meat is the the least efficient of all.

3198
02:55:47.859 --> 02:55:50.046
You feed 100 calories into a cow

3199
02:55:50.046 --> 02:55:54.815
and you get three calories of meat out the other side.

3200
02:55:54.815 --> 02:55:58.482
Now, often when I speak to this, people say,

3201
02:55:59.527 --> 02:56:01.700
"Why on the grass-fed beef

3202
02:56:01.700 --> 02:56:05.485
and they're not feeding them feed crops,

3203
02:56:05.485 --> 02:56:07.822
they're out grazing?"

3204
02:56:07.822 --> 02:56:09.316
And that's true,

3205
02:56:09.316 --> 02:56:12.663
but the biggest problem with grazing

3206
02:56:12.663 --> 02:56:16.179
is the amount of land that it's using.

3207
02:56:16.179 --> 02:56:19.929
And in order to make space for that industry,

3208
02:56:23.360 --> 02:56:25.724
which provides very few calories,

3209
02:56:25.724 --> 02:56:28.898
we have to clear a lot of land.

3210
02:56:28.898 --> 02:56:33.733
And there's a really interesting paper from 2018,

3211
02:56:33.733 --> 02:56:36.722
Tim searching app that has gone back

3212
02:56:36.722 --> 02:56:38.666
and looked at today's grasslands

3213
02:56:38.666 --> 02:56:43.445
and at least 41% of them were forests or woody savanna

3214
02:56:43.445 --> 02:56:44.862
not too long ago.

3215
02:56:46.091 --> 02:56:49.591
And globally right now, still to this day,

3216
02:56:51.150 --> 02:56:55.977
the number one cause of deforestation is animal agriculture.

3217
02:56:55.977 --> 02:56:59.142
So, I could do a four-hour podcast

3218
02:56:59.142 --> 02:57:03.157
on the environmental implications of animal agriculture

3219
02:57:03.157 --> 02:57:06.498
and the impact of a pivot to a plant-centric diet

3220
02:57:06.498 --> 02:57:09.331
in terms of the ecological impact.

3221
02:57:10.298 --> 02:57:11.279
We don't have four hours,

3222
02:57:11.279 --> 02:57:14.840
so I wanna focus our remaining time

3223
02:57:14.840 --> 02:57:19.090
on the conversation around regenerative agriculture

3224
02:57:20.297 --> 02:57:23.030
because I think there's a lot of confusion here.

3225
02:57:23.030 --> 02:57:27.094
I've hosted people from this arena on the podcast,

3226
02:57:27.094 --> 02:57:29.338
we're both friends with Rylan Engelhardt

3227
02:57:29.338 --> 02:57:31.393
who's behind the Kiss the Ground Movement

3228
02:57:31.393 --> 02:57:33.611
and the Cafe Gratitude restaurants,

3229
02:57:33.611 --> 02:57:35.181
I've had John and Molly Chester

3230
02:57:35.181 --> 02:57:37.832
from "Biggest Little Farm" on here,

3231
02:57:37.832 --> 02:57:39.749
I have a lot of respect

3232
02:57:40.650 --> 02:57:44.356
for people who have devoted their time and energy

3233
02:57:44.356 --> 02:57:46.512
towards the regeneration of our soils

3234
02:57:46.512 --> 02:57:47.798
because I think this is crucial.

3235
02:57:47.798 --> 02:57:51.673
It's a crucial piece in redressing climate change

3236
02:57:51.673 --> 02:57:54.816
and pivoting from a consumptive society

3237
02:57:54.816 --> 02:57:56.599
into a regenerative one,

3238
02:57:56.599 --> 02:57:59.485
but I also think there's a lot of misunderstanding

3239
02:57:59.485 --> 02:58:02.068
as I said and also greenwashing

3240
02:58:03.010 --> 02:58:05.529
when it comes to how we're understanding

3241
02:58:05.529 --> 02:58:07.381
what exactly it is that we're talking about

3242
02:58:07.381 --> 02:58:10.107
with respect to regenerative agriculture.

3243
02:58:10.107 --> 02:58:12.016
I think there's a lot of well-intentioned,

3244
02:58:12.016 --> 02:58:14.804
but perhaps misconceived narratives about this,

3245
02:58:14.804 --> 02:58:17.802
so I wanna parse truth from fiction

3246
02:58:17.802 --> 02:58:22.802
when it comes to what we're talking about with Region Ag,

3247
02:58:22.973 --> 02:58:25.058
what exactly can be accomplished,

3248
02:58:25.058 --> 02:58:27.124
what is getting overlooked

3249
02:58:27.124 --> 02:58:29.749
and what the kind of inherent flaws are

3250
02:58:29.749 --> 02:58:32.458
when it comes to the claims being made

3251
02:58:32.458 --> 02:58:36.625
by a holistic land management cohort of advocates.

3252
02:58:39.198 --> 02:58:41.602
Gosh, a big question.

3253
02:58:41.602 --> 02:58:43.685
So, I am a huge proponent

3254
02:58:45.166 --> 02:58:47.796
of regenerative agriculture overall,

3255
02:58:47.796 --> 02:58:49.596
I think it makes a lot of sense.

3256
02:58:49.596 --> 02:58:54.179
I think that we certainly need to consider soil health.

3257
02:58:56.017 --> 02:58:59.184
I think definitions are important here

3258
02:59:00.400 --> 02:59:04.603
and also looking at what the science says

3259
02:59:04.603 --> 02:59:07.759
because it is an area like the dire wars

3260
02:59:07.759 --> 02:59:12.259
where emotion can affect the interpretation of science

3261
02:59:13.605 --> 02:59:18.155
and so I think there's a little bit of science

3262
02:59:18.155 --> 02:59:20.405
to go through here as well.

3263
02:59:21.287 --> 02:59:25.204
Regarding definitions, regenerative agriculture

3264
02:59:26.977 --> 02:59:28.810
is really nothing new.

3265
02:59:29.656 --> 02:59:32.825
A lot of the practices, be it poly cropping

3266
02:59:32.825 --> 02:59:36.742
or intercropping or cover cropping, these are--

3267
02:59:39.337 --> 02:59:41.444
Those are ancestral practices,

3268
02:59:41.444 --> 02:59:43.440
what is new is industrialized farming.

3269
02:59:43.440 --> 02:59:47.588
That's right and so a lot of regenerative agriculture

3270
02:59:47.588 --> 02:59:51.436
I guess in some ways it's become a bit of a buzz word,

3271
02:59:51.436 --> 02:59:52.628
but I do think it's important

3272
02:59:52.628 --> 02:59:54.521
for people to understand the roots

3273
02:59:54.521 --> 02:59:56.311
of many of these practices

3274
02:59:56.311 --> 02:59:59.561
are from First Nations' land management

3275
03:00:01.357 --> 03:00:04.357
and regenerative agriculture overall

3276
03:00:07.231 --> 03:00:10.565
is not the same as regenerative beef,

3277
03:00:10.565 --> 03:00:14.061
regenerative beef is one practice, this holistic grazing

3278
03:00:14.061 --> 03:00:15.854
within a whole plethora

3279
03:00:15.854 --> 03:00:19.104
of regenerative agricultural practices.

3280
03:00:19.945 --> 03:00:24.528
And so there have been many, many First Nations' people

3281
03:00:25.590 --> 03:00:29.750
around the world that have improved soil

3282
03:00:29.750 --> 03:00:32.412
without animal integration using poly cropping,

3283
03:00:32.412 --> 03:00:36.330
cover cropping, green manure, et cetera.

3284
03:00:36.330 --> 03:00:38.295
So, I think that's an important point

3285
03:00:38.295 --> 03:00:39.688
because I think sometimes when people think

3286
03:00:39.688 --> 03:00:40.849
of regenerative agriculture,

3287
03:00:40.849 --> 03:00:45.046
they immediately think of regenerative grazing.

3288
03:00:45.046 --> 03:00:48.541
I don't think that there's much debate at all

3289
03:00:48.541 --> 03:00:52.015
about poly cropping and intercropping and cover cropping,

3290
03:00:52.015 --> 03:00:54.637
I think that's generally accepted across the board

3291
03:00:54.637 --> 03:00:56.795
that those practices are much better

3292
03:00:56.795 --> 03:01:00.506
for promoting biodiversity and improving soil health.

3293
03:01:00.506 --> 03:01:01.832
I think where--

3294
03:01:01.832 --> 03:01:03.300
And sequestration.

3295
03:01:03.300 --> 03:01:05.473
And sequestration, right.

3296
03:01:05.473 --> 03:01:08.422
I think where there is dispute

3297
03:01:08.422 --> 03:01:11.032
is around regenerative grazing.

3298
03:01:11.032 --> 03:01:15.282
And I believe and I've had enough people on my show

3299
03:01:16.463 --> 03:01:19.258
to talk about this as well from both sides--

3300
03:01:19.258 --> 03:01:22.254
I should point out so I don't forget,

3301
03:01:22.254 --> 03:01:25.947
you've done a number of podcasts with Nicholas Carter,

3302
03:01:25.947 --> 03:01:27.124
I don't know, three or four with him,

3303
03:01:27.124 --> 03:01:28.731
I mean, those are fantastic.

3304
03:01:28.731 --> 03:01:29.564
So, we're not gonna be able

3305
03:01:29.564 --> 03:01:32.359
to get into the super nitty-gritty of all these,

3306
03:01:32.359 --> 03:01:37.288
but everybody should go listen to Simon's conversations

3307
03:01:37.288 --> 03:01:39.298
with Nicolas on his Plant Proof Podcast

3308
03:01:39.298 --> 03:01:40.619
because it's very--

3309
03:01:40.619 --> 03:01:42.322
You should have him on,

3310
03:01:42.322 --> 03:01:46.822
he's an incredible environmental researcher scientist,

3311
03:01:49.263 --> 03:01:52.930
but I guess big claims require big evidence.

3312
03:01:55.425 --> 03:01:58.425
And there have been some wild claims

3313
03:01:59.566 --> 03:02:01.562
made about regenerative grazing over the years,

3314
03:02:01.562 --> 03:02:03.729
Allan Savory most notably,

3315
03:02:04.745 --> 03:02:08.502
he's well known for a YouTube TED Talk

3316
03:02:08.502 --> 03:02:13.169
and he said this was the key to reversing climate change

3317
03:02:14.071 --> 03:02:18.159
and his idea is that he believes through his practices

3318
03:02:18.159 --> 03:02:22.403
that you can sequester an enormous amount of carbon.

3319
03:02:22.403 --> 03:02:24.570
And that would be amazing.

3320
03:02:25.635 --> 03:02:30.219
You imagine we could draw down all of this carbon

3321
03:02:30.219 --> 03:02:33.621
that has been emitted since the Industrial Revolution,

3322
03:02:33.621 --> 03:02:37.263
get it into our soils and cool the planet.

3323
03:02:37.263 --> 03:02:39.510
That is a great story.

3324
03:02:39.510 --> 03:02:42.487
Now, where does the science lie?

3325
03:02:42.487 --> 03:02:46.434
That's ultimately what we should be most concerned with.

3326
03:02:46.434 --> 03:02:50.851
And the science doesn't stack up with these practices

3327
03:02:53.325 --> 03:02:56.742
being a net carbon sequestering practice.

3328
03:02:57.680 --> 03:02:58.884
So, it's true

3329
03:02:58.884 --> 03:03:03.458
that you can sequester some carbon, absolutely.

3330
03:03:03.458 --> 03:03:07.041
The problem is that when you are accounting

3331
03:03:10.259 --> 03:03:15.009
for methane emissions and nitrous oxide from the animals,

3332
03:03:16.496 --> 03:03:20.442
the net position is one of greenhouse gas emissions.

3333
03:03:20.442 --> 03:03:25.442
And there was a big report done out of Oxford University

3334
03:03:25.545 --> 03:03:29.644
called Grazed and Confused by Dr. Tara Gannet

3335
03:03:29.644 --> 03:03:31.897
and she walks through this,

3336
03:03:31.897 --> 03:03:35.035
the fact that you can probably offset

3337
03:03:35.035 --> 03:03:39.868
about 40 to 60% of emissions, but even then over time

3338
03:03:42.435 --> 03:03:47.018
what happens is the soil becomes saturated with carbon.

3339
03:03:48.100 --> 03:03:49.933
And so over the years,

3340
03:03:51.743 --> 03:03:53.366
you're sequestering less and less carbon,

3341
03:03:53.366 --> 03:03:55.174
but you still have the same amount of methane

3342
03:03:55.174 --> 03:03:56.852
and nitrous oxide emissions

3343
03:03:56.852 --> 03:04:01.852
so the practice is becoming less net sequestering over time.

3344
03:04:01.869 --> 03:04:05.698
The livestock themselves being the emitters,

3345
03:04:05.698 --> 03:04:08.576
but also part of that report was this idea

3346
03:04:08.576 --> 03:04:11.560
that the sequestration is not indefinite,

3347
03:04:11.560 --> 03:04:13.251
but time-sensitive

3348
03:04:13.251 --> 03:04:16.922
in that it's not permanently sequestered in the soil.

3349
03:04:16.922 --> 03:04:17.755
That's right.

3350
03:04:17.755 --> 03:04:19.755
So it can be reversible.

3351
03:04:21.734 --> 03:04:26.151
And still to this day and it is somewhat frustrating,

3352
03:04:28.808 --> 03:04:32.725
I actually think that some regenerative grazing

3353
03:04:35.319 --> 03:04:36.791
like let's say we were moving to a world

3354
03:04:36.791 --> 03:04:40.753
where everyone was adopting very plant-predominant diets

3355
03:04:40.753 --> 03:04:43.997
and the only beef production was regenerative grazing,

3356
03:04:43.997 --> 03:04:47.123
I think that's a great reserve for the world.

3357
03:04:47.123 --> 03:04:50.790
But what I've noticed is from this industry,

3358
03:04:52.299 --> 03:04:55.031
there doesn't seem to be a message about reduction,

3359
03:04:55.031 --> 03:04:57.847
it seems to be a message about it's not the cow,

3360
03:04:57.847 --> 03:05:02.532
it's the how and it's a nice message to the mainstream

3361
03:05:02.532 --> 03:05:06.704
who perhaps don't wanna change their meat consumption.

3362
03:05:06.704 --> 03:05:07.537
They don't have to,

3363
03:05:07.537 --> 03:05:10.051
they can just change where it's come from.

3364
03:05:10.051 --> 03:05:11.887
There's an intellectual dishonesty

3365
03:05:11.887 --> 03:05:14.630
with advocating for a solution

3366
03:05:14.630 --> 03:05:18.630
premised on more grazing without the counterpart

3367
03:05:20.139 --> 03:05:22.566
being this is all contingent

3368
03:05:22.566 --> 03:05:26.165
upon everybody moving to a more plant-predominant diet

3369
03:05:26.165 --> 03:05:30.573
because by dint of sheer land availability,

3370
03:05:30.573 --> 03:05:34.727
this model cannot scale to meet global meat demand

3371
03:05:34.727 --> 03:05:36.600
as it currently stands.

3372
03:05:36.600 --> 03:05:39.206
You're correct in that if everybody pivoted

3373
03:05:39.206 --> 03:05:42.877
to only eating beef that was grass-fed

3374
03:05:42.877 --> 03:05:46.689
and grazed on regenerative lands such as this,

3375
03:05:46.689 --> 03:05:48.709
we would be in a much better state,

3376
03:05:48.709 --> 03:05:51.792
but it's a mathematical impossibility

3377
03:05:52.813 --> 03:05:56.938
to meet global meat demand using this model.

3378
03:05:56.938 --> 03:05:58.241
So, something has to change

3379
03:05:58.241 --> 03:06:01.518
and as much as I admire the people

3380
03:06:01.518 --> 03:06:02.542
behind "Kiss the Ground"

3381
03:06:02.542 --> 03:06:05.148
and what they're trying to communicate,

3382
03:06:05.148 --> 03:06:07.652
I think there's a lot of good in that movie,

3383
03:06:07.652 --> 03:06:11.500
I feel like it overlooked or gave short shrift

3384
03:06:11.500 --> 03:06:16.500
to a very important point, which is that no matter what,

3385
03:06:16.886 --> 03:06:19.853
no matter how efficacious this model is,

3386
03:06:19.853 --> 03:06:21.561
it will still all demand

3387
03:06:21.561 --> 03:06:24.561
that we reduce our meat consumption.

3388
03:06:25.774 --> 03:06:28.536
And the key point being here

3389
03:06:28.536 --> 03:06:31.188
why do we need to reduce our meat consumption?

3390
03:06:31.188 --> 03:06:33.458
Because we need to free up land,

3391
03:06:33.458 --> 03:06:37.139
we need to produce more calories from less land

3392
03:06:37.139 --> 03:06:41.889
and restore vast amounts of land back to wild ecosystems.

3393
03:06:42.880 --> 03:06:46.803
And those wild ecosystems, forests, et cetera

3394
03:06:46.803 --> 03:06:49.857
are actually better at sequestering carbon

3395
03:06:49.857 --> 03:06:53.500
than a grazed regenerative farm would be.

3396
03:06:53.500 --> 03:06:58.361
Yeah, so this is a really interesting point too

3397
03:06:58.361 --> 03:07:02.778
because there is this sort of prevailing idea I guess

3398
03:07:03.786 --> 03:07:06.840
from people within holistic grazing

3399
03:07:06.840 --> 03:07:11.101
that you need the animals to regenerate the land.

3400
03:07:11.101 --> 03:07:12.768
And that's not true.

3401
03:07:13.824 --> 03:07:16.324
There is a meta analysis 2020,

3402
03:07:17.543 --> 03:07:21.432
which specifically looked at what happens to biodiversity

3403
03:07:21.432 --> 03:07:26.106
when you include livestock versus excluding them.

3404
03:07:26.106 --> 03:07:27.597
And they specifically showed that

3405
03:07:27.597 --> 03:07:30.680
when you exclude livestock from land,

3406
03:07:31.836 --> 03:07:34.405
you get greater returns in biodiversity,

3407
03:07:34.405 --> 03:07:36.043
increased numbers of herbivores,

3408
03:07:36.043 --> 03:07:38.430
you restore the predator-prey relationship,

3409
03:07:38.430 --> 03:07:41.881
you increase the number of inter vertebrate pollinators,

3410
03:07:41.881 --> 03:07:46.131
so I believe that the solution to this Rich is that

3411
03:07:51.412 --> 03:07:54.567
until there's an incentive for the landowner

3412
03:07:54.567 --> 03:07:56.650
to practice conservation,

3413
03:07:57.552 --> 03:07:59.590
like let's think about regeneration

3414
03:07:59.590 --> 03:08:03.389
and just drop agriculture off the end of it for a moment.

3415
03:08:03.389 --> 03:08:05.852
I understand that farmers

3416
03:08:05.852 --> 03:08:07.901
and landowners need to make an income.

3417
03:08:07.901 --> 03:08:10.401
And I think the grazing aspect

3418
03:08:12.135 --> 03:08:14.635
is a result of economics and--

3419
03:08:16.541 --> 03:08:19.897
It's a result of incentives or misalignment of incentives.

3420
03:08:19.897 --> 03:08:21.647
And in New Zealand,

3421
03:08:22.843 --> 03:08:26.343
they have a an incentive to afforestation,

3422
03:08:27.789 --> 03:08:31.367
so converting grasslands into forests

3423
03:08:31.367 --> 03:08:35.134
and it's now got to a point so 2020 paper that just...

3424
03:08:35.134 --> 03:08:39.384
Or 2021, it's now got to a point where land owners,

3425
03:08:42.145 --> 03:08:46.069
the more economical choice is to practice tree planting

3426
03:08:46.069 --> 03:08:49.681
in afforestation than livestock grazing.

3427
03:08:49.681 --> 03:08:52.855
How did they create that incentive structure?

3428
03:08:52.855 --> 03:08:57.855
Well, they are valuing carbon draw down and biodiversity.

3429
03:08:58.405 --> 03:09:01.961
And so I think that this is sort of critical

3430
03:09:01.961 --> 03:09:04.544
to solving this is our mindset,

3431
03:09:06.464 --> 03:09:09.446
if you go back to the First Nations' people,

3432
03:09:09.446 --> 03:09:13.279
something that we can learn from their culture

3433
03:09:14.827 --> 03:09:18.113
is living in harmony with the environment,

3434
03:09:18.113 --> 03:09:21.298
being stewards of the environment,

3435
03:09:21.298 --> 03:09:23.920
being guardians of the environment

3436
03:09:23.920 --> 03:09:27.425
and it's a different approach to looking at land

3437
03:09:27.425 --> 03:09:31.323
and thinking about what value can I extract from that

3438
03:09:31.323 --> 03:09:34.014
in the form of calories.

3439
03:09:34.014 --> 03:09:38.431
And the New Zealand government what they have done is

3440
03:09:40.991 --> 03:09:45.939
they have placed value on the carbon, on carbon storage

3441
03:09:45.939 --> 03:09:48.356
and so you create a situation

3442
03:09:49.205 --> 03:09:53.705
where the owner of that land stands to make more money

3443
03:09:54.594 --> 03:09:57.482
by practicing carbon farming

3444
03:09:57.482 --> 03:10:00.149
as opposed to livestock grazing.

3445
03:10:01.706 --> 03:10:04.626
We need some fundamental changes from the top down here

3446
03:10:04.626 --> 03:10:07.524
as well as from the bottom up

3447
03:10:07.524 --> 03:10:09.083
the Farm Bill being one of them.

3448
03:10:09.083 --> 03:10:10.325
I know were Island's very active

3449
03:10:10.325 --> 03:10:13.672
and doing some advocacy around the next vote

3450
03:10:13.672 --> 03:10:16.755
that's taking place I think next year

3451
03:10:18.702 --> 03:10:20.485
and we do need a better incentive structure

3452
03:10:20.485 --> 03:10:23.632
because these farmers are well-intentioned

3453
03:10:23.632 --> 03:10:25.073
and they just wanna make a living

3454
03:10:25.073 --> 03:10:27.141
and they're just trying to get by,

3455
03:10:27.141 --> 03:10:29.951
so how can we create a system that drives them

3456
03:10:29.951 --> 03:10:32.876
towards making that regenerative choice

3457
03:10:32.876 --> 03:10:35.624
in a way that's restoring our land, protecting our soils

3458
03:10:35.624 --> 03:10:39.207
and promoting the increase in biodiversity?

3459
03:10:42.157 --> 03:10:45.207
It always goes back to that analogy with the microbiome,

3460
03:10:45.207 --> 03:10:46.930
like these are all micro systems

3461
03:10:46.930 --> 03:10:49.331
and macro systems that mimic each other

3462
03:10:49.331 --> 03:10:53.168
in terms of how health outcomes are produced

3463
03:10:53.168 --> 03:10:56.822
and it starts at the very micro level

3464
03:10:56.822 --> 03:10:59.363
and scales up from there.

3465
03:10:59.363 --> 03:11:02.369
There is a wonderful documentary

3466
03:11:02.369 --> 03:11:05.352
called "Rewilding a Mountain".

3467
03:11:05.352 --> 03:11:07.077
You had a hot mountain, it's in Oregon--

3468
03:11:07.077 --> 03:11:08.920
Is this in China?

3469
03:11:08.920 --> 03:11:10.939
They did something similar in China too,

3470
03:11:10.939 --> 03:11:13.070
you wanna talk about that or this is different?--

3471
03:11:13.070 --> 03:11:16.385
This is in Oregon I believe, hot mountain.

3472
03:11:16.385 --> 03:11:18.218
And it's a documentary

3473
03:11:19.718 --> 03:11:23.301
that looked at a 20-year rewilding project.

3474
03:11:24.528 --> 03:11:27.853
The land manager at the time consulted scientists

3475
03:11:27.853 --> 03:11:31.527
and they said to him, "You have to remove livestock"

3476
03:11:31.527 --> 03:11:33.555
and all the riparian areas

3477
03:11:33.555 --> 03:11:36.242
which completely damaged the...

3478
03:11:36.242 --> 03:11:40.159
The birds had left, the land was very degraded.

3479
03:11:41.417 --> 03:11:43.549
A lot of degraded land around the world today

3480
03:11:43.549 --> 03:11:45.774
is through overgrazing.

3481
03:11:45.774 --> 03:11:49.857
And this documentary steps through what happened.

3482
03:11:50.775 --> 03:11:53.740
And you see the pronghorn antelope come back

3483
03:11:53.740 --> 03:11:56.903
and that predator-prey relationship comes back

3484
03:11:56.903 --> 03:11:59.518
and you see the grasslands grow

3485
03:11:59.518 --> 03:12:02.643
and it's not just this kind of mono grassland,

3486
03:12:02.643 --> 03:12:06.716
but it's native grasslands and that's an important point,

3487
03:12:06.716 --> 03:12:08.698
native grasslands are actually different

3488
03:12:08.698 --> 03:12:11.545
to a lot of these grazing grasslands.

3489
03:12:11.545 --> 03:12:15.462
They support a very diverse range of herbivores

3490
03:12:17.002 --> 03:12:20.335
and so that's a nice I think documentary

3491
03:12:21.236 --> 03:12:24.024
for people to watch if they wanna see what happens

3492
03:12:24.024 --> 03:12:27.441
when you exclude livestock from the land.

3493
03:12:28.482 --> 03:12:31.065
And this is just critical to us

3494
03:12:32.736 --> 03:12:37.569
solving the climate change problem in terms of agriculture

3495
03:12:39.059 --> 03:12:41.226
is rewilding a lot of land

3496
03:12:42.711 --> 03:12:45.513
and producing more calories from less land

3497
03:12:45.513 --> 03:12:49.763
and sometimes I hear of this idea of marginal land,

3498
03:12:50.921 --> 03:12:53.600
I'm not sure if you've heard of that.

3499
03:12:53.600 --> 03:12:57.767
And the idea with marginal land is that this land,

3500
03:12:58.799 --> 03:13:03.799
you can't do anything with it except for graze cattle on it.

3501
03:13:03.907 --> 03:13:05.574
But that's not true.

3502
03:13:07.009 --> 03:13:08.490
That's if you're looking at it

3503
03:13:08.490 --> 03:13:10.724
from an extraction of calories lens,

3504
03:13:10.724 --> 03:13:13.770
there's nothing marginal about that land

3505
03:13:13.770 --> 03:13:14.603
to the animals

3506
03:13:14.603 --> 03:13:18.336
and the ecosystem should you regenerate it and--

3507
03:13:18.336 --> 03:13:21.201
Marginalized, that's a subjective assessment

3508
03:13:21.201 --> 03:13:25.012
only by virtue of how valuable it is to us.

3509
03:13:25.012 --> 03:13:28.595
So, I think if we can change that mindset

3510
03:13:31.321 --> 03:13:34.488
from a less kind of dominating mindset

3511
03:13:35.788 --> 03:13:40.436
and more to practicing conservation and get the incentives

3512
03:13:40.436 --> 03:13:44.218
in like New Zealand, this is happening now around the world,

3513
03:13:44.218 --> 03:13:47.801
then farmers can be a part, play a key role

3514
03:13:51.021 --> 03:13:53.521
in helping to heal the planet.

3515
03:13:55.090 --> 03:13:56.623
I would imagine that,

3516
03:13:56.623 --> 03:14:00.719
if you can create that sort of structure for these farmers,

3517
03:14:00.719 --> 03:14:03.052
just how much more fulfilled

3518
03:14:04.443 --> 03:14:06.936
and purpose-driven they would feel

3519
03:14:06.936 --> 03:14:11.103
as stewards of the land rather than as extractors.

3520
03:14:13.146 --> 03:14:17.146
Well, I think we've gone over three hours, dude.

3521
03:14:18.564 --> 03:14:20.434
So much more to say.

3522
03:14:20.434 --> 03:14:23.969
I think we did two things on my entire outline,

3523
03:14:23.969 --> 03:14:26.941
but I'm not letting you go without congratulating you

3524
03:14:26.941 --> 03:14:28.038
on the new book.

3525
03:14:28.038 --> 03:14:33.038
This really is like the ultimate primer just chock-a-block

3526
03:14:33.574 --> 03:14:37.440
with accessible tools that walk you through

3527
03:14:37.440 --> 03:14:39.714
everything that we talked about today and so much more

3528
03:14:39.714 --> 03:14:44.422
with respect to how to be more plant-predominant

3529
03:14:44.422 --> 03:14:47.602
and understanding all the hows and whys behind it.

3530
03:14:47.602 --> 03:14:49.875
I know years of your life went into this,

3531
03:14:49.875 --> 03:14:52.300
when I was in Australia that January

3532
03:14:52.300 --> 03:14:53.143
you were writing it then

3533
03:14:53.143 --> 03:14:56.110
and that seems like a past life, so--

3534
03:14:56.110 --> 03:14:57.977
You gave me some great tips actually.

3535
03:14:57.977 --> 03:15:01.495
It's finally reached North American shores,

3536
03:15:01.495 --> 03:15:04.626
it's coming out September 30, what's the date?

3537
03:15:04.626 --> 03:15:05.459
November 1.

3538
03:15:05.459 --> 03:15:07.089
November 1, got it.

3539
03:15:07.089 --> 03:15:10.966
We've countered this to go out right around the same time.

3540
03:15:10.966 --> 03:15:12.949
So, kudos on that,

3541
03:15:12.949 --> 03:15:17.792
everybody should check out Simon's podcast, Plant Proof

3542
03:15:17.792 --> 03:15:19.125
and beyond that,

3543
03:15:20.665 --> 03:15:23.688
plantproof.com is an incredible resource

3544
03:15:23.688 --> 03:15:26.927
with tons of articles that are freely available

3545
03:15:26.927 --> 03:15:29.430
where you can kind of learn the nuts and bolts

3546
03:15:29.430 --> 03:15:34.097
of how to do this properly and everything I might add is

3547
03:15:35.728 --> 03:15:38.713
or nothing I should add about Plant Proof

3548
03:15:38.713 --> 03:15:42.104
or this book is being monetized by you basically.

3549
03:15:42.104 --> 03:15:44.886
This is stuff that you make freely available

3550
03:15:44.886 --> 03:15:48.996
and the proceeds of the book are being directed to charity,

3551
03:15:48.996 --> 03:15:50.032
am I correct?

3552
03:15:50.032 --> 03:15:53.630
Yeah, maybe we should finish this on a nice, optimistic...

3553
03:15:53.630 --> 03:15:55.026
I got some good news last night.

3554
03:15:55.026 --> 03:15:56.193
What's that?

3555
03:15:57.829 --> 03:16:01.227
The Daintree Rainforest in Australia,

3556
03:16:01.227 --> 03:16:05.144
which has been threatened through deforestation

3557
03:16:07.202 --> 03:16:08.241
over a number of years,

3558
03:16:08.241 --> 03:16:10.494
it's the world's oldest tropical rainforest,

3559
03:16:10.494 --> 03:16:13.673
one of the most biodiverse places on earth.

3560
03:16:13.673 --> 03:16:16.336
And it's a world heritage site now.

3561
03:16:16.336 --> 03:16:20.003
And overnight, there was a an agreement made

3562
03:16:21.730 --> 03:16:25.072
between the government and the First Nations' people

3563
03:16:25.072 --> 03:16:27.072
that are from that area.

3564
03:16:28.014 --> 03:16:30.931
And they have been granted the land

3565
03:16:32.392 --> 03:16:33.925
to be the custodians of the land,

3566
03:16:33.925 --> 03:16:37.625
so it's gonna be handed back to them to be under their care,

3567
03:16:37.625 --> 03:16:42.042
the Kuku Yalanji people, eastern Kuku Yalanji people.

3568
03:16:44.397 --> 03:16:48.397
So, it's a small win, it's 160,000 hectares zone

3569
03:16:50.403 --> 03:16:53.986
that will now be protected under their care

3570
03:16:56.235 --> 03:16:59.516
along with all the Kakadu in Australia,

3571
03:16:59.516 --> 03:17:02.766
so I woke to that and that's incredible

3572
03:17:04.069 --> 03:17:05.569
because that means

3573
03:17:06.573 --> 03:17:09.481
this wonderful rainforest will be protected

3574
03:17:09.481 --> 03:17:11.898
and their culture can live on

3575
03:17:13.137 --> 03:17:15.582
and we're at risk of losing a lot of the culture

3576
03:17:15.582 --> 03:17:16.784
from First Nations' people

3577
03:17:16.784 --> 03:17:20.951
so I see this kind of agreement as some reparation

3578
03:17:23.404 --> 03:17:25.542
making amends for things of the past

3579
03:17:25.542 --> 03:17:27.542
and important going back

3580
03:17:28.766 --> 03:17:30.463
to what we were talking about earlier

3581
03:17:30.463 --> 03:17:34.700
in just creating a societal perspective shift

3582
03:17:34.700 --> 03:17:38.130
to one of guardianship and stewardship.

3583
03:17:38.130 --> 03:17:40.547
And the more we can integrate

3584
03:17:41.624 --> 03:17:46.076
these First Nation cultures back into society, the better.

3585
03:17:46.076 --> 03:17:49.065
So, the reason I bring that up

3586
03:17:49.065 --> 03:17:50.620
is that you're right,

3587
03:17:50.620 --> 03:17:53.479
the proceeds profits of the book will be going...

3588
03:17:53.479 --> 03:17:55.607
There are still parts of the Daintree

3589
03:17:55.607 --> 03:17:56.829
that are privately-owned

3590
03:17:56.829 --> 03:18:00.313
and are under threat of deforestation,

3591
03:18:00.313 --> 03:18:03.076
so Half Cut, the organization

3592
03:18:03.076 --> 03:18:04.897
led by a friend of mine actually

3593
03:18:04.897 --> 03:18:06.673
has half a beard, which--

3594
03:18:06.673 --> 03:18:09.044
Oh, yeah, that guy, I know that guy.

3595
03:18:09.044 --> 03:18:09.877
What's his name?

3596
03:18:09.877 --> 03:18:11.707
Jimmy Halfcut--

3597
03:18:11.707 --> 03:18:12.655
Jimmy Halfcut.

3598
03:18:12.655 --> 03:18:14.774
Half a bead to represent half the world's--

3599
03:18:14.774 --> 03:18:16.470
He sounds like he is "Goodfellas" or something?

3600
03:18:16.470 --> 03:18:20.217
Yeah, so half the world's forests have been cleared

3601
03:18:20.217 --> 03:18:22.297
and that's what his half beard represent--

3602
03:18:22.297 --> 03:18:23.263
Got it.

3603
03:18:23.263 --> 03:18:26.231
I've never understood what the symbolic nature of that was.

3604
03:18:26.231 --> 03:18:28.779
So, it's a conversation starter.

3605
03:18:28.779 --> 03:18:31.180
Whenever I'm out with him, everyone of course comes up

3606
03:18:31.180 --> 03:18:32.589
and says, "Hey, dude, what's going on?"

3607
03:18:32.589 --> 03:18:33.928
How long has he had half a beard?

3608
03:18:33.928 --> 03:18:36.286
Five years I think, yeah, a long time.

3609
03:18:36.286 --> 03:18:38.457
He says he's going to regenerate it though.

3610
03:18:38.457 --> 03:18:41.957
So, he's regenerating it as we regenerate.

3611
03:18:43.528 --> 03:18:47.082
So, I hope that we see a day where he has a full beard,

3612
03:18:47.082 --> 03:18:49.812
but he runs the organization HalfCut

3613
03:18:49.812 --> 03:18:53.933
that is buying back some of the land around the Daintree,

3614
03:18:53.933 --> 03:18:57.489
still part of the Daintree that isn't under world heritage,

3615
03:18:57.489 --> 03:18:59.114
but buying it back

3616
03:18:59.114 --> 03:19:02.508
and then having it listed on the world heritage

3617
03:19:02.508 --> 03:19:06.398
and under the care of the indigenous people

3618
03:19:06.398 --> 03:19:08.222
that are from that area.

3619
03:19:08.222 --> 03:19:10.717
You're beautiful, man, Simon Hill.

3620
03:19:10.717 --> 03:19:12.544
Thank you.
Come back anytime man.

3621
03:19:12.544 --> 03:19:14.193
We got plenty more we could talk about.

3622
03:19:14.193 --> 03:19:17.610
Yeah, I hope I haven't confused people.

3623
03:19:19.157 --> 03:19:20.894
I think you've cut through a lot of confusion

3624
03:19:20.894 --> 03:19:24.020
and created clarity from my perspective.

3625
03:19:24.020 --> 03:19:26.377
More clarity again can be found in Simon's book,

3626
03:19:26.377 --> 03:19:29.653
"The Proof Is In The Plants", plantproof.com.

3627
03:19:29.653 --> 03:19:33.112
On Instagram, Simon is constantly sharing

3628
03:19:33.112 --> 03:19:36.279
super helpful information @plant_proof

3629
03:19:37.647 --> 03:19:38.586
and you're on Twitter a little bit,

3630
03:19:38.586 --> 03:19:41.592
you mix it up that's once in while--

3631
03:19:41.592 --> 03:19:43.984
Am an avid Twitter user,

3632
03:19:43.984 --> 03:19:47.013
I don't have many followers on there--

3633
03:19:47.013 --> 03:19:49.929
It's a good time, I always check in

3634
03:19:49.929 --> 03:19:52.940
like you're not afraid to scrap it up a little bit

3635
03:19:52.940 --> 03:19:53.773
with the--

3636
03:19:53.773 --> 03:19:55.420
I wondered actually, does Rich watch this?

3637
03:19:55.420 --> 03:19:58.595
Oh, yeah, oh, dude, I watch from the sidelines,

3638
03:19:58.595 --> 03:20:00.180
I don't participate--

3639
03:20:00.180 --> 03:20:01.833
I have a standard copy and paste line

3640
03:20:01.833 --> 03:20:06.833
which is "Can you provide a study that supports this claim?"

3641
03:20:07.462 --> 03:20:10.343
And tell you what, my strike rate is not great,

3642
03:20:10.343 --> 03:20:12.427
I get barely any replies to that line.

3643
03:20:12.427 --> 03:20:14.510
That's the conversation ender?

3644
03:20:14.510 --> 03:20:15.343
Yeah.

3645
03:20:15.343 --> 03:20:16.176
There you go.

3646
03:20:16.176 --> 03:20:18.592
There are some people who of course can engage

3647
03:20:18.592 --> 03:20:21.009
in a civil conversation but--

3648
03:20:22.867 --> 03:20:24.483
Yeah, but three or four tweets

3649
03:20:24.483 --> 03:20:28.416
and it generally devolves into less civil,

3650
03:20:28.416 --> 03:20:29.843
but we kept it civil here today--

3651
03:20:29.843 --> 03:20:30.919
We did.

3652
03:20:30.919 --> 03:20:32.542
And civil in the world

3653
03:20:32.542 --> 03:20:35.280
and enjoy the rest of your time in LA

3654
03:20:35.280 --> 03:20:36.859
and we'll do this again soon.

3655
03:20:36.859 --> 03:20:37.970
Rich, love you, brother.

3656
03:20:37.970 --> 03:20:40.967
Thank you so much for having me.

3657
03:20:40.967 --> 03:20:44.478
And if you find yourself in Bondi,

3658
03:20:44.478 --> 03:20:47.207
Bondi Beach where Simon hails from,

3659
03:20:47.207 --> 03:20:49.576
you got to visit in Simon's restaurant--

3660
03:20:49.576 --> 03:20:50.612
And I can't take the credit,

3661
03:20:50.612 --> 03:20:52.416
I can't take all the credit for that, I need to--

3662
03:20:52.416 --> 03:20:53.399
Well, Tania.

3663
03:20:53.399 --> 03:20:55.649
Tania, my lovely partner,

3664
03:20:58.008 --> 03:20:59.866
she's made that the place that it is

3665
03:20:59.866 --> 03:21:03.012
so of course if you're in town come on down.

3666
03:21:03.012 --> 03:21:03.845
Yeah, for sure.

3667
03:21:03.845 --> 03:21:06.919
Best plant beast eats in all of Sydney

3668
03:21:06.919 --> 03:21:08.857
right there in Bondi Beach,

3669
03:21:08.857 --> 03:21:11.959
when I was spending time there that was my second home

3670
03:21:11.959 --> 03:21:14.422
and next time you're gonna have to let me pay for a meal

3671
03:21:14.422 --> 03:21:15.897
because--

3672
03:21:15.897 --> 03:21:18.257
No, we love to have you.

3673
03:21:18.257 --> 03:21:19.090
It was great.

3674
03:21:19.090 --> 03:21:20.042
So, thanks, man.

3675
03:21:20.042 --> 03:21:23.211
Much love, talk to you soon, peace, plants.

3676
03:21:23.211 --> 03:21:26.378
(bright upbeat music)

